://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/01/new-years-day-1864/', 'snippet': 'Americans greeted another year of the Civil War with a mix of optimism and fear.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is easy enough to conclude in hindsight that 1863 was a year of “turning points” — the Emancipation Proclamation, Gettysburg and Vicksburg most prominent among them — on the Union’s road to victory. Indeed, the sesquicentennial commemorations of 2013 featured a wide array of programs, articles, symposiums, forums and blogs with “turning points” as their theme. But as the year draws to a close, we should pause to remember how uncertain “the situation” still looked to Northerners in the first days of 1864. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Year’s Day, 1864', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New Year', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lincoln, Abraham', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T03:29:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth R. Varon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Varon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f39d4961-1e04-56ae-a187-22f9b62c8d39', 'word_count': 1194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f39d4961-1e04-56ae-a187-22f9b62c8d39'}, {'abstract': 'In which I lose a middle initial.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/01/whats-missing-in-my-byline/', 'snippet': 'In which I lose a middle initial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you look closely at my Times byline, beginning with Thursday’s column, you’ll notice something odd. Well, actually, you probably won’t notice it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s Missing in My Byline?', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T04:00:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/533d4d82-1786-51e5-ac57-2635a2f05e69', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/533d4d82-1786-51e5-ac57-2635a2f05e69'}, {'abstract': 'China’s murky penal system for prostitutes is similar to re-education through labor, with women being held in centers for up to two years and often required to work seven days a week for no pay.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/world/asia/for-prostitutes-in-china-jail-with-no-recourse.html', 'snippet': 'China’s murky penal system for prostitutes is similar to re-education through labor, with women being held in centers for up to two years and often required to work seven days a week for no pay.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — Bathed in the fluorescent pink light that signaled she was ready for business, the woman rattled off the occupational hazards of working as a prostitute in China: abusive clients, the specter of H.I.V. and the scathing glares of neighbors that tear at her soul. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/02/world/asia/sub-Laborcamp/sub-Laborcamp-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/02/world/asia/sub-Laborcamp/sub-Lab