'nyt://article/509d34fe-fb79-5b86-9826-ce31ca0a4523'}, {'abstract': 'The up-and-coming Brooklyn designers Chen Chen and Kai Williams devised their own rugmaking technique that combines multiple disparate fibers into whimsical assemblages.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/t-magazine/chen-chen-and-kai-williams-cleverly-made-carpets.html', 'snippet': 'The up-and-coming Brooklyn designers Chen Chen and Kai Williams devised their own rugmaking technique that combines multiple disparate fibers into whimsical assemblages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since joining forces in 2011, the Brooklyn designers Chen Chen and Kai Williams have created a number of surprising products derived from experiments with process. One of the most interesting was their cold-cut coasters, made by wrapping materials such as fishnet and rope around a central wooden rod or “bone” and then slicing it, like a maki roll. The resulting multicolored disks really do look almost edible. The coasters were the inspiration for a set of four circular rugs the duo created for the luxury custom carpet company Tai Ping, which was unveiled last month at Design Miami. Using a technique the designers came up with to combine multiple fibers, the carpets were fabricated in wool, cotton, chenille, bamboo, PVC, silk and more, giving the appearance of a cross section of a living organism beneath your feet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/t-magazine/03rugs-delavan/03rugs-delavan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 554, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/03/t-magazine/03rugs-delavan/03rugs-delavan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 554}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/t-magazine/03rugs-delavan/03rugs-delavan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 945, 