jpg', 'height': 1800, 'width': 2021, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/02/arts/jpambassador/jpambassador-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/02/arts/jpambassador/jpambassador-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/02/arts/jpambassador/jpambassador-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Newbery Winner to Promote Her Genre', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Newbery Winner To Promote Her Genre', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'DiCamillo, Kate', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Library of Congress', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T05:11:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julie Bosman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bosman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56dec6e7-f17a-5aff-9ebd-146efa4637bc', 'word_count': 780, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56dec6e7-f17a-5aff-9ebd-146efa4637bc'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '7 P.M. (Syfy) PAUL (2011) He looks like any other extraterrestrial, with his otherworldly cast, bald bobble head, big eyes and stick limbs. Until you get to the board shorts and the backpack. In other words, Paul, voiced by Seth Rogen and the title character of this comedy from Greg Mottola, is a little green dude. And he comes in peace, man, despite causing havoc while he tries to phone home. Simon Pegg, above with Paul, and Nick Frost are the best friends, fresh from a Comic-Con convention in San Diego, who run into Paul as they guide their recreational vehicle toward Area 51, the military base in Nevada where the conspiracy minded think the government stashes aliens. Jason Bateman is one of the men in black hunting\xa0Paul; Kristen Wiig is the love interest who wears a T-shirt depicting Jesus blowing away Darwin; and John Carroll Lynch is her daddy, a member of the Bible and gun club. “As genial, foolish and demographically engineered as it sounds (hailing all fan boys and girls),” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times, “\xa0‘Paul’ is at once a buddy flick and a classic American road movie of self- (and other) discovery, interspersed with buckets of expletives and some startling (especially for a big-studio release) pokes at Christian fundamentalism.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T06:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/25db0385-eb20-5f23-9a85-26a7a5092386', 'word_count': 745, 'uri': 'nyt://article/25db0385-eb20-5f23-9a85-26a7a5092386'}, {'abstract': 'Schools that cannot meet the 2009 law’s infrastructure requirements, even if they cannot afford the necessary repairs, are at risk of closure.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/private-schools-for-poor-pressured-by-right-to-education-act/', 'snippet': 'Schools that cannot meet the 2009 law’s infrastructure requirements, even if they cannot afford the necessary repairs, are at risk of closure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MUMBAI — In Dharavi, a Mumbai slum, a \xa0ramshackle building houses the Bombay South Indian Adi-Dravida Sangh school, where 1,000 students from poor families take their classes in English, a language increasingly perceived as the key to a white-collar job.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Private Schools for Poor Pressured by Right to Education Act', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private and Sectarian Schools', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gujarat State (India)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T06:50:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neha Thirani Bagri', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neha', 'middlename': 'Thirani', 'lastname': 'Bagri', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/04b93433-05c5-5a78-946f-146573223e63', 'wor