rticle', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Tough', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tough', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ca9b6f6d-7a82-5a7b-b9b2-1c9f4f92279d', 'word_count': 7497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ca9b6f6d-7a82-5a7b-b9b2-1c9f4f92279d'}, {'abstract': 'The career of Kikujiro Fukushima, who is still an active photojournalist at 92, spans from the aftermath of the dropping of the atomic bomb in 1945 to the Fukushima disaster of 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/photographing-hiroshima-fukushima-and-everything-in-between/', 'snippet': 'The career of Kikujiro Fukushima, who is still an active photojournalist at 92, spans from the aftermath of the dropping of the atomic bomb in 1945 to the Fukushima disaster of 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kikujiro Fukushima’s life in photography took off when he promised to avenge the Hiroshima bombing. It was 1952, and Mr. Fukushima — a watchmaker, volunteer social worker and photographer — met Sugimatsu Nakamura, a 43-year-old fisherman, who was gravely ill from the atomic bomb’s effects.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/blogs/20131230-lens-fukushima-slide-JRWI/20131230-lens-fukushima-slide-JRWI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 342, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/12/30/blogs/20131230-lens-fukushima-slide-JRWI/20131230-lens-fukushima-slide-JRWI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 342}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/12/30/blogs/20131230-lens-fukushima-slide-JRWI/20131230-lens-fukushima-slide-JRWI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 584,