_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Medina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Medina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60bce9c2-063a-5003-8f17-9db93a1b20ae', 'word_count': 1142, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60bce9c2-063a-5003-8f17-9db93a1b20ae'}, {'abstract': 'Chang-rae Lee’s new novel imagines a future after the breakdown of society.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/on-such-a-full-sea-by-chang-rae-lee.html', 'snippet': 'Chang-rae Lee’s new novel imagines a future after the breakdown of society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Watching a talented writer take a risk is one of the pleasures of devoted reading, and “On Such a Full Sea” provides all that and more. It’s a wonderful addition not only to Chang-rae Lee’s body of work but to the ranks of “serious” writers venturing into the realm of dystopian fantasy. Colson Whitehead, Cormac McCarthy and Margaret Atwood (in her recently concluded MaddAddam trilogy) have all tackled this genre. Doris Lessing’s “Mara and Dann” is a classic, as is Anthony Burgess’s “Clockwork Orange.” Further back in time, one has only to think of Orwell, Huxley and Wells, even Jack London and Mary Shelley. As the author of such carefully realist fiction as “Native Speaker” and “A Gesture Life,” Lee has always been preoccupied by the themes of hope and betrayal, by the tensions that arise in small lives in the midst of great social change. His marvelous new book, which imagines a future after the breakdown of our own society, takes on those concerns with his customary mastery of quiet detail — and a touch of the fantastic.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-GREER-sub/0105-bks-GREER-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 594, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-GREER-sub/0105-bks-GREER-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 594}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-GREER-sub/0105-bks-GREER-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1013, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-GREER-sub/0105-bks-GREER-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2027, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-GREER-sub/0105-bks-GREER-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-GREER-sub/0105-bks-GREER-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-GREER-sub/0105-bks-GREER-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Diving Into the Wreck', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Diving Into The Wreck', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Chang-Rae', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:28:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Sean Greer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'Sean', 'lastname': 'Greer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bad091d-5abc-5d8b-acc0-fe884416301c', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bad091d-5abc-5d8b-acc0-fe884416301c'}, {'abstract': 'A son’s story of moving from Leningrad to Queens with parents unburdened by any understanding of American life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/little-failure-by-gary-shteyngart.html', 'snippet': 'A son’s story of moving from Leningrad to Queens with parents unburdened by any understanding of American life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early in his hilarious and moving memoir, Gary Shteyngart reveals that his mother’s post-collegiate nickname for him is Little Failure, which would seem more insulting if his father didn’t already call him Snotty. In this Russian Jewish family — the Shteyngarts moved from Leningrad to Queens in 1979, when Gary was 7 — barbed jokes, or shutki, are tokens of love: weird, hostile tokens, but tokens nonetheless. The shutki continue to fly in Shteyngart’s direction even after — or maybe especially after — he becomes a successful novelist (“The Russian Debutante’s Handbook,” “Absurdistan,” “Super Sad True Love Story”). Treating his parents to dinner at the revolving restaurant atop the Marriott Marquis to celebrate his mother’s birthday, Shteyngart is a sitting duck as his father fires off this one: “I read on the Russian Internet that you and your novels will soon be forgotten.” While that may not sound exactly like a joke, it’s riotous compared with what he tells his son at another dinner: “I burn with a black envy toward you. I should have been an artist as well.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BOROWITZ/05BOROWITZ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 762, 'width': 534, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BOROWITZ/05BOROWITZ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 762, 'width': 534, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BOROWITZ/05BOROWITZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BOROWITZ/05BOROWITZ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BOROWITZ/05BOROWITZ-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mr. Shteyngart’s Planet', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mr. Shteyngart’s Planet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shteyngart, Gary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:29:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Borowitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borowitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0405399b-5344-50d7-aa1e-af1e150cf849', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0405399b-5344-50d7-aa1e-af1e150cf849'}, {'abstract': 'A biography traces Barbara Stanwyck’s evolution from Brooklyn dancer to screen star.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/a-life-of-barbara-stanwyck-by-victoria-wilson.html', 'snippet': 'A biography traces Barbara Stanwyck’s evolution from Brooklyn dancer to screen star.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Start with the voice, which seems to have been around since the world began: lush, weary, tender, worldly, skeptical, ranging nimbly between hard and soft. It could be metallic, mannish and brittle or gentle as a down pillow, sometimes within the same film, as befits an actress who was at ease in every genre, from woman’s melodrama to the western, with noir and screwball comedy in between. Though film buffs have treasured her for years, Barbara Stanwyck has burned less brightly among general moviegoers for whom a higher voltage is synonymous with stardom.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05haskell-1/05haskell-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1096, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05haskell-1/05haskell-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1096}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05haskell-1/05haskell-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 561, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05haskell-1/05haskell-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1644, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05haskell-1/05haskell-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05haskell-1/05haskell-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05haskell-1/05haskell-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Girl Named Ruby', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Girl Named Ruby', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stanwyck, Barbara', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Victoria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:29:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Molly Haskell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Molly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haskell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6013331b-8ab3-5e7c-a29e-7f23fcac9043', 'word_count': 2719, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6013331b-8ab3-5e7c-a29e-7f23fcac9043'}, {'abstract': 'A new study conducted in Oregon found that after getting health insurance, people went to the emergency room more often than their uninsured counterparts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/03/health/access-to-health-care-may-increase-er-visits-study-suggests.html', 'snippet': 'A new study conducted in Oregon found that after getting health insurance, people went to the emergency room more often than their uninsured counterparts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Supporters of President Obama’s health care law had predicted that expanding insurance coverage for the poor would reduce costly emergency room visits because people would go to primary care doctors instead. But a rigorous new experiment in Oregon has raised questions about that assumption, finding that newly insured people actually went to the emergency room a good deal more often.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/us/MEDICAID/MEDICAID-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 409, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/03/us/MEDICAID/MEDICAID-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 409}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/us/MEDICAID/MEDICAID-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 698, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/us/MEDICAID/MEDICAID-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1396, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/us/MEDICAID/MEDICAID-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/03/us/MEDICAID/MEDICAID-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/03/us/MEDICAID/MEDICAID-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Emergency Visits Seen Increasing With Health Law', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Emergency Visits Seen Increasing With Health Law', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tavernise, Sabrina', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Medical Treatment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hospitals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Oregon', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:31:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sabrina Tavernise', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sabrina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tavernise', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/33d4f1bd-0482-5c17-90d7-d908effa9a35', 'word_count': 1156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/33d4f1bd-0482-5c17-90d7-d908effa9a35'}, {'abstract': 'The lawyer, Chris Gossage, was fined and rebuked for breaking client confidentiality rules.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/lawyer-is-fined-for-revealing-rowling-as-author-of-detective-novel/', 'snippet': 'The lawyer, Chris Gossage, was fined and rebuked for breaking client confidentiality rules.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chris Gossage, the lawyer whose indiscreet chatter led to the public unmasking of J. K. Rowling as the author of “The Cuckoo’s Calling” — the detective novel that she published under the pseudonym Robert Galbraith — has been fined £1,000 (about $1,645) by the Solicitors Regulation Authority for breaking the authority’s client confidentiality rules. Mr. Gossage, a partner at Russells Solicitors, also received a written rebuke. The ruling was issued by the authority on Nov. 26, but was not made public until this week.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lawyer Is Fined for Revealing Rowling as Author of Detective Novel', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lawyer Is Fined for Unmasking Rowling as Detective Novel’s Author', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rowling, J K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:39:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1b8fe5f9-dc32-54d0-a3ea-b84c4462f45e', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1b8fe5f9-dc32-54d0-a3ea-b84c4462f45e'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Deborah Solomon’s “American Mirror” and more.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/letters-norman-rockwell-new-yorker.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond to a recent review of Deborah Solomon’s “American Mirror” and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Larger Understanding', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Letters: Norman Rockwell, New Yorker', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Letters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:39:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72e310d9-7de8-50df-bdc2-eb99d69966a5', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72e310d9-7de8-50df-bdc2-eb99d69966a5'}, {'abstract': 'I’m settling into my new life, though perhaps not as well as my dog.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/the-getting-was-good/', 'snippet': 'I’m settling into my new life, though perhaps not as well as my dog.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The very day I turned 66, 177 of you wished me well on a decision to take the advice I’d been giving others: To leave a house I loved as one loves something animate, not merely a construction of clapboard and stone, before the inevitable crisis that would make it too hard to live there and also too hard to leave under my own steam.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Getting Was Good', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Moving and Moving Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:45:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Gross', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gross', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c04f61b-3b85-5553-af41-39ee9ad65d33', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c04f61b-3b85-5553-af41-39ee9ad65d33'}, {'abstract': 'Steven Kern, executive director of the Everson Museum of Art in Syracuse, will become director and chief officer of the Newark Museum.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/02/new-director-named-for-newark-museum/', 'snippet': 'Steven Kern, executive director of the Everson Museum of Art in Syracuse, will become director and chief officer of the Newark Museum.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steven Kern has been appointed director and chief executive officer of the Newark Museum, the museum announced Thursday. Mr. Kern, who is currently the executive director of the Everson Museum of Art in Syracuse, will take up his new position in February. He succeeds Mary Sue Sweeney Price, who retired in May.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Director Named for Newark Museum', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kern, Steven', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Newark Museum', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Newark (NJ)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-02T19:45:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae537f75-6343-5f78-b844-8bdc63a4fbdc', 'word_count': 158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae537f75-6343-5f78-b844-8bdc63a4fbdc'}, {'abstract': 'Sanitation trucks were working to clear the roads as New York’s new mayor, Bill de Blasio, told residents to “be extremely careful going outdoors.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.