ront lines of reform, but it also maddened colleagues and complicated his legacy. Now, Wall Street is hoping for a friendlier regulator.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gensler Bids Farewell to Trading Commission', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T12:44:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden and Sydney Ember', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Sydney', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ember', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/122e89cb-594e-55d7-aa3c-f562649aa358', 'word_count': 2075, 'uri': 'nyt://article/122e89cb-594e-55d7-aa3c-f562649aa358'}, {'abstract': 'Is China the next Japan?', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/03/babaru-memories/', 'snippet': 'Is China the next Japan?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Lots of talk about China lately; and it’s still very worrisome. I am, however, mostly on vacation. But a brief note: what I haven’t seen many people mentioning is the obvious parallel, which is not so much to the US in the last decade as to Japan in the 1980s.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Babaru Memories', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T13:25:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b72671d-98d7-576a-af7c-7f3f07b84b8b', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b72671d-98d7-576a-af7c-7f3f07b84b8b'}, {'abstract': 'The Snow Dragon, a Chinese vessel, requested help and an Australian icebreaker was placed on standby in case it became icebound.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/04/world/antarctica-ship.html', 'snippet': 'The Snow Dragon, a Chinese vessel, requested help and an Australian icebreaker was placed on standby in case it became icebound.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — One day after a Chinese icebreaker played a central role in the rescue of 52 passengers from an icebound research vessel in Antarctica, crew members on the Chinese ship said it might itself become trapped by ice, according to Australian officials on Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'After Antarctic Drama, Chinese Rescue Ship Is Threatened by Ice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rescues', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ice', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ships and Shipping', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T13:28:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Wong and Alan Cowell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None