/144c0e45-47a8-5514-8eb1-a8780173fc2a', 'word_count': 1001, 'uri': 'nyt://article/144c0e45-47a8-5514-8eb1-a8780173fc2a'}, {'abstract': 'A newly translated novel by a Turkish master satirizes his country’s 20th-century transformation.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/the-time-regulation-institute-by-ahmet-hamdi-tanpinar.html', 'snippet': 'A newly translated novel by a Turkish master satirizes his country’s 20th-century transformation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’re having a particularly good season for literary discoveries from the past, with recent publications of Volumes 1 and 2 of Ahmad Faris al-Shidyaq’s “Leg Over Leg” (1855), the marathon translation of Giacomo Leopardi’s 2,600-page “Zibaldone” (1898) and now “The Time Regulation Institute” (1962), the second great novel from Ahmet Hamdi Tanpinar. They arrive, such books, in a category all their own, in one sense new, in another sense old, as if to remind us that this thing called literature is much larger than our own little moment.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05RIKER-sub/05RIKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 587, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05RIKER-sub/05RIKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 587}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05RIKER-sub/05RIKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05RIKER-sub/05RIKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2005, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05RIKER-sub/05RIKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05RIKER-sub/05RIKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05RIKER-sub/05RIKER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Ramshackle Modernity', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Ramshackle Modernity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freely, Maureen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanpinar, Ahmet Hamdi', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dawe, Alexander', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T16:15:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Riker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Riker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/aea8d2c7-6416-568d-a89e-0bfb2935bebd', 'word_count': 1183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aea8d2c7-6416-568d-a89e-0bfb2935bebd'}, {'abstract': 'A memoir examines the advice and improvement industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/promise-land-by-jessica-lamb-shapiro.html', 'snippet': 'A memoir examines the advice and improvement industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s ingrained in human nature to look at ourselves with a weary awareness of all that’s wrong within, and the optimism that someone, somewhere can tell us how to fix it. As Jessica Lamb-Shapiro points out in her ambitious if unfulfilling new memoir-cum-odyssey, “Promise Land,” we’ve been gobbling up self-help advice for nearly as long as the written word has existed, devouring it in the ancient Egyptian Sebayt writings and the Book of Proverbs. But our contemporary mania for the wisdom of Dr. Phil is different from what generations past gleaned from Epictetus or even Dale Carnegie, and Lamb-Shapiro aims to explain how. Along the way, she’s on a quest to fix herself.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05williams/0105-bks-openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 220, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05williams/0105-bks-openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 220}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05williams/0105-bks-openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 376, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05williams/0105-bks-openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 1629, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05williams/0105-bks-openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05williams/0105-bks-openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05williams/0105-bks-openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Heal Thyself', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Heal Thyself', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lamb-Shapiro, Jessica', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T16:15:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Elizabeth Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': 'Elizabeth', 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/70125b75-c903-5a05-9d5e-0e762bf37f5e', 'word_count': 612, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70125b75-c903-5a05-9d5e-0e762bf37f5e'}, {'abstract': 'A military wife confronts the death of her husband.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/unremarried-widow-by-artis-henderson.html', 'snippet': 'A military wife confronts the death of her husband.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One covets a gold star as a small child (chores done!), a student (great work!), an entrepreneur (superior service!). For a military spouse, however, gold star status is anathema, for it is a gold star, edged in blue and floating on a field of white bordered in red, that forms the flag indicating a family member has died during service. A gold star wife, like the author Artis \xadHenderson, is a widow.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BURANA/05BURANA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 913, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BURANA/05BURANA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 913}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BURANA/05BURANA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 673, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BURANA/05BURANA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2046, 'width': 1344, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BURANA/05BURANA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BURANA/05BURANA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05BURANA/05BURANA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Regret to Inform', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Regret to Inform', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Henderson, Artis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Widows and Widowers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T16:15:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lily Burana', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burana', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/56b5f900-e4d8-557e-9618-20abe9a9a464', 'word_count': 623, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56b5f900-e4d8-557e-9618-20abe9a9a464'}, {'abstract': 'Martin Gardner immersed himself in philosophy, religion and literature, even as he made a career of his fascination with science.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/undiluted-hocus-pocus-by-martin-gardner.html', 'snippet': 'Martin Gardner immersed himself in philosophy, religion and literature, even as he made a career of his fascination with science.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You can construct a Möbius strip from a piece of paper. You cut a strip, say, an inch wide and two feet long, twist one end 180 degrees, and join that to the other end with rubber cement. You now hold a physical paradox: If you trace a pencil line continuously down the middle of your strip, you end up with one continuous line down the middle of both sides. This proves that the paper has only one side, even though, when you pinch it between your fingers, you can feel both sides at the same time. Your intellect collides with your senses, and the result, if you are Martin Gardner, is inspiration. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-TELLER/0105-bks-TELLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-TELLER/0105-bks-TELLER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-TELLER/0105-bks-TELLER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 649, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-TELLER/0105-bks-TELLER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1297, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-TELLER/0105-bks-TELLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-TELLER/0105-bks-TELLER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/0105-bks-TELLER/0105-bks-TELLER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Brainteaser', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Brainteaser', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gardner, Martin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T16:16:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teller', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a42a57c-c214-5022-b854-214614036515', 'word_count': 1575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a42a57c-c214-5022-b854-214614036515'}, {'abstract': 'An English aristocrat with vicious parents, a dead older brother and an enigmatic story.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/books/review/the-secret-rooms-by-catherine-bailey.html', 'snippet': 'An English aristocrat with vicious parents, a dead older brother and an enigmatic story.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is it possible nowadays for otherwise intelligent Americans to reflect on England without thinking first of “Downton Abbey”? To put it another way: Can beleaguered American publishers expect to sell any English author without promising — however absurdly — a tie-in with Julian Fellowes’s opulent confection? I wonder. But in the case of Catherine Bailey’s stylish new book about one of England’s grandest dynasties, the link proves apt.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05SWINEHEART/05SWINEHEART-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05SWINEHEART/05SWINEHEART-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 667}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05SWINEHEART/05SWINEHEART-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 921, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05SWINEHEART/05SWINEHEART-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1162, 'width': 1045, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/books/review/05SWINEHEART/05