dc1-154348a55417'}, {'abstract': 'Unfettered optimism was the unofficial religion of the United States in the early 20th century, but the photographer Lewis Hine declined to buy in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/arts/design/captured-in-time-a-certain-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'Unfettered optimism was the unofficial religion of the United States in the early 20th century, but the photographer Lewis Hine declined to buy in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unfettered optimism was the unofficial religion of the United States in the early 20th century, but the photographer Lewis Hine (1874-1940) declined to buy in, refused to be steamrollered by our natural cultural euphoria, as the retrospective “Lewis Hine” at the International Center of Photography clearly shows. The exhibition includes Hine’s earliest work, from Ellis Island (1905), and includes selections from his later major photographic essays, including those on immigration, child labor, working men and construction of the Empire State Building. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Captured in Time: A Certain New York', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Captured in Time: A Certain New York', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T18:18:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dana Jennings', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dana', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jennings', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/505c6941-8e80-5730-a6be-ccf7ce0cdbc6', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/505c6941-8e80-5730-a6be-ccf7ce0cdbc6'}, {'abstract': 'Prototype: Opera/Theater/Now, an ambitious festival started in January 2013 by Beth Morrison Projects and the Here Arts Center, returns on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/arts/music/political-torment-teenage-angst.html', 'snippet': 'Prototype: Opera/Theater/Now, an ambitious festival started in January 2013 by Beth Morrison Projects and the Here Arts Center, returns on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prototype: Opera/Theater/Now, an ambitious festival started in January 2013 by Beth Morrison Projects and the Here Arts Center, returns on Wednesday for a second season, with four principal shows and a bevy of side attractions. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Political Torment, Teenage Angst', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Political Torment, Teenage Angst', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T18:18:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/03fac408-9137-575e-abcc-a97edb757c0a', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03fac408-9137-575e-abcc-a97edb757c0a'}, {'abstract': 'This year’s edition of Winter Jazz Festival runs five nights instead of two.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/arts/music/gems-of-jazz-for-winters-night.html', 'snippet': 'This year’s edition of Winter Jazz Festival runs five nights instead of two.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year’s edition of Winter Jazz Festival runs five nights instead of two. It was already a significant jazz event in New York — now even more so. The first three nights are self-contained, concerts at Le Poisson Rouge and Town Hall: the drummer Bobby Previte’s all-star group, Terminals, on Tuesday; a head-to-head, double-piano concert involving Jason Moran, Robert Glasper and their various associates on Wednesday; and the Revive Big Band on Thursday. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Gems of Jazz for Winter’s Night', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Gems of Jazz For Winter’s Night', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T18:19:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Ratliff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ratliff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/36923374-6969-5d10-a605-9707b30d61a9', 'word_count': 166, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36923374-6969-5d10-a605-9707b30d61a9'}, {'abstract': 'At the Association of Performing Arts Presenters meets in New York, one subject delegates are sure to ponder is the choosing of dance groups to present in coming months.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/arts/dance/20-companies-at-joyce-and-nyu.html', 'snippet': 'At the Association of Performing Arts Presenters meets in New York, one subject delegates are sure to ponder is the choosing of dance groups to present in coming months.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every January, the Association of Performing Arts Presenters meets in New York, and one subject delegates are sure to ponder is the choosing of dance groups to present in coming months. No wonder dancers hope to attract attention by performing at conference time. Gotham Arts Exchange, which provides management assistance to artists, hopes to entice both presenters and the public with its Focus series at two theaters. Eight companies will appear at the Joyce Theater (opening Tuesday), and there will be 12 at the Skirball Center (opening Friday), with each performance offering at least two attractions. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '20 Companies at Joyce and N.Y.U.', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '20 Companies At Joyce and N.Y.U.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T18:19:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2cdcf33-7f72-5416-8f10-272fcd2c5923', 'word_count': 169, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2cdcf33-7f72-5416-8f10-272fcd2c5923'}, {'abstract': 'The stranded ship Akademik Shokalskiy only had five days of food left when a Chinese helicopter rescued passengers. Now, the rescuers themselves may need to be rescued.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/03/stranded-antarctic-ship-story-like-the-ice-will-not-let-go/', 'snippet': 'The stranded ship Akademik Shokalskiy only had five days of food left when a Chinese helicopter rescued passengers. Now, the rescuers themselves may need to be rescued.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A team of rescuers from a Chinese icebreaker may need to be rescued themselves, soon after they plucked dozens of people from the Antarctic ice aboard a ship that had been stranded for more than a week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stranded Antarctic Ship Story, Like the Ice, Will Not Let Go', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Aurora Australis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ice', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Icebreakers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rescues', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Turney, Chris', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'China Central Television', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANTARCTIC REGIONS', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Australia', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tasmania (Australia)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T18:21:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/712a151c-58cb-52f0-af71-b22230591e25', 'word_count': 614, 'uri': 'nyt://article/712a151c-58cb-52f0-af71-b22230591e25'}, {'abstract': 'The Robertson clan is getting into guns.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/03/the-duck-dynasty-diversifies/', 'snippet': 'The Robertson clan is getting into guns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Selling plain old duck calls — simple tools that lure prey with fake quacks — underpins the phenomenal financial success of “Duck Dynasty’s” Robertson clan, one of cable’s favorite families. Phil Robertson, the outspoken evangelical patriarch, got into trouble lately with some homophobic, fire-and-brimstone remarks, but after a brief suspension from the show, he was reinstated—in a fight between high-mindedness and ratings, ratings always win — and the duck calls kept selling by the millions. Actual hunters comprise a minority of sales, according to Willie Robertson, the family business executive who said most are bought by people who “put it on their desk and toot on it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Duck Dynasty Diversifies', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robertson, Phil', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T18:22:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francis X. Clines', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francis', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Clines', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/98afd9f4-4a63-584e-a269-fc93dc9c3cb1', 'word_count': 222, 'uri': 'nyt://article/98afd9f4-4a63-584e-a269-fc93dc9c3cb1'}, {'abstract': 'The Writers Guild nominees include well-known names like David O. Russell, Woody Allen and Spike Jonze.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/03/writers-guild-unveils-picks-for-screenplay-categories/', 'snippet': 'The Writers Guild nominees include well-known names like David O. Russell, Woody Allen and Spike Jonze.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Writers Guild of America has released its nominations for best screenplay. Here’s a quick look at its picks. Stay tuned for the Bagger’s analysis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Writers Guild Unveils Picks for Screenplay Categories', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-03T18:24:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [],