/2014/01/04/can-upward-mobility-cost-you-your-health/', 'snippet': 'Poor kids who succeed suffer from higher obesity, stress and blood pressure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Americans love a good rags-to-riches story. Even in an age of soaring inequality, we like to think that people can still make it big here if they work hard and stay out of trouble. The socioeconomic reality of most of the last four decades — stagnant wages, soaring income and wealth inequality, and reduced equality of opportunity — have dented, but not destroyed, the appeal of the American dream.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Upward Mobility Cost You Your Health?', 'kicker': 'The Great Divide', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Can Upward Mobility Cost You Your Health?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-04T19:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory E. Miller, Edith Chen and GENE H. BRODY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Edith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Gene', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'BRODY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e30bd289-727c-55f1-9a63-c146d625be94', 'word_count': 1120, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e30bd289-727c-55f1-9a63-c146d625be94'}, {'abstract': 'The debate over the government’s role in creating opportunity and ending deprivation has flared anew, with inequality as acute as it was in the Roaring Twenties and the ranks of the poor and near-poor at record highs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/05/business/50-years-later-war-on-poverty-is-a-mixed-bag.html', 'snippet': 'The debate over the government’s role in creating opportunity and ending deprivation has flared anew, with inequality as acute as it was in the Roaring Twenties and the ranks of the poor and near-poor at record highs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — To many Americans, the war on poverty declared 50 years ago by President Lyndon B. Johnson has largely failed. The poverty rate has fallen only to 15 percent from 19 percent in two generations, and 46 million Americans live in households where the government considers their income scarcely adequate.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/us/JP-POVERTY/JP-POVERTY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 851, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/05/us/JP-POVERTY/JP-POVERTY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 851}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/us/JP-POVERTY/JP-POVERTY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/05/us/JP-POVERTY/JP-POVERTY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1445, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u