multimedia-theater pioneer Jay Scheib has created a live cinematic adaptation of Chekhov’s unfinished play.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/06/theater/jay-scheib-stages-platonov-or-the-disinherited.html', 'snippet': 'The director and multimedia-theater pioneer Jay Scheib has created a live cinematic adaptation of Chekhov’s unfinished play.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A century and a half ago, Anton Chekhov wrote a play while still a medical student. Never performed or published in his lifetime, it was discovered in a locked box years after his death. This sprawling 20-character work, left untitled, has subsequently appeared as “Play Without Title,” “Without Fathers,” “A Country Scandal,” “Don Juan in the Russian Manner” and as the film “Unfinished Piece for the Player Piano.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/plantonov/plantonov-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/plantonov/plantonov-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/plantonov/plantonov-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 630, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/plantonov/plantonov-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1260, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/plantonov/plantonov-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/plantonov/plantonov-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/plantonov/plantonov-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Double Dose of Chekhov', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Double Dose of Chekhov', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scheib, Jay', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chekhov, Anton', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kitchen, The (Manhattan, NY, Performance Space)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-05T22:18:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alexis Soloski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alexis', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soloski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f23fee20-756a-5d10-a0e6-988f68fe27b8', 'word_count': 1131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f23fee20-756a-5d10-a0e6-988f68fe27b8'}, {'abstract': 'In “The Trip to Echo Spring,” Olivia Laing combines literary analysis, memoir and travelogue in examining six writers and their alcoholism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/06/books/olivia-laings-the-trip-to-echo-spring.html', 'snippet': 'In “The Trip to Echo Spring,” Olivia Laing combines literary analysis, memoir and travelogue in examining six writers and their alcoholism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “The Sutton Place Story,” John Cheever describes Deborah, “not quite 3 years old,” who, because of her home environment, “had naturally come to assume that cocktails were the axis of the adult world. She made Martinis in the sand pile and thought all the illustrations of cups, goblets, and glasses in her nursery books were filled with old-fashioneds.” Cheever and five other writers who had alcohol much on their minds (and in their livers) are brought together in Olivia Laing’s “The Trip to Echo Spring,” a combination of literary analysis, memoir and travelogue that is most beguiling and incisive when rambling through the work and lives of Cheever, Raymond Carver, F. Scott Fitzgerald, Ernest Hemingway, Tennessee Williams and John Berryman.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/jpbook/jpbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/jpbook/jpbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 752, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/jpbook/jpbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 752, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/06/arts/jpbook/jpbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '