lue': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests (Medical)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-06T22:21:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/00f221d8-8789-5da0-8723-0150ce0cc628', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00f221d8-8789-5da0-8723-0150ce0cc628'}, {'abstract': 'The new CBS series “Intelligence” centers on an agent whose abilities have been broadened by a microchip implanted in his brain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/07/arts/television/josh-holloway-stars-in-intelligence.html', 'snippet': 'The new CBS series “Intelligence” centers on an agent whose abilities have been broadened by a microchip implanted in his brain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gabriel Vaughn, the commando turned human computer at the center of CBS’s melodramatic new action series “Intelligence,” has multiple operating modes. The microchip in his brain makes him a walking Google as well as a one-man surveillance state, able to distract terrorists by reciting their emails to them. He’s also his own wireless hot spot — in Tuesday’s pilot, he works the digital lock of a Pakistani interrogation cell from across the room. Where was this guy when it was taking 10 years to find Osama bin Laden?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/07/arts/07INTELLIGENCE/07INTELLIGENCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/07/arts/07INTELLIGENCE/07INTELLIGENCE-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumb