al': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/e42dc60a-60ce-5bbe-a0c5-e7be73766bd1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/e42dc60a-60ce-5bbe-a0c5-e7be73766bd1'}, {'abstract': 'Dark themes are explored after most of a family is shot dead in “The Kept,” James Scott’s first novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/08/books/the-kept-james-scotts-dark-and-mysterious-first-novel.html', 'snippet': 'Dark themes are explored after most of a family is shot dead in “The Kept,” James Scott’s first novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'James Scott’s debut novel, “The Kept,” opens on a scene of massacred children. It’s daring and bleak in a novel whose daring is found in its bleakness. Elspeth Howell, a midwife, returns to her family’s remote homestead in far upstate New York in a snowstorm to find four of her five children and her husband shot dead and left where they fell. Her fifth child, Caleb, has been hiding in a closet since three men came and gunned down the Howells. Thinking his mother is one of the gunmen returning to finish him off, Caleb shoots her, wounding her badly. If you’re expecting things to look up, read no further. What follows is an unrelentingly somber story of revenge played out across an austere landscape.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 871, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 871}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/08/arts/08BOOK/08BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 706, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'ima