rganizations', 'value': 'Bowl Championship Series', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Florida State University', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Auburn University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-07T22:42:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37daec9a-204c-55ae-b90f-4c6f73b9ec26', 'word_count': 1632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37daec9a-204c-55ae-b90f-4c6f73b9ec26'}, {'abstract': 'Guest blogger Ellie Feinglass looks what is standing in the way of victory in the fight against AIDS.', 'web_url': 'https://kristof.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/07/the-missing-piece-in-the-war-on-aids/', 'snippet': 'Guest blogger Ellie Feinglass looks what is standing in the way of victory in the fight against AIDS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One afternoon last spring I met a farmer named Salomão in a rural community in Southern Mozambique.\xa0 Salomão was in his late 30s, living with both HIV and tuberculosis, and very poor, with few possessions aside from the clothing on his back, some cooking pots, and his hoe.\xa0\xa0 In late March, he walked without shoes for eight hours in the pouring rain so that he could pick up his month’s supply of drugs.\xa0 When he got to the health center, he was tired and weak.\xa0 He was then humiliated and turned away because his health card was wet.\xa0 And in the process, Salomão’s HIV status – something that should be held in complete confidentiality – was disclosed to everyone who happened to be in the crowded waiting area at the outpatient clinic.\xa0\xa0 Upon returning to his village, Salomão was shunned by his family and many of his neighbors.\xa0 Without his medicine, his health deteriorated rapidly.\xa0 By May, he had developed several abscesses on his leg.\xa0 He resisted returning to the health facility.\xa0 Within a few weeks Salomão died at home, alone, and in silence.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Missing Piece in the War on AIDS', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-07T22:44:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ellie Feinglass', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ellie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Feinglass', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/305351d6-c063-548b-bbe5-a895c606f906', 'word_count': 873, 'uri': 'nyt://article/305351d6-c063-548b-bbe5-a895c606f906'}, {'abstract': 'JPMorgan Chase could have dug deeper despite an obligation to monitor accounts for suspicious activity, writes Peter J. Henning. But the victims of the fraud will be getting some repayment after prosecutors got creative, he says.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/07/what-the-jpmorgan-settlement-means/', 'snippet': 'JPMorgan Chase could have dug deeper despite an obligation to monitor accounts for suspicious activity, writes Peter J. Henning. But the victims of the fraud will be getting some repayment after prosecutors got creative, he says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JPMorgan Chase’s latest criminal settlement shows that prosecutors are becoming more creative about getting money to victims of a fraud rather than just putting it into federal coffers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'