7b412962-1323-5bac-917d-ef1b6f42edc4'}, {'abstract': 'Thomas Vinterberg, director of Denmark’s foreign-language Oscar hopeful, “The Hunt,” speaks about his involvement with the Dogma 95 movement and how his new film grew out of an earlier success.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/08/from-denmark-a-hopeful-contends-with-his-own-legacy/', 'snippet': 'Thomas Vinterberg, director of Denmark’s foreign-language Oscar hopeful, “The Hunt,” speaks about his involvement with the Dogma 95 movement and how his new film grew out of an earlier success.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The Danish director Thomas Vinterberg remembers the exact moment the idea for “The Hunt,” one of the nine films shortlisted in the Oscar competition for best foreign-language film, began percolating in his head. A psychological drama in which Mads Mikkelsen is cast as a timid kindergarten teacher unjustly dragged into scandal, “The Hunt” grew directly out of one of Mr. Vinterberg’s earlier films, “The Celebration,” a 1998 prizewinner at Cannes about a family rocked by allegations of sexual abuse. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Denmark, a Hopeful Contends With His Own Legacy', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mikkelsen, Mads', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vinterberg, Thomas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Denmark', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-08T13:30:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Larry Rohter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Larry', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ee043d3-7144-5f9c-b85b-53e90cfff23e', 'word_count': 1213, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ee043d3-7144-5f9c-b85b-53e90cfff23e'}, {'abstract': 'JPMorgan’s legal penalties now total $20 billion. | A former SAC employee is expected to be the first witness called in insider trading trial. | Goldman Sachs is reshuffling its technology, media and telecommunications group.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/08/morning-agenda-jpmorgan-settles-madoff-charges/', 'snippet': 'JPMorgan’s legal penalties now total $20 billion. | A former SAC employee is expected to be the first witness called in insider trading trial. | Goldman Sachs is reshuffling its technology, media and telecommunications group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JPMORGAN SETTLES MADOFF CHARGES \xa0|\xa0 Over the last year, JPMorgan Chase has agreed to legal penalties now totaling $20 billion, but the settlements have not taken as large a toll on the bank as the numbers might suggest, Peter Eavis writes in DealBook. On Tuesday, Preet Bharara, the United States attorney in Manhattan, imposed a record $1.7 billion fine for felony violations of the Bank Secrecy Act for failing to report Bernard L. Madoff’s suspicious activities. On top of that, the bank agreed to pay $350 million to the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, bringing the total settlement to $2 billion.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'JPMorgan Settles Madoff Charges', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fines (Penalties)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of I