ations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bailey, Harry C', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T01:26:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kirk Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kirk', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0cf1a138-091a-54fd-85f3-db315b8283ed', 'word_count': 627, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0cf1a138-091a-54fd-85f3-db315b8283ed'}, {'abstract': 'Semiretired in recent years, he is back in a new role and with a new name. He isn’t lonely anymore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/09/business/media/maytag-repairmans-new-job-keeps-him-busy.html', 'snippet': 'Semiretired in recent years, he is back in a new role and with a new name. He isn’t lonely anymore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IN 1967, three years after Mr. Whipple began imploring shoppers to stop squeezing Charmin in the toilet paper aisle, another melancholic brand character was introduced, the Maytag Repairman, who described himself in his first commercial as “the loneliest man in town” — because the appliances never broke. Now the character, who was depicted by several actors over the decades, is getting a tuneup himself, with a new attitude, function — and name.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/09/business/Adco/Adco-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/09/business/Adco/Adco-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2