ations', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T17:51:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone and Thomas Erdbrink', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erdbrink', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e21bdfc-f31a-5856-a69a-a1ead9b04a01', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e21bdfc-f31a-5856-a69a-a1ead9b04a01'}, {'abstract': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'web_url': 'https://nocera.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/09/the-gun-report-january-9-2014/', 'snippet': 'A day in the life of armed America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First it was Instagram. Now, Reddit, the so-called “front page of the Internet,” has been exposed as an active marketplace for assault rifles, high-capacity magazines and other powerful firearms, according to an investigation by Mother Jones. The Guns for Sale page, which counts more than 7,200 subscribers, offers buyers and sellers a forum on the site, which boasts tens of millions of users and drives major traffic to other websites.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Gun Report: January 9, 2014', 'kicker': 'Joe Nocera', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T17:56:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Mascia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mascia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b95d93b-bc9b-5f78-8b97-f65bda11fd82', 'word_count': 1369, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b95d93b-bc9b-5f78-8b97-f65bda11fd82'}, {'abstract': 'Seeking advice from parents who helped their teenagers inclined to be couch potatoes to stay active in winter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/booming/how-did-you-motivate-your-hibernating-teenager-to-stay-healthy-in-winter.html', 'snippet': 'Seeking advice from parents who helped their teenagers inclined to be couch potatoes to stay active in winter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s winter. In much of the country, it’s cold, often bleak, snowy, icy, rainy. It’s not appealing weather for getting outside and getting moving. Younger children see that differently: They see snow for sledding and ice for sliding, and really, why wouldn’t you want to go out? And teenagers who are already active in sports, dance, or enjoy a regular workout routine just keep going. But for teenagers who are even more inclined to hibernate in winter, how can you keep them moving and healthy?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/booming/10parent-booming-teens/10parent-booming-teens-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/booming/10parent-booming-teens/10parent-booming-teens-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/booming/10parent-booming-teens/10parent-booming-teens-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/booming/10