'By Peggy Post', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peggy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Post', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81884edd-79ef-5249-9436-8a77a6165308', 'word_count': 1197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81884edd-79ef-5249-9436-8a77a6165308'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition at City Reliquary traces the doughnut’s local history all the way back to the 17th century.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/nyregion/a-sweet-history-doughnuts-in-new-york-city.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition at City Reliquary traces the doughnut’s local history all the way back to the 17th century.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Miser grew up just a few towns south of the birthplace of that symbol of American low cuisine, Krispy Kreme. So captivating were those confections’ sugary spell that it wasn’t until adolescence that he was even aware of the existence of other doughnuts. Doughnuts, for all he knew, simply were Krispy Kremes, and vice versa. Dunkin’ Donuts, he assumed, was merely a figure of speech, not a rival purveyor.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10MISER_SPAN/10MISER_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10MISER_SPAN/10MISER_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10MISER_SPAN/10MISER_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10MISER_SPAN/10MISER_SPA