Princeton Symphony Orchestra, also leads a group in Spain, among his other commitments.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/nyregion/a-conductor-wields-a-baton-across-cultures.html', 'snippet': 'Rossen Milanov, the music director of the Princeton Symphony Orchestra, also leads a group in Spain, among his other commitments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The conductor Rossen Milanov balances two approaches when leading an orchestra. ', 'print_section': 'NJ', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN/12ARTSNJ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN/12ARTSNJ1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN/12ARTSNJ1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN/12ARTSNJ1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN/12ARTSNJ1_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conductor Wields a Baton Across Cultures', 'kicker': 'Arts | New Jersey', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Wielding a Baton Across Cultures', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Milanov, Rossen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Princeton Symphony Orchestra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Princeton (NJ)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T21:19:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tammy La Gorce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tammy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'La Gorce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ce57bcf-2070-5bfe-a0b1-3568f7ff8237', 'word_count': 944, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ce57bcf-2070-5bfe-a0b1-3568f7ff8237'}, {'abstract': 'In E. L. Doctorow’s new novel, a scientist tells the story of his puzzling life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/andrews-brain-by-e-l-doctorow.html', 'snippet': 'In E. L. Doctorow’s new novel, a scientist tells the story of his puzzling life.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ve always responded to the history of my times,” says the beleaguered narrator of “Andrew’s Brain,” almost ruefully, as if he wished it weren’t so. He has no choice, though: Responding to the history of one’s times is the sworn duty of a character in a novel by E. L. Doctorow, who has in his half-century of writing fiction placed a remarkable number of people, both real and imagined, in their history just to watch them respond. At first Andrew, whose time is the bewilderingly eventful opening decade of this century, doesn’t sound like much of a witness: A self-\xaddescribed “freakishly depressive cognitive scientist klutz,” he seems far more interested in the workings of his own mind than those of the world outside, and for a long time speaks — to someone he calls Doc — only of his messy private life and his largely fruitless research into the mysteries of the human brain. He cracks cynical jokes, tells parts of his life story in the third person, indulges in a fair bit of glib self-analysis (“I am finally, terribly, unfeeling”) and generally plays the fool. “Andrew’s Brain” is cunning. It isn’t hard to figure out that Andrew is an unreliable narrator, but by the end of this sly book Doctorow has you wondering if there’s any other kind.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Rafferty/0112-bks-Rafferty-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 416, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Rafferty/0112-bks-Rafferty-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 416}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Rafferty/0112-bks-Rafferty-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 710, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Rafferty/0112-bks-Rafferty-superJumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 1419, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Rafferty/0112-bks-Rafferty-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Rafferty/0112-bks-Rafferty-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Rafferty/0112-bks-Rafferty-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Mind’s Jailer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Mind’s Jailer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doctorow, E L', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T21:29:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terrence Rafferty', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terrence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rafferty', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45bad3a3-fd65-56eb-9d97-7fff3fe3569a', 'word_count': 1185, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45bad3a3-fd65-56eb-9d97-7fff3fe3569a'}, {'abstract': 'An author drops in on a book club discussing his novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/i-read-you-loud-and-clear.html', 'snippet': 'An author drops in on a book club discussing his novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I love book clubs. I love reading for them, I love talking to them, and if I had my choice I’d probably do nothing but visit them to promote my books. Where else do you find people who have already made a commitment to read your book, and to read it closely enough to discuss it in a knowledgeable fashion with their friends? The best insights I’ve ever been offered about my work have come from book club members. In a world full of readings attended by the inevitable, random 5-to-10 bookstore browsers and 20-year-old assistant night managers who consistently mangle the title of your work, book clubs are an oasis of intelligent thought and discussion.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-BAKER/0112-bks-BAKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 242, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-BAKER/0112-bks-BAKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 242}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-BAKER/0112-bks-BAKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 413, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-BAKER/0112-bks-BAKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 825, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-BAKER/0112-bks-BAKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-BAKER/0112-bks-BAKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-BAKER/0112-bks-BAKER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbL