andard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/09/blogs/20140109POD-slide-SHXI/20140109POD-slide-SHXI-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pictures of the Day: South Sudan and Elsewhere', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:01:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5bf97df1-248f-5046-b5c0-28e12618c1ff', 'word_count': 15, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5bf97df1-248f-5046-b5c0-28e12618c1ff'}, {'abstract': '“Devotion,” an exhibition at New York University’s 80WSE, shows a surprising range in the work by Bob Mizer, known for his erotic photographs of men.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/arts/design/devotion-excavating-bob-mizer-at-nyus-80wse.html', 'snippet': '“Devotion,” an exhibition at New York University’s 80WSE, shows a surprising range in the work by Bob Mizer, known for his erotic photographs of men.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1951, Bob Mizer began Physique Pictorial, a magazine loaded with photographs of muscular young men wearing little more than posing straps — G-strings for men. Its imagery ostensibly illustrated health and fitness, but was really aimed at what was then an underground gay market. Mizer (1922-92) was a pioneer in making explicit the genre’s erotic appeal, enough so to attract attention from legal authorities. In 1947, he was convicted of contributing to the delinquency of a minor by taking nude pictures of a 17-year-old, and he spent a year in prison. In 1954, he was convicted of distributing obscene material through the mail. (That conviction was reversed on appeal a couple of years later.) Those speed bumps didn’t stop Mizer’s enterprise as a commercial photographer and publisher from growing to the point at which it occupied four lots near downtown Los Angeles, like a Hollywood movie studio, earning him the nickname “the Hugh Hefner of gay publishing.” Mizer, evidently, was blessedly free of high-art pretension, but Physique Pictorial was nevertheless an inspiration for artists like David Hockney and Robert Mapplethorpe. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '33', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beyond Beefcake in the Work of a Gay Pioneer', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beyond Beefcake In the Work Of a Gay Pioneer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mizer, Bob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:05:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/585361a0-23f1-5868-8da2-600ff48ad770', 'word_count': 985, 'uri': 'nyt://article/585361a0-23f1-5868-8da2-600ff48ad770'}, {'abstract': 'Two recent cases involving a patient kept alive by ventilator despite a diagnosis of brain death raise questions about exactly what this term means.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/health/the-science-behind-brain-death.html', 'snippet': 'Two recent cases involving a patient kept alive by ventilator despite a diagnosis of brain death raise questions about exactly what this term means.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In one way, the cases are polar opposites: the parents of Jahi McMath in Oakland, Calif., have fought to keep their daughter connected to a ventilator, while the parents and husband of Marlise Muñoz in Fort Worth, Tex., want desperately to turn the machine off. In another way, the cases are identical: both families have been shocked to learn that a loved one was declared brain-dead — and that hospital officials defied the family’s wishes for treatment.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/us/BRAINDEAD-1/BRAINDEAD-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 377, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/us/BRAINDEAD-1/BRAINDEAD-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 377}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/us/BRAINDEAD-1/BRAINDEAD-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/us/BRAINDEAD-1/BRAINDEAD-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1287, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/us/BRAINDEAD-1/BRAINDEAD-1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/10/us/BRAINDEAD-1/BRAINDEAD-1-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/us/BRAINDEAD-1/BRAINDEAD-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At Issue in 2 Wrenching Cases: What to Do After the Brain Dies', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Issue in 2 Wrenching Cases: What to Do After the Brain Dies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Life-Sustaining Support Systems, Withdrawal Of', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carey, Benedict', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coma', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grady, Denise', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Brain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Munoz, Marlise', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McMath, Jahi', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Institutional)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Death and Dying', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:07:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benedict Carey and Denise Grady', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benedict', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Denise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grady', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b0690bd9-64c9-5fa9-b447-19769e152f1e', 'word_count': 1128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b0690bd9-64c9-5fa9-b447-19769e152f1e'}, {'abstract': 'Even after three years in business, InThai is something of a secret beyond its area along Route 7 in Norwalk. The food is authentic, the prices low and the atmosphere friendly.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/nyregion/a-review-of-inthai-restaurant-in-norwalk.html', 'snippet': 'Even after three years in business, InThai is something of a secret beyond its area along Route 7 in Norwalk. The food is authentic, the prices low and the atmosphere friendly.', 'lead_paragraph': 'InThai Restaurant is handy for workers in several high-rise office buildings along Route 7 in Norwalk near the Wilton line, many of whom cross the road for a quick lunch or a takeout dinner. But even after three years in business, the restaurant is still something of a secret beyond that area, and those acquainted with its extremely low prices and good spicy Thai food may want it to retain its sleeper status a while longer. ', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12DINECT2/12DINECT2-t