e approximately every 18 months, critics began predicting that the era of “Moore’s Law” would draw to a close.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/science/10markoff/10markoff-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/science/10markoff/10markoff-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/science/10markoff/10markoff-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/science/10markoff/10markoff-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/science/10markoff/10markoff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/10/science/10markoff/10markoff-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/science/10markoff/10markoff-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Designing the Next Wave of Computer Chips', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nanotechnology', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Chips', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:41:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Markoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Markoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e758f8a3-0006-5292-9654-0c3dcf3e1817', 'word_count': 1099, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e758f8a3-0006-5292-9654-0c3dcf3e1817'}, {'abstract': 'In “a quartet,” Heather Kravas plays with patterns and the word “want.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/arts/dance/heather-kravass-a-quartet-in-a-new-york-premiere.html', 'snippet': 'In “a quartet,” Heather Kravas plays with patterns and the word “want.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the start of Heather Kravas’s “a quartet,” the work’s four dancers stand in line like serious kids pretending to be a train. As they repeatedly bend forward and arch back, their collective motion — switching between unison, relay and counterpoint — takes on the aspect of a stationary machine. Then, as their pelvises rock, they add a word — “want” — which becomes a mantra. Accelerating, the sequence approaches the edge of comedy.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10KRAVAS_SPAN/KRAVAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10KRAVAS_SPAN/KRAVAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10KRAVAS_SPAN/KRAVAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10KRAVAS_SPAN/KRAVAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10KRAVAS_SPAN/KRAVAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10KRAVAS_SPAN/KRAVAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10KRAVAS_SPAN/KRAVAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Barbershops Here, Nor Violins or Cellos', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Barbershops Here, Nor Violins or Cellos', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kravas, Heather', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:44:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/03b56d4b-b19c-5d8e-805c-de3b31fecf64', 'word_count': 464, 'uri': 'nyt://article/03b56d4b-b19c-5d8e-805c-de3b31fecf64'}, {'abstract': '“In Bloom,” Georgia’s entry for the Best Foreign Language Film Oscar, tracks the lives of two girls in Tbilisi in 1992, during the newly independent country’s civil war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/movies/in-bloom-on-two-young-girls-growing-up-in-tbilisi.html', 'snippet': '“In Bloom,” Georgia’s entry for the Best Foreign Language Film Oscar, tracks the lives of two girls in Tbilisi in 1992, during the newly independent country’s civil war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eka and Natia , the 14-year-old best friends at the heart of “In Bloom,” live in a place that seems to be crumbling at the edges. It’s 1992, and their homeland, the former Soviet republic of Georgia, is at war. The fighting, in the northern Abkhazia region, is far from the capital, Tbilisi, where the girls live, but the conflict pops up frequently in the adult conversations, at least when the grown-ups are not screaming and cursing about other things. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Coming-of-Age in Wartime', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Coming-of-Age in Wartime', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'In Bloom (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ekvtimishvili, Nana', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gross, Simon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:44:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b138a428-d13b-5fc5-9edc-0c00a25db843', 'word_count': 550, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b138a428-d13b-5fc5-9edc-0c00a25db843'}, {'abstract': 'In his show “New Sculpture,” at the Gagosian Gallery, Richard Serra continues along the road that emerged from the hugely successful “Torqued Ellipses” of the ’90s, but also circles back to his earlier oeuvre.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/arts/design/richard-serra-at-the-gagosian-gallery.html', 'snippet': 'In his show “New Sculpture,” at the Gagosian Gallery, Richard Serra continues along the road that emerged from the hugely successful “Torqued Ellipses” of the ’90s, but also circles back to his earlier oeuvre.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the end of December, the PBS host Charlie Rose conducted a curious interview with Richard Serra about his new show in two locations of the Gagosian Gallery in New York, and it quickly became popular on the Internet. The conversation was notable mostly because it reached an uncomfortable deadlock over how to talk about Mr. Serra’s sculpture. (Mr. Rose thought one work in Mr. Serra’s new show looked like a cemetery; Mr. Serra disagreed. Repeat.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10SERRA1_SPAN/SERRA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10SERRA1_SPAN/SERRA1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10SERRA1_SPAN/SERRA1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10SERRA1_SPAN/SERRA1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10SERRA1_SPAN/SERRA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10SERRA1_SPAN/SERRA1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10SERRA1_SPAN/SERRA1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shifting His Tectonic Plates', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shifting His Tectonic Plates', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Serra, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sculpture', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Gagosian Gallery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:45:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Schwendener', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwendener', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/2be4d0a7-83cf-5f51-9a85-7c242f5d7ca7', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2be4d0a7-83cf-5f51-9a85-7c242f5d7ca7'}, {'abstract': 'Robert Glasper and Jason Moran headlined a 75th anniversary concert for Blue Note Records on Wednesday at Town Hall.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/arts/music/celebrating-blue-note-records-at-town-hall.html', 'snippet': 'Robert Glasper and Jason Moran headlined a 75th anniversary concert for Blue Note Records on Wednesday at Town Hall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An anniversary concert for a record label, like an anniversary edition of a music festival, always comes preloaded with a burden of intention. What’s implicit is some form of brand reinforcement — but an album catalog contains its own narrative, and an artist roster has its own identity, and the two may not naturally converge. So one question for any label at a milestone is what to celebrate most: a corpus or an ethos? ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10GLASPER_SPAN/GLASPER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10GLASPER_SPAN/GLASPER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10GLASPER_SPAN/GLASPER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10GLASPER_SPAN/GLASPER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10GLASPER_SPAN/GLASPER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10GLASPER_SPAN/GLASPER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/10GLASPER_SPAN/GLASPER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Celebrating Age as Well as Attitude at a Record Label’s Anniversary Party', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Celebrating Age as Well as Attitude at a Record Label’s Anniversary Party', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jazz', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blue Note Records', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Glasper, Robert', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moran, Jason', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T22:52:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae7f0dff-5d4f-5547-b43f-8505b16139e7', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae7f0dff-5d4f-5547-b43f-8505b16139e7'}, {'abstract': 'The Detroit automaker, which sold fewer than 4,000 cars in Japan last year, will advertise the small car in a tough market where the competition is stiff.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/business/international/ford-looks-to-fiesta-to-increase-meager-sales-in-japan.html', 'snippet': 'The Detroit automaker, which sold fewer than 4,000 cars in Japan last year, will advertise the small car in a tough market where the competition is stiff.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Ford might be an auto giant in America, but odds are you will not spot one of its cars on Japan’s busy roads. Last year, the Detroit automaker sold all of 3,896 cars in Japan.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': Non