sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T23:16:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/dbbb1a7b-5aeb-573c-a068-e75046be9aea', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/dbbb1a7b-5aeb-573c-a068-e75046be9aea'}, {'abstract': 'The Metropolitan Museum is dotted with paintings that have been left incomplete, giving viewers hints into the impulses behind the genius of Degas, Bassano, Greuze, Rembrandt and others.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/arts/design/the-fascination-of-the-unfinished.html', 'snippet': 'The Metropolitan Museum is dotted with paintings that have been left incomplete, giving viewers hints into the impulses behind the genius of Degas, Bassano, Greuze, Rembrandt and others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unfinished paintings are enticing cracks in the facade of art history, lures along the path to a deeper understanding of artistic processes and impulses. For all the paintings that artists complete, countless others are left incomplete for any number of reasons — poverty or war, a change of plan or vision, the illness or death of the artist. While many of these works have been destroyed, and others forgotten, some are now recognized as significant works of art, accorded a special place in history and in an artist’s body of work, in part because they can bring us closer to understanding the mysterious process of painting, and, indeed, to painting’s future. After all, nothing inspires a young artist like a close look at how an earlier one worked.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Fascination of the Unfinished', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Fascination of the Unfinished', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-09T23:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24c05279-ba37-5047-8d35-36b062e52c9d', 'word_count': 1960, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24c05279-ba37-5047-8d35-36b062e52c9d'}, {'abstract': 'Apollo’s newest fund, the largest in its history, underscoring investors’ confidence that firm can find buying opportunities at a time when stock prices have risen drastically.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/09/apollo-secures-18-4-billion-war-chest-for-private-equity/', 'snippet': 'Apollo’s newest fund, the largest in its history, underscoring investors’ confidence that firm can find buying opportunities at a time when stock prices have risen drastically.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leon D. Black, the head of Apollo Global Management, said last year that his firm was “selling everything that’s not nailed down.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apollo Secures $18.4 Billion War Chest for Private Equity', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Company Reports', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Equity', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Leon D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name'