d_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5a401b8-b609-53b5-9c28-6c5ccdaa447c'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor Rahm Emanuel of Chicago said Thursday that he would ask a federal judge who declared the city’s ban on firearms sales unconstitutional to give lawmakers six months to adopt a new ordinance regulating gun shops.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/us/illinois-chicago-wants-six-months-to-revise-rules-on-gun-sales.html', 'snippet': 'Mayor Rahm Emanuel of Chicago said Thursday that he would ask a federal judge who declared the city’s ban on firearms sales unconstitutional to give lawmakers six months to adopt a new ordinance regulating gun shops.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Mayor Rahm Emanuel of Chicago said Thursday that he would ask a federal judge who declared the city’s ban on firearms sales unconstitutional to give lawmakers six months to adopt a new ordinance regulating gun shops. The judge, Edmond E. Chang of the Northern District of Illinois, signaled in his decision on\xa0Monday\xa0that Chicago could still restrict gun transactions as long as they stopped short of a complete ban. “Though I fully disagree, the city has little option but to follow the ruling of the court,” Mr. Emanuel, who will not file an appeal, said in a statement. A status hearing is scheduled for next week. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Illinois: Chicago Wants Six Months to Revise Rules on Gun Sales', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Midwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Illinois: Chicago Wants Six Months To Revise Rules On Gun Sales', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yaccino, Steven', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emanuel, Rahm', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gun Control', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T03:06:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Yaccino', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yaccino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7f38ecc-a0b3-56b5-be8d-97c6c924497d', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7f38ecc-a0b3-56b5-be8d-97c6c924497d'}, {'abstract': 'Traffic snarled again in Fort Lee, N.J., as the governor visited on Thursday afternoon to express his regrets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/nyregion/christie-brings-his-remorse-to-an-inconvenienced-borough.html', 'snippet': 'Traffic snarled again in Fort Lee, N.J., as the governor visited on Thursday afternoon to express his regrets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FORT LEE, N.J. — Nobody had ever claimed that Mark Sokolich was one of the most powerful mayors in America. But on Thursday afternoon, Chris Christie, one of the most powerful governors in the country, flew to Mr. Sokolich’s borough to deliver a personal apology.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/nyregion/ft-lee-1/ft-lee-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/nyregion/ft-lee-1/ft-lee-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/nyregion/ft-lee-1/ft-lee-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank'