nyt://article/76b3d2b6-e8a3-5e44-b5c7-b251691fc49f', 'word_count': 655, 'uri': 'nyt://article/76b3d2b6-e8a3-5e44-b5c7-b251691fc49f'}, {'abstract': 'The author of “The World Until Yesterday” on what we can learn from traditional societies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/jared-diamond-new-guinean-kids-are-not-brats.html', 'snippet': 'The author of “The World Until Yesterday” on what we can learn from traditional societies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Your latest book, “The World Until Yesterday,” is about traditional societies and your research in New Guinea. Why is the acronym Weird central to the book?  In Weird — Western, Educated, Industrialized, Rich and Democratic — societies we take these things for granted that just didn’t exist anywhere in the world until a few thousand years ago. We encounter strangers, and it’s normal, and we don’t freak out and try to kill them. We eat food that somebody else grew for us. We have a government with police and lawyers to settle disputes.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12talk/12talk-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 976, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12talk/12talk-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 976}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12talk/12talk-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 630, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12talk/12talk-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1259, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption':