es.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At 10 p.m. on New Year’s Eve, I hailed a taxi outside my apartment in Adams Morgan and directed it to a friend’s apartment in Shaw, a nearby neighborhood, also in Northwest Washington. It took a mere minute to find an open car, and the traffic-clogged two-mile trip cost $13, including tip.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12economy/mag-12Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 435, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12economy/mag-12Economy-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 435}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12economy/mag-12Economy-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 743, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12economy/mag-12Economy-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1350, 'width': 1860, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12economy/mag-12Economy-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12economy/mag-12Economy-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12economy/mag-12Economy-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Uber’s Surge-Pricing an Example of High-Tech Gouging?', 'kicker': "It's the Economy", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fare Game', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Uber Technologies Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lowrey, Annie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Car Services and Livery Cabs', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kalanick, Travis', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e1056f7-a71d-5ad0-aff4-843293f94a50', 'word_count': 1437, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e1056f7-a71d-5ad0-aff4-843293f94a50'}, {'abstract': 'Shaun White cut his hair, changed his clothes and became a businessman. Now if only he could master that pesky triple cork.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/the-flying-tomato-would-rather-you-not-call-him-that-anymore.html', 'snippet': 'Shaun White cut his hair, changed his clothes and became a businessman. Now if only he could master that pesky triple cork.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Shaun White sat in a lawn chair on the side of the mountain at Northstar California, a ski resort near Lake Tahoe, trying to think of a nice way to ask one of the young men grooming the hill to shovel some snow up onto the air bag, please. White was there in April to learn a triple cork 1440, a snowboarding trick that requires flipping three times in the air while moving through three different planes — horizontal, lateral and vertical — all while doing four 360-degree spins. Landing a triple cork is considered essential to winning slopestyle, a snowboarding event that will debut in the Olympic Games next month and that involves riding down a course filled with jumps and rails, sort of like those in a skateboard park. Because White had not yet mastered the triple cork, he needed to practice the trick by crash-landing into a giant yellow air bag. Judging from his emphatic cursing, the impact felt horrible.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12white-cover/12white-cover-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12white-cover/12white-cover-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12white-cover/12white-cover-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12white-cover/12white-cover-superJumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 1151, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12white-cover/12white-cover-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12white-cover/12white-cover-thumbStandard-v4.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12white-cover/12white-cover-thumbLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Flying Tomato Would Rather You Not Call Him That Anymore', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Future Boy Grows Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Shaun', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weil, Elizabeth', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snowboarding', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2014)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'X Games', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Weil', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weil', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bde0125-0ba2-5df1-a65c-72a6a01daf9b', 'word_count': 6086, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bde0125-0ba2-5df1-a65c-72a6a01daf9b'}, {'abstract': 'Unwanted items: one precious, one lurid.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/care-of-my-wifes-lovers-neighbor.html', 'snippet': 'Unwanted items: one precious, one lurid.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I received an envelope that was addressed to my home but did not include a name. I recognized the last name on the return-address label. I opened the envelope and realized it was intended for my neighbor, a woman I have known, along with her husband, for many years. The letter chronicled the dates and locations of an affair the sender claimed his wife was having with my neighbor’s husband. I regret reading the letter. Upon looking more closely at the envelope, I was able to discern my neighbor’s name (the wife) and the words “private and confidential,” but these were obscured by the postmark. It seems the sender intentionally sent the letter to my home to keep the husband from intercepting it, counting on me to deliver it to the wife. Now that the envelope is open, the neighbors will know that I have read the allegations. What is my obligation: To deliver the letter or to inform the sender that this plan didn’t go as intended? NAME WITHHELD', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Care of: My Wife’s Lover’s Neighbor', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Care of: My Wife’s Lover’s Neighbor', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADULTERY', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ivory', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Letters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Waste Materials and Disposal', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fur', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/aa0efcba-68d4-5f7c-ae5e-f196a9b6a21e', 'word_count': 848, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aa0efcba-68d4-5f7c-ae5e-f196a9b6a21e'}, {'abstract': 'How a simple experiment about impulse control became a seductive gospel truth.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/we-didnt-eat-the-marshmallow-the-marshmallow-ate-us.html', 'snippet': 'How a simple experiment about impulse control became a seductive gospel truth.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a series of famous experiments in the 1960s and ’70s conducted by the Stanford psychologist Walter Mischel, preschoolers were invited to sit alone in a room furnished only with a small desk. On the desk sat two marshmallows (or equivalently tempting treats) and a bell. The researcher told each child that he had to leave, but that when he returned, she could eat both marshmallows. If she wanted one marshmallow before then, however, she could ring the bell and eat one, but not both. Then the researcher shut the door, leaving the child alone with the forbidden marshmallows.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12riff/mag-12Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 87, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12riff/mag-12Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 87}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12riff/mag-12Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 148, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12riff/mag-12Riff-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 297, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12riff/mag-12Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12riff/mag-12Riff-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12riff/mag-12Riff-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'We Didn’t Eat the Marshmallow. The Marshmallow Ate Us.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'We Didn’t Eat the Marshmallow. The Marshmallow Ate Us.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bourne, Michael', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mischel, Walter', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sociology', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Bourne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bourne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3559dd2-26cc-54ff-835a-dba84ace9d0c', 'word_count': 1871, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3559dd2-26cc-54ff-835a-dba84ace9d0c'}, {'abstract': 'Sweet or savory, pâte à choux is easier than it looks. Really.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/no-puff-piece.html', 'snippet': 'Sweet or savory, pâte à choux is easier than it looks. Really.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A friend who almost never cooks recently made gougères — baked puff pastries with cheese. “I could not believe how easy that was,” he told me. In fact, I can’t think of anything as impressive that needs so little work.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12eat1/12eat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12eat1/12eat1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12eat1/12eat1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12eat1/12eat1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12eat1/12eat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12eat1/12eat1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12eat1/12eat1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'No Puff Piece', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'No Puff Piece', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bittman, Mark', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Desserts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Bittman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebe2e5a1-b8c7-5160-81d8-78f612c89d09', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebe2e5a1-b8c7-5160-81d8-78f612c89d09'}, {'abstract': 'The winner of the novel-writing contest was a complete unknown, right down to the fact that he was serving a life sentence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/the-murderer-and-the-manuscript.html', 'snippet': 'The winner of the novel-writing contest was a complete unknown, right down to the fact that he was serving a life sentence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just before Labor Day in 2011, Toni Kirkpatrick, an editor at Thomas Dunne Books, an imprint of St. Martin’s Press, picked up her phone and called an unfamiliar number with a Southern California area code. She was hoping to deliver good news to a man named Alaric Hunt, the newest winner of a debut-detective-novel writing contest, jointly sponsored by Minotaur Books (another St. Martin’s imprint) and the Private Eye Writers of America. The contest has a good track record — a past winner, Michael Koryta, a 21-year-old phenom, has since published 10 successful thrillers — and it also comes with a substantial prize: a $10,000 advance and a guaranteed publishing contract for the book that has been submitted.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/09/magazine/9-alaric-letter-full/9-alaric-letter-full-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/09/magazine/9-alaric-letter-full/9-alaric-letter-full-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/09/magazine/9-alaric-letter-full/9-alaric-letter-full-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/09/magazine/9-alaric-letter-full/9-alaric-letter-full-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/09/magazine/9-alaric-letter-full/9-alaric-letter-full-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/09/magazine/9-alaric-letter-full/9-alaric-letter-full-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/09/magazine/9-alaric-letter-full/9-alaric-letter-full-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Murderer and the Manuscript', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Mysterious Case of The Murderer And the Manuscript', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Cuts Through Bone (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunt, Alaric', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Weinman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weinman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f5d9b49b-d1f8-52cb-9099-1358a3052240', 'word_count': 2737, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f5d9b49b-d1f8-52cb-9099-1358a3052240'}, {'abstract': 'The nearly lost art of American textile manufacturing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/fruits-of-the-loom.html', 'snippet': 'The nearly lost art of American textile manufacturing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1776, America didn’t have a single textile mill. There were no spinning mules, no water-powered looms. There were only rumors of what such things might look like and a few nonfunctioning models built from those rumors. Nearly every American woman, except the wealthiest, knew how to spin her own yarn and weave her own cloth, even as across the Atlantic women were moving out of the home and into millwork as England — bent on protecting its export market by safeguarding its trade secrets — industrialized the manufacturing of textiles.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fruits of the Loom', 'kicker': 'Photo Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fruits of the Loom', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Galchen, Rivka', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Textiles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wool and Woolen Goods', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Photographs by Christopher Payne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Photographs', 'middlename': 'Christopher', 'lastname': 'Payne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed34daad-5828-5166-868f-80776e6a4a10', 'word_count': 1216, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed34daad-5828-5166-868f-80776e6a4a10'}, {'abstract': 'She spent half a lifetime trying to escape the shadow of her famous father. But for her latest album, she went back to the South.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/magazine/rosanne-cash-goes-home.html', 'snippet': 'She spent half a lifetime trying to escape the shadow of her famous father. But for her latest album, she went back to the South.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On an icy December morning, Rosanne Cash was making tea in the kitchen of the Chelsea brownstone she shares with her husband, the producer-songwriter-guitarist John Leventhal, and their 14-year-old son, Jakob. Her 25-year-old daughter Carrie, also a singer and the youngest of three children that Cash had with her ex-husband, the singer-songwriter Rodney Crowell, was also staying there for a while. It was a full house, and Cash seemed to like it that way. Carrie appeared from a downstairs bedroom to ask Cash to help her find the “save the date” cards she wanted to get in the mail for her May wedding to Dan Knobler, a guitarist and producer. Mother and daughter moved around a partly finished jigsaw puzzle on a nearby table, and Cash began looking through a cabinet, pausing to answer a phone call from a close friend, the former New Yorker TV critic Nancy Franklin. Soon the cards were found, and Carrie was off to address them.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12cash1/12cash1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12cash1/12cash1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 727}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12cash1/12cash1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 846, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12cash1/12cash1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1691, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12cash1/12cash1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12cash1/12cash1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/magazine/12cash1/12cash1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rosanne Cash Goes Home', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rosanne Cash Goes Home', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cash, Rosanne', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tucker, Ken', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cash, Johnny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Country Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Folk Music', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T10:00:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ken Tucker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ken', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tucker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/407a57a1-b8c3-526