antastical abilities, including the power of speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The cherished bond between humans and horses is celebrated in many beloved children’s books, from “National Velvet” to “War Horse.” But when the real connection is already palpable, why do writers take their horse stories deeply into the realm of the imaginary? Two new books for middle-grade readers use different approaches to the equestrian genre to explore these fantasies.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Dunham-kids/0112-bks-Dunham-kids-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Dunham-kids/0112-bks-Dunham-kids-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 599, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Dunham-kids/0112-bks-Dunham-kids-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Dunham-kids/0112-bks-Dunham-kids-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Dunham-kids/0112-bks-Dunham-kids-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Unbridled', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unbridled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunham, Jillian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lean, Sarah', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lasky, Kathryn', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:13:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jillian Dunham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jillian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dunham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfd369d5-381c-54d4-ac96-c06af623239b', 'word_count': 574, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfd369d5-381c-54d4-ac96-c06af623239b'}, {'abstract': 'The daughter of a traumatized war veteran must care for her father and survive senior year in Laurie Halse Anderson’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/laurie-halse-andersons-impossible-knife-of-memory.html', 'snippet': 'The daughter of a traumatized war veteran must care for her father and survive senior year in Laurie Halse Anderson’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Beginning with the publication of her award-winning young adult novel “Speak” in 1999, Laurie Halse Anderson has written with honesty and grit, bravely shedding light on such once-taboo topics as rape, eating disorders, suicide and addiction. In doing so, she has helped build the current landscape of contemporary young adult literature. Anderson writes the hard truth, stirring debate and discussion among both fans and objectors, and ultimately creating long overdue conversations about the real issues teenagers face every day.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Line of Fire', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Line of Fire', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Anderson, Laurie Halse', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:13:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jo Knowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Knowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/37d24cbd-e7ae-5c8a-a0a0-29e66e54fedc', 'word_count': 718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37d24cbd-e7ae-5c8a-a0a0-29e66e54fedc'}, {'abstract': 'James Parker joins the list of Bookends contributors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/poems-protests-and-prayers.html', 'snippet': 'James Parker joins the list of Bookends contributors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the next few weeks, we welcome three new writers to our roster of Bookends contributors: James Parker, Thomas Mallon and Leslie Jamison. Parker, who makes his initial appearance this week, is a contributing editor at The Atlantic, where he has written about everyone from Norman Rockwell and Martin Amis to Charlie Sheen and the lead singer of the 1980s hair-metal band Ratt. He was a staff writer at The Boston Phoenix and in 2008 won a Deems Taylor Award for music criticism from the American Society of Composers, Authors and Publishers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12openbook/12openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 420, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12openbook/12openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth':