sons', 'value': 'Marsa, Linda', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'MacKinnon, J B', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:15:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Coral Davenport', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Coral', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davenport', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/30a8ad3b-03e9-5315-b019-632d59df3f8a', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30a8ad3b-03e9-5315-b019-632d59df3f8a'}, {'abstract': 'At 92, the novelist and short-story writer Elizabeth Spencer remains a distinguished regional voice.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/starting-over-by-elizabeth-spencer.html', 'snippet': 'At 92, the novelist and short-story writer Elizabeth Spencer remains a distinguished regional voice.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elizabeth Spencer published her first novel in 1948. Seven more followed, along with five volumes of short stories and the novella “The Light in the Piazza.” There would also be a play and an exquisite memoir. Now there is a new book of stories, “Starting Over.” Given that Spencer is 92, it’s tempting to call the title audacious, but since there seems to be nothing this extraordinary writer can’t do, maybe she’s just being realistic.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12JONES/12JONES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12JONES/12JONES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 741}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12JONES/12JONES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 830, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12JONES/12JONES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1217, 'width': 986, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12JONES/12JONES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12JONES/12JONES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12JONES/12JONES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Southern Landscape', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Southern Landscape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spencer, Elizabeth (1921-2019)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:19:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Malcolm Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Malcolm', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5101a73f-f47e-54d3-9185-b892473de627', 'word_count': 1228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5101a73f-f47e-54d3-9185-b892473de627'}, {'abstract': 'In Richard Powers’s novel, a composer’s experiment with sound and microbiology attracts the attention of Homeland Security.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/orfeo-by-richard-powers.html', 'snippet': 'In Richard Powers’s novel, a composer’s experiment with sound and microbiology attracts the attention of Homeland Security.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Is it premature to talk of the “Powers Problem”? For the last three decades, Richard Powers has been bringing out hefty novels at the rate of one every 2.5 years: 11 in all. At his current age of 56, he is, as a novelist, midway on life’s path; presumably he has another 11 or so novels still in him. Powers has won a National Book Award and has been a finalist for the Pulitzer Prize; he has been the recipient of a MacArthur “genius grant”; he has elicited lavish praise from the critics — most of them, anyway. Two of the words most frequently employed in connection with his literary output are “cerebral” and “ambitious.” “Cerebral” refers to his tendency to lace his novels with scientific and scholarly themes, like artificial intelligence in “Galatea 2.2,” game theory in “Prisoner’s Dilemma” and musicology cum genetic recombination in “The Gold Bug Variations.” “Ambitious” refers to his penchant for fashioning narrative structures and symbolic networks on a heroic scale.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-HOLT/0112-bks-HOLT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 624, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-HOLT/0112-bks-HOLT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 624}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-HOLT/0112-bks-HOLT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 986, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-HOLT/0112-bks-HOLT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1931, 'width': 1858, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-HOLT/0112-bks-HOLT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-HOLT/0112-bks-HOLT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-HOLT/0112-bks-HOLT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Music Gene', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Music Gene', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Powers, Richard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:19:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Holt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/670cd7b1-99c7-5997-86d2-5292a9b05616', 'word_count': 1329, 'uri': 'nyt://article/670cd7b1-99c7-5997-86d2-5292a9b05616'}, {'abstract': 'Diane Johnson, who writes frequently about France, discovers the appeal of her native Midwest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/diane-johnsons-flyover-lives.html', 'snippet': 'Diane Johnson, who writes frequently about France, discovers the appeal of her native Midwest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As a diminutive child growing up in the “landlocked Illinois” town of Moline, where John Deere built his plow-and-\xadtractor empire, Diane Johnson (author of the popular novel “Le Divorce,” among other books about Americans abroad) dreamed of a life of adventure, “preferably at sea, before the mast.” She fled the Flyover (as coastal sophisticates call the Midwest), first to New York, where she was part of the same cohort of aspiring college students at Mademoiselle magazine that Sylvia Plath recorded in “The Bell Jar,” then to her first marriage to a doctor in California and finally to France, where she now spends much of her time. “The one thing you, we, Americans, are not allowed to say is that there is somewhere better than America to live,” she writes, confidently having found “one of the better places.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12benfey-sub/12benfey-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 729, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12benfey-sub/12benfey-sub-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 729}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12benfey-sub/12benfey-sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 729, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12benfey-sub/12benfey-sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 729, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'cr