'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Flying Through the Air', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'White, Shaun', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snowboarding', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lake Tahoe (Nev)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:19:57+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/ab930d2f-a04c-536e-be8d-b566cd7ebcb4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/ab930d2f-a04c-536e-be8d-b566cd7ebcb4'}, {'abstract': 'Elisabeth de Waal’s novel of betrayal and loss in postwar Vienna.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/the-exiles-return-by-elisabeth-de-waal.html', 'snippet': 'Elisabeth de Waal’s novel of betrayal and loss in postwar Vienna.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Elisabeth de Waal’s posthumously published novel takes place in the aftermath of some of the 20th century’s greatest calamities, as it follows Austrian exiles returning to their homeland to encounter a dramatically altered physical and psychological landscape. By 1954, when the action of the novel begins, Vienna has witnessed not only the dissolution of the Austro-Hungarian Empire but the Anschluss and Hitler’s reign of terror against the Jewish population, as well as repeated bombings during World War II and simultaneous occupation by four victorious foreign powers. Written during the late 1950s, “The Exiles Return” has an immediacy that makes de Waal’s readers feel the experiences of its characters in a visceral way.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12ERVIN/12ERVIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 285, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12ERVIN/12ERVIN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 285}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12ERVIN/12ERVIN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 949, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12ERVIN/12ERVIN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 451, 'width': 949, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12ERVIN/12ERVIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12ERVIN/12ERVIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12ERVIN/12ERVIN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Life in Transit', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Life in Transit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Waal, Edmund', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Waal, Elisabeth', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:20:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Ervin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ervin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/51bef4b6-a04d-5a15-a9ee-7ce3e4b6b9d0', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51bef4b6-a04d-5a15-a9ee-7ce3e4b6b9d0'}, {'abstract': 'An unexpected arrival stirs memories of war.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/the-bird-skinner-by-alice-greenway.html', 'snippet': 'An unexpected arrival stirs memories of war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If there were a prize for ornery old men, the ornithologist Jim Kennoway in Alice Greenway’s novel “The Bird Skinner” would trounce every recluse in New England. Refusing help from his remarkably well-\xadadjusted son and incredibly patient neighbors, he’s drinking and smoking himself to death on an island in Maine. Trudging along in this Lear-like existence — “Wintertime, Jesus Christ, he lived like a bear” — he’s taken by surprise by the ebullient Cadillac, a remarkable young woman from the Solomon Islands who comes to stay with him before studying medicine at Yale. (Thirty years prior, stationed in the Solomons during the Second World War, Jim befriended Cadillac’s father, with whom he has since lost all contact.) If this premise sounds familiar — striking young woman brings hope to forsaken old man — the style with which Greenway weaves her tale of memory and loss is bracing in its restraint.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Islanders', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Islanders', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenway, Alice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:21:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joanna Hershon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joanna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hershon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/73055755-5321-581c-af79-1a427e4aed4e', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73055755-5321-581c-af79-1a427e4aed4e'}, {'abstract': 'Greg Grandin tells the story of the slave uprising that inspired Melville’s “Benito Cereno.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/greg-grandins-empire-of-necessity.html', 'snippet': 'Greg Grandin tells the story of the slave uprising that inspired Melville’s “Benito Cereno.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Between the early 16th century and the middle of the 19th, more than 12 million human beings were shipped against their will from Africa to the New World and sold into slavery. An untold number died at different stages of the journey — overland in Africa, during the “middle passage” at sea or soon after arrival. Among those who perished, most died of disease, some by suicide and still others from wounds or execution following failed revolts. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUB2DELBANCO/12SUB2DELBANCO-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 408, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUB2DELBANCO/12SUB2DELBANCO-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 408}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUB2DELBANCO/12SUB2DELBANCO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 696, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUB2DELBANCO/12SUB2DELBANCO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1393, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUB2DELBANCO/12SUB2DELBANCO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUB2DELBANCO/12SUB2DELBANCO-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUB2DELBANCO/12SUB2DELBANCO-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Vengeful Fury', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Vengeful Fury', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Delbanco, Andrew', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grandin, Greg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:21:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Delbanco', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Delbanco', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/985fd2a8-a36f-595b-be80-58b6251e400f', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/985fd2a8-a36f-595b-be80-58b6251e400f'}, {'abstract': 'In Rachel Cantor’s novel, rival fast-food factions pursue philosophical truths.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/a-highly-unlikely-scenario-by-rachel-cantor.html', 'snippet': 'In Rachel Cantor’s novel, rival fast-food factions pursue philosophical truths.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a not-too-distant future, in a world much like our own, a young man sits in a white room, answering a white phone, listening. In his world, fast-food chains and political philosophies are one. Jack-o-Bites chefs and Whiggery Piggery employees brawl with kebab sticks in the public square, and neo-Baconians keep safe houses to protect themselves from the Cathars at the Strawberry Parfait. At the center of it all, Leonard, a customer-service rep for a Pythagorean pizza company, must save the world by time traveling and speaking to historical figures through the Neetsa Pizza support line.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-NETZER/0112-bks-NETZER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 426, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-NETZER/0112-bks-NETZER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 426}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-NETZER/0112-bks-NETZER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-NETZER/0112-bks-NETZER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1442, 'width': 2032, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-NETZER/0112-bks-NETZER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-NETZER/0112-bks-NETZER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-NETZER/0112-bks-NETZER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mystical Pizza', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mystical Pizza', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cantor, Rachel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:22:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lydia Netzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lydia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Netzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1b158d7-0220-5e1e-8355-6e0f612ff99f', 'word_count': 913, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1b158d7-0220-5e1e-8355-6e0f612ff99f'}, {'abstract': 'In a postmodern Russian epistolary novel, two lovers are separated both by distance and by time.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/the-light-and-the-dark-by-mikhail-shishkin.html', 'snippet': 'In a postmodern Russian epistolary novel, two lovers are separated both by distance and by time.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Contemporary Russia is short on pleasant surprises, but one is its formidable audience not only for the trashy novels prohibited by the Soviets but also for aggressively untraditional work. In the West, the phrase “Russian literature” typically conjures standard-bearers of \xadrealism — Tolstoy, Chekhov — but perhaps modern Russia is too real for realism. Imagine the madcap American fabulist Barry Yourgrau as an Oprah pick, and you have an inkling of the situation in Moscow, where bookstores are thick with nihilistic postmodern hallucinations by the likes of Vladimir Sorokin, perhaps best known for a sodomy scene between clones of Khrushchev and Stalin.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Dear Sasha, Dear Volodya', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dear Sasha, Dear Volodya', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fishman, Boris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shishkin, Mikhail', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bromfield, Andrew', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:22:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Boris Fishman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Boris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fishman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/add142f5-0e61-50ae-892d-8c9bb13ccbd0', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/add142f5-0e61-50ae-892d-8c9bb13ccbd0'}, {'abstract': 'Chasing love at a Soviet youth camp.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/books/review/the-scent-of-pine-by-lara-vapnyar.html', 'snippet': 'Chasing love at a Soviet youth camp.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her first published story for The New Yorker, “Love Lessons Mondays, 9 A.M.,” Lara Vapnyar wrote about a painfully shy 18-year-old in Moscow, teaching sex ed to 10th-grade girls who have more experience than she does. By the end the young narrator is dating the school \xadLothario and laughing uncontrollably when one of her students asks why it’s just as arousing to kiss a boy she doesn’t like as one she does. The narrator’s answer: “I don’t know!”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUBWALKER/12SUBWALKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1017, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUBWALKER/12SUBWALKER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1017}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUBWALKER/12SUBWALKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 604, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUBWALKER/12SUBWALKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1208, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUBWALKER/12SUBWALKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUBWALKER/12SUBWALKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12SUBWALKER/12SUBWALKER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Campfire Girl', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Campfire Girl', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vapnyar, Lara', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:23:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrea Walker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrea', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Walker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdfa310d-5dc4-537a-8f25-084722529f6c', 'word_count': 656, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdfa310d-5dc4-537a-8f25-084722529f6c'}, {'abstract': 'Questions and answers about Alex Rodriguez’s arbitration case.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/11/sports/baseball/q-and-a-alex-rodriguezs-suspension-appeal.html', 'snippet': 'Questions and answers about Alex Rodriguez’s arbitration case.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Questions and answers in connection with Alex Rodriguez’s arbitration case and the ruling, announced Saturday, that he would be suspended for the entire 2014 season. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/sports/10AROD9/10AROD9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/sports/10AROD9/10AROD9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 412}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/sports/10AROD9/10AROD9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name'