'nyt://article/d0ecc8c8-163b-5dff-997e-beca1fd5227a', 'word_count': 987, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0ecc8c8-163b-5dff-997e-beca1fd5227a'}, {'abstract': 'Jack Higgins, whose novel “The Death Trade” is No. 11 on the hardcover fiction list, does much of his writing at a restaurant near his home in the Channel Islands.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Jack Higgins, whose novel “The Death Trade” is No. 11 on the hardcover fiction list, does much of his writing at a restaurant near his home in the Channel Islands.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Order Now: For the first time in three weeks, there are new names on the hardcover lists — although “new” may not be the best word to describe the thriller writers W. E. B. Griffin (“Hazardous Duty,” No. 3) and Jack Higgins (“The Death Trade,” No. 11). Higgins is well known for his audacious story lines: His 1975 breakthrough, “The Eagle Has Landed,” concerned a Nazi plot to kidnap Churchill, while his 1989 best seller “A Season in Hell” imagined smugglers transporting heroin in the corpses of drowning victims. By those lights, “The Death Trade” — about a nuclear scientist forced to work for the Iranian government — seems almost workaday. Higgins’s eating habits are less colorful. “I’m notorious for liking very simple food,” he told Virgin Media in 2000. “You can take me to some of the world’s top restaurants and I’ll leave half of it on the plate. Food is not hugely important to me.” That’s not to say Higgins never dines out — a restaurant near his home in the Channel Islands doubles as his office, he explained in the same interview. “I go in at lunchtime, do two hours’ work, and then I eat,” he said. “And then in the evening I’ll go in and do three hours and then I’ll eat. I’ll write by hand and I get so absorbed it doesn’t bother me if the place is full or the music’s playing. It’s very common, particularly at night, for the waiters to come up and say, ‘Look, it’s 10 o’clock and you haven’t had anything to eat yet.’ ”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/12insidelist/12insidelist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:25:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d968ab47-6f29-59ff-acc1-a6e125e20229', 'word_count': 538, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d968ab47-6f29-59ff-acc1-a6e125e20229'}, {'abstract': 'The silent half of the illusionist duo has a lot to say about making a film about a hypothesis that Vermeer used optical equipment for his paintings.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/teller-on-penns-idea-tims-hypothesis-and-vermeers-painting/', 'snippet': 'The silent half of the illusionist duo has a lot to say about making a film about a hypothesis that Vermeer used optical equipment for his paintings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The “Tim” in “Tim’s Vermeer,” shortlisted for the Oscar for best feature documentary, is Tim Jenison, an inventor and friend of the illusionists Penn and Teller. The movie grew out of a conversation Mr. Jenison had with Penn Jillette in which he casually remarked that he thought he had figured out how the 17th-century Dutch painter Johannes Vermeer was able to produce canvases so lifelike that they still mystify painters and art historians alike.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/teller/teller-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 926, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/teller/teller-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 926}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/teller/teller-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 664, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/arts/teller/teller-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1425, 'width': 923, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':