'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T16:50:06+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/468cb5ae-1f78-5d55-960e-b46f334d3ac4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/468cb5ae-1f78-5d55-960e-b46f334d3ac4'}, {'abstract': 'A leader of Antarctic tourist voyages in Antarctica explores the rights and responsibilities of anyone plying those icy waters.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/10/when-tourists-rescue-scientists-in-antarctica/', 'snippet': 'A leader of Antarctic tourist voyages in Antarctica explores the rights and responsibilities of anyone plying those icy waters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After I covered efforts to evacuate the participants in the ice-trapped Antarctic “Spirit of Mawson” expedition, an interesting comment was posted by Ted Cheeseman, the co-owner of Cheesemans’ Ecology Safaris, which runs frequent Antarctic tourist trips. He noted that tourist ships have come to the aid of scientists on occasion, and expressed concern that I was implying that tourists have no right to visit the region. I invited him to send a “Your Dot” piece building on his comment. Here it is (I added some contextual links):', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/blogs/dotantarctic/dotantarctic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 809, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/10/blogs/dotantarctic/dotantarctic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 809}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/10/blogs/dotantarctic/dotantarctic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 760, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': '