': '2014-01-10T19:29:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Hauser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hauser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f1251f1c-a8ed-5d29-bdd5-bbd4fb3e2f9b', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f1251f1c-a8ed-5d29-bdd5-bbd4fb3e2f9b'}, {'abstract': '“Friday Night Tykes,” a reality show that has its premiere on Tuesday night at 9 on the Esquire Network, follows five San Antonio football teams of 8- and 9-year-olds.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/arts/television/pint-size-players-texas-size-fervor.html', 'snippet': '“Friday Night Tykes,” a reality show that has its premiere on Tuesday night at 9 on the Esquire Network, follows five San Antonio football teams of 8- and 9-year-olds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There’s no quitting in football. But there’s plenty of blood, sweat and tears when you’re a baby linebacker exhausted in 99-degree heat, and the coach orders you to bear-crawl across the field. “I don’t care how much pain you’re in,” bellows Charles Chavarria, a former Marine, at his team, the Junior Broncos. “You don’t quit.”', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/arts/12GUIDE6_SPAN/12GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/arts/12GUIDE6_SPAN/12GUIDE6-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/arts/12GUIDE6_SPAN/12GUIDE6-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo