ioning Americans planning to travel next month to Sochi, Russia, that terrorists have threatened to attack the Games.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The State Department issued a travel advisory on Friday cautioning Americans planning to attend the Winter Olympics next month in Sochi, Russia, that terrorists have threatened to attack the Games. The advisory also expressed concern about the quality of medical care in the region, noting that it could “differ substantially from Western standards.” ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/sports/CITY-SOCHI/CITY-SOCHI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/11/sports/CITY-SOCHI/CITY-SOCHI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/sports/CITY-SOCHI/CITY-SOCHI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/sports/CITY-SOCHI/CITY-SOCHI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Americans Traveling to Winter Games Cautioned', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Terrorism Among Dangers as U.S. Cautions Americans Traveling to Sochi', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2014)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sochi (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel Warnings', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T22:31:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Olympics', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe9d88b2-60c1-5dc2-a0b0-ed45bbb045a2', 'word_count': 561, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe9d88b2-60c1-5dc2-a0b0-ed45bbb045a2'}, {'abstract': 'Bartlett Sher’s “L’Elisir d’Amore,” at the Metropolitan Opera, features Andriana Chuchman in her Met debut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/11/arts/music/bartlett-shers-lelisir-damore-is-revived-at-the-met.html', 'snippet': 'Bartlett Sher’s “L’Elisir d’Amore,” at the Metropolitan Opera, features Andriana Chuchman in her Met debut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s unusual for a tragedy-obsessed art form, but for the last month or so, the fare at the Metropolitan Opera has been dominated by works that end happily and without too many bodies piled up. There have been runs of Strauss’s elegant “Der Rosenkavalier” and Mozart’s sublime “The Magic Flute,” as well as new productions of Verdi’s merry “Falstaff” and Johann Strauss Jr.’s frothy “Die Fledermaus.” (If you really needed death, “Tosca” and “Eugene Onegin” were there, too.)', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/arts/11ELISIR/11ELISIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 412, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/11/arts/11ELISIR/11ELISIR-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 412}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/arts/11ELISIR/11ELISIR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 704, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/arts/11ELISIR/11ELISIR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1408, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/arts/11ELISIR/11ELISIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/11/arts/11ELISIR/11ELISIR-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/arts/11ELISIR/11ELISIR-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Even Cheap Wine Can Work a Little Magic', 'kicker': 'Opera Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Even Cheap Wine Can Work a Little Magic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Woolfe, Zachary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sher, Bartlett', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chuchman, Andriana', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-10T22:33:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zachary Woolfe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zachary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Woolfe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/69ec11cc-4d27-55ea-93e2-a568045f60da', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69ec11cc-4d27-55ea-93e2-a568045f60da'}, {'abstract': 'An exhibition of work by Paulo Bruscky displays the radical art that flourished under a violent and repressive government in Brazil.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/nyregion/a-review-of-paulo-bruscky-art-is-our-last-hope-at-the-bronx-museum.html', 'snippet': 'An exhibition of work by Paulo Bruscky displays the radical art that flourished under a violent and repressive government in Brazil.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paulo Bruscky’s career as an artist began in a difficult time and place: Recife, Brazil, a few years after the 1964 coup that led to a dictatorship lasting nearly 20 years. Like other artists — and anyone who came under government suspicion — Mr. Bruscky was arrested (more than once), interrogated and tortured. But focusing only on the violent context of his work undermines what it set out to prove, which is that radical art can flourish even in repressive circumstances, and that it can be a form of communication, liberation and optimism, as the title “Paulo Bruscky: Art Is Our Last Hope” suggests. Installed at the Bronx Museum, the exhibition includes 140 works of sculpture, performance documentation, mail art and photography made between 1971 and 2011. ', 'print_section': 'WE', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12SUBARTSWE1_SPAN/12SUBARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/nyregion/12SUBARTSWE1_SPAN/12SUBARTSWE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': Non