e to bring more diverse types of people into your organization.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'This interview with  Michael P. Gregoire, chief executive of CA Technologies, the software makers based in Islandia, N.Y., was conducted and condensed by  Adam Bryant.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/business/12-CORNER/12-CORNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 916, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/business/12-CORNER/12-CORNER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 916}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/business/12-CORNER/12-CORNER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 671, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/business/12-CORNER/12-CORNER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1342, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/business/12-CORNER/12-CORNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/business/12-CORNER/12-CORNER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/business/12-CORNER/12-CORNER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michael Gregoire, on Diversity’s Importance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Diverse Team Is a Creative Team', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gregoire, Michael P', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hiring and Promotion', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'CA Technologies Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-11T15:55:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Bryant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bryant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/2c136bcc-5df4-5bc7-b6a9-44d7e57ff830', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2c136bcc-5df4-5bc7-b6a9-44d7e57ff830'}, {'abstract': '“You know, when you live with shelling all the time, you just let everything go,” said the Syrian activist Razan Ghazzawi. “You have to laugh.”', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/11/syrias-conflict-told-through-a-caustic-wit/', 'snippet': '“You know, when you live with shelling all the time, you just let everything go,” said the Syrian activist Razan Ghazzawi. “You have to laugh.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Raed Fares and Razan Ghazzawi are well-known Syrian activists who are wrapping up a speaking tour of North America. Ms. Ghazzawi, a prominent blogger who fled Damascus after facing government persecution and arrest, has traveled across Syria and the Middle East to raise awareness about the human cost of the Syria conflict. Mr. Fares runs an activist media center in the northern village of Kafranbel, which has gained a measure of international attention because of the witty banners and posters, many of them designed by Mr. Fares and his team, that protesters have displayed there. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syria’s Conflict Told Through a Caustic Wit', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-11T16:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d17a92e-54a5-5621-9ddd-9b2fa8667133', 'word_count': 1086, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d17a92e-54a5-5621-9ddd-9b2fa8667133'}, {'abstract': 'He was the one Israeli leader who could have reached a settlement with the Palestinians.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/11/opinion/ariel-sharon-the-warrior-who-could-have-made-peace.html', 'snippet': 'He was the one Israeli leader who could have reached a settlement with the Palestinians.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TEL AVIV — POLITICS, to Ariel Sharon, was like a Ferris wheel. But he didn’t make do with just staying on the wheel; he did all he could to climb to the top and stay there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/opinion/11sharon/11sharon-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 931, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/11/opinion/11sharon/11sharon-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 931}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/opinion/11sharon/11sharon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 661, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/opinion/11sharon/11sharon-su