': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/a54498e1-c4cd-51df-9d57-c651df83a3fb', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/a54498e1-c4cd-51df-9d57-c651df83a3fb'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey remains in hot water after his office was linked to lane closings causing gridlock in Fort Lee, N.J., as political punishment for the town’s mayor who did not endorse the governor. Sunday morning show guests dole out advice for the possible 2016 presidential candidate.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/11/sunday-breakfast-menu-jan-11/', 'snippet': 'Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey remains in hot water after his office was linked to lane closings causing gridlock in Fort Lee, N.J., as political punishment for the town’s mayor who did not endorse the governor. Sunday morning show guests dole ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was not Gov. Chris Christie’s week. The New Jersey Republican faced mounting criticism after emails and texts linked his office to lane closures on the George Washington Bridge from Fort Lee, N.J., in September. The closures were apparently retribution for the town’s mayor declining to endorse Mr. Christie for re-election. In a two-hour news conference on Thursday, Mr. Christie apologized, saying he was “blindsided” by the news. He fired Bridget Anne Kelly, his deputy chief of staff who wrote in one email, “time for some traffic problems in Fort Lee.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sunday Breakfast Menu, Jan. 11', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-11T19:16:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elena Schneider', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schneider', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organizati