 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-12T00:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonny Vandevelde', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vandevelde', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14f50f7e-c793-5c69-8b6b-4b0e1d53333b', 'word_count': 15, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14f50f7e-c793-5c69-8b6b-4b0e1d53333b'}, {'abstract': 'Roger Shuler’s accusatory posts have earned him many enemies. But even many of them are shocked by what has happened to him.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/us/bloggers-incarceration-raises-first-amendment-questions.html', 'snippet': 'Roger Shuler’s accusatory posts have earned him many enemies. But even many of them are shocked by what has happened to him.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BIRMINGHAM, Ala. — For over six years, Roger Shuler has hounded figures of the state legal and political establishment on his blog, Legal Schnauzer, a hothouse of furious but often fuzzily sourced allegations of deep corruption and wide-ranging conspiracy. Some of these allegations he has tested in court, having sued his neighbor, his neighbor’s lawyer, his former employer, the Police Department, the Sheriff’s Department, the Alabama State Bar and two county circuit judges, among others. Mostly, he has lost. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/us/12blogger/12blogger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/us/12blogger/12blogger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'u