': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb599acc-e205-5b50-ae53-13b7b50f1026', 'word_count': 819, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb599acc-e205-5b50-ae53-13b7b50f1026'}, {'abstract': 'An outdoor advertising company has been using short, catchy phrases with pictures of food on billboards to sell the billboards themselves.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/billboards-by-stuart-miller/', 'snippet': 'An outdoor advertising company has been using short, catchy phrases with pictures of food on billboards to sell the billboards themselves.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eric Justusson, a software engineer, was walking to work from Brooklyn across the Manhattan Bridge when he first saw the enigmatic billboard, the words “Easy As” in gigantic letters followed by a 12-foot-high picture of a slice of pie. For BJ Gruber, an actor who lives in Astoria, Queens, it was the words “Cool As A,” followed by a photo of a cucumber slice that he spotted while driving on the Brooklyn Queens Expressway. Soledad Tejada, a high school student from Brooklyn, noticed one with “The Big” and a tremendous hunk of cheese in her home borough. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/nyregion/10cityroom-1/10cityroom-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/11/nyregion/10cityroom-1/10cityroom-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/11/nyregion/10cityroom-1/10cityroom-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'