aterial': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ecbb0943-1bfb-54ab-9203-cac5e25f4a2d', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ecbb0943-1bfb-54ab-9203-cac5e25f4a2d'}, {'abstract': 'Wu Wei, a former Chinese official who in the 1980s was heavily involved in developing plans for measured political reform, says recently announced economic changes will stall without substantial changes to the political system.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/q-and-a-wu-wei-reflects-on-reform-now-and-in-the-1980s/', 'snippet': 'Wu Wei, a former Chinese official who in the 1980s was heavily involved in developing plans for measured political reform, says recently announced economic changes will stall without substantial changes to the political system.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Wu Wei is a former Chinese official who in the 1980s was heavily involved in developing the Communist Party chief Zhao Ziyang’s plans for measured political reform. Mr. Wu also served as a secretary to Bao Tong, the senior aide to Mr. Zhao. Those reforms collapsed in 1989, when the party patriarch, Deng Xiaoping, turned against Mr. Zhao and ordered troops to suppress student protests based in Tiananmen Square in Beijing. Mr. Wu was detained and investigated after the June 4, 1989, crackdown, and later went into publishing and business. He has remained a close observer of Chinese politics, and last year his book “On Stage and Backstage: China’s Political Reform in the 1980s” was published in Hong Kong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/13/world/13sino-wu/13sino-wu-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 368, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/13/world/13sino-wu/13sino-wu-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 368}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images