house in the town of Garrison, N.Y., in the lower Hudson Valley. We love the place for its scenic beauty, its peace and quiet, and its old-fashioned sense of community. For us, it’s a refuge from the pace of city life, a place with an easygoing mix of lifestyles and a widely shared ethos about preserving what makes it special. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Weisberg/0112-bks-Weisberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 989, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Weisberg/0112-bks-Weisberg-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 989}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Weisberg/0112-bks-Weisberg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 622, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Weisberg/0112-bks-Weisberg-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1243, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Weisberg/0112-bks-Weisberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Weisberg/0112-bks-Weisberg-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Weisberg/0112-bks-Weisberg-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Networker', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Networker', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Weisberg, Jacob', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Loudest Voice in the Room (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherman, Gabriel', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AILES, ROGER E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fox News Channel', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-12T23:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jacob Weisberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jacob', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weisberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1c5db5f-83a9-53b5-a85c-e8a49272ddcc', 'word_count': 1638, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1c5db5f-83a9-53b5-a85c-e8a49272ddcc'}, {'abstract': 'Lynn Lempel offers us an inclusive puzzle.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/12/everybody/', 'snippet': 'Lynn Lempel offers us an inclusive puzzle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONDAY’S PUZZLE — One of the things I like most about solving crossword puzzles is that it’s an ALL-inclusive pastime. Anyone can do it, if they are so inclined. And today, Lynn Lempel starts our solving week by inviting ONE AND ALL to solve her puzzle.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Everybody', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-12T23:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/51b2ea7a-60a1-588d-9ae5-66f234dd306b', 'word_count': 360, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51b2ea7a-60a1-588d-9ae5-66f234dd306b'}, {'abstract': 'The 92nd Street Y’s Lyrics & Lyricists series celebrates Arthur Freed, the producer of classic MGM musicals.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/13/arts/music/lyrics-lyricists-at-92nd-street-y-celebrates-arthur-freed.html', 'snippet': 'The 92nd Street Y’s Lyrics & Lyricists series celebrates Arthur Freed, the producer of classic MGM musicals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Why is it that with few exceptions visionary film producers go largely unrecognized by the public while directors and movie stars become legends? That question informed “Going Hollywood: MGM Musicals,” the ebullient season-opening program of the 92nd Street Y’s Lyrics & Lyricists series on Saturday night, hosted by the director and choreographer Kathleen Marshall. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With a Happy Refrain: MGM’s Magic Era', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With a Happy Refrain: MGM’s Magic Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freed, Arthur', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': '92nd Street Y', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-12T23:01:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b54e5dae-3e3f-5846-9ce3-59c2a1186ff3', 'word_count': 353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b54e5dae-3e3f-5846-9ce3-59c2a1186ff3'}, {'abstract': 'Ute Lemper is singing at 54 Below, delivering the European repertoire to American cabaret audiences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/13/arts/music/ute-lemper-performs-at-54-below.html', 'snippet': 'Ute Lemper is singing at 54 Below, delivering the European repertoire to American cabaret audiences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The narrowed cat eyes that suddenly widen into a maniacal blue-eyed stare, the pleated platinum hair of a ’40s femme fatale, the deployment of a voice that can rise to a furious yowl by an imperious diva sheathed in black satin: No expressionistic detail was overlooked in Ute Lemper’s new show, a kind of best-of concert, which began a run at 54 Below on Wednesday evening. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/13/arts/SUBLEMPER/SUBLEMPER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/13/arts/SUBLEMPER/SUBLEMPER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/13/arts/SUBLEMPER/SUBLEMPER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'widt