bsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Esha Chabbra', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Esha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chabbra', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe85e95e-9361-53bb-8afc-ab574ee20c47', 'word_count': 945, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe85e95e-9361-53bb-8afc-ab574ee20c47'}, {'abstract': 'A couple of riders on the London metro drew some sidelong glances as they took part in the “No Trousers Tube Ride” on Sunday, part of an international “No Pants Subway Ride” event.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/13/from-todays-paper-breeches-of-etiquette/', 'snippet': 'A couple of riders on the London metro drew some sidelong glances as they took part in the “No Trousers Tube Ride” on Sunday, part of an international “No Pants Subway Ride” event.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A couple of riders on the London metro drew some sidelong glances as they took part in the “No Trousers Tube Ride” on Sunday, part of an international “No Pants Subway Ride” event. The annual happening was started in New York by a comedy group in 2002, and has since become a rite of winter in which pants-less people casually wait at stations along a subway line and hop onboard. Groups in dozens of cities across the world are said to be participating in the event.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Today’s Paper: Breeches of Etiquette', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'London (England)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-13T07:25:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d7bca4c-fa5c-5a52-93fb-3674ad8276fc', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d7bca4c-fa5c-5a52-93fb-3674ad8276fc'}, {'abstract': 'A daughter of the Communist Party elite has offered public penance for her part in one of the most notorious killings of the Cultural Revolution.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/13/bowed-and-remorseful-former-red-guard-recalls-teachers-death/', 'snippet': 'A daughter of the Communist Party elite has offered public penance for her part in one of the most notorious killings of the Cultural Revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 3:54 a.m. ET | Nearly half a century after Bian Zhongyun was beaten, kicked, tormented and left to die, bloody and alone, at the Beijing girls’ school where she was deputy principal, a daughter of the Communist Party elite has offered public penance — of a kind that instantly brought controversy — for her part in one of the most notorious killings of the Cultural Revolution.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bowed and Remorseful, Former Red Guard Recalls Teacher’s Death', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Remorse Over Death Of Teacher In Mao Era', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Apologies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cultural Revolution', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bian Zhongyun', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mao Zedong', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Song Binbin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Song Renqiong', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beijing Normal University', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-13T07:29:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Buckley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buckley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f2334026-b9e6-5c0e-bfd5-a3dbde1cb207', 'word_count': 1199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f2334026-b9e6-5c0e-bfd5-a3dbde1cb207'}, {'abstract': 'The Australian Open has its own official weather forecaster, who will be checking the heat index this week as the temperatures climb to over 100 degrees Fahrenheit.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/14/sports/tennis/australian-open-weather.html', 'snippet': 'The Australian Open has its own official weather forecaster, who will be checking the heat index this week as the temperatures climb to over 100 degrees Fahrenheit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — On the first two days of the Australian Open, players gulped water and applied ice packs to foreheads while on changeovers. Fans lathered on sunscreen, wore hats, sought shade. A heat wave has arrived, with temperatures expected to climb well over 100 degrees Fahrenheit.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/14/sports/14iht-weather-cnd01/14iht-weather-cnd01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/14/sports/14iht-weather-cnd01/14iht-weather-cnd01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 60