'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution (Biology)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-13T21:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/a4e02168-3cee-5724-a1c0-bf60ac90d8b4', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/a4e02168-3cee-5724-a1c0-bf60ac90d8b4'}, {'abstract': 'Based on fossils of Tiktaalik roseae, a transitional species that lived 375 million years ago, scientists have concluded that the modification of fins into four limbs began before vertebrates left the water.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/14/science/an-ancient-fishs-four-wheel-drive.html', 'snippet': 'Based on fossils of Tiktaalik roseae, a transitional species that lived 375 million years ago, scientists have concluded that the modification of fins into four limbs began before vertebrates left the water.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Based on fossils found a decade ago, scientists had assumed that the earliest known species of fish to make the momentous transition to four-legged animals, capable of living and moving about on dry land, had developed a kind of front-wheel drive. They used enhanced forward fins to crawl out of shallow waters, and only later adapted their rear fins into limbs.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Ancient Fish’s Four-Wheel Drive', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Ancient Fish’s Four-Wheel Drive', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilford, John Noble', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fossils', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-13T21:30:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Noble Wilford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': 'Noble', 'lastname': 'Wilford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c78d3938-5896-5242-ae42-320d0b2181a8', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c78d3938-5896-5242-ae42-320d0b2181a8'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Chris Christie faces new questions about political retribution and Hurricane Sandy aid as Trenton lawmakers outline plans to create special investigative legislative committees.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/13/as-christie-faces-new-questions-state-lawmakers-expand-investigation/', 'snippet': 'Gov. Chris Christie faces new questions about political retribution and Hurricane Sandy aid as Trenton lawmakers outline plans to create special investigative legislative committees.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Gov. Chris Christie prepared to deliver his annual State of the State address on Tuesday, he faced new questions just days after he dismissed two aides, including his longtime political adviser Bill Stepien, in a political payback scandal that involved manufacturing a huge traffic jam last September at the George Washington Bridge. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/14/nyregion/ledechristie/ledechristie-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/14/nyregion/ledechristie/ledechristie-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image