e', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Eder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/75acfb99-557d-53c7-8e7d-8e008e92b884', 'word_count': 901, 'uri': 'nyt://article/75acfb99-557d-53c7-8e7d-8e008e92b884'}, {'abstract': 'F.B.I. investigators believe that officials with the tax-collecting agency did not break the law by giving special attention to conservative groups.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/14/us/charges-seen-as-unlikely-in-scrutiny-by-the-irs.html', 'snippet': 'F.B.I. investigators believe that officials with the tax-collecting agency did not break the law by giving special attention to conservative groups.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — F.B.I. investigators do not believe Internal Revenue Service officials committed crimes in the unusually heavy scrutiny of conservative groups that applied for tax-exempt status, a law enforcement official said Monday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Charges Seen as Unlikely in Scrutiny by the I.R.S.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Charges Seen as Unlikely in Scrutiny by the I.R.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmidt, Michael S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service Political Profiling', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-14T03:12:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael S. Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd7c8f4c-dcfd-59f3-9055-8a14e1777eff', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd7c8f4c-dcfd-59f3-9055-8a14e1777eff'}, {'abstract': 'David Satter, an American journalist and the author of books on Russia and the Soviet Union, said he had been barred from the country by the Russian security services.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/14/world/europe/russia-us-journalist-is-barred.html', 'snippet': 'David Satter, an American journalist and the author of books on Russia and the Soviet Union, said he had been barred from the country by the Russian security services.', 'lead_paragraph': ' David Satter, an American journalist and the author of three books on Russia and the Soviet Union, said Monday that he had been barred from the country by the Russian security services. Mr. Satter had been working in Moscow as an adviser and a contributor to Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty, which is financed by the American government. In a telephone interview from London, Mr. Satter said the Russian Foreign Ministry told him last month that he had been approved to receive a new visa, but that when he went to pick it up at the Russian Embassy in Ukraine he was told that he had been barred from Russia. An embassy official said the “competent organs” had declared his presence in Russia to be “undesirable,” Mr. Satter said. The American Embassy in Moscow has lodged a formal protest. Kevin Klose, the president of Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty, called the visa denial a “fundamental violation of free speech and journalistic liberty.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: American Journalist Is Barred', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: U.S. Journalist Barred', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Satter, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Visas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Radio Free Europe-Radio Liberty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-14T03:20:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By David M. Herszenhorn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/9df21288-8509-5950-99da-51e383f58603', 'word_count': 160, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9df21288-8509-5950-99da-51e383f58603'}, {'abstract': 'The popularity of free products at sporting events has led to an arms race to develop the most entertaining and marketable T-shirt cannon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/14/sports/crowd-pleasing-blasts-with-civil-war-roots.html', 'snippet': 'The popularity of free products at sporting events has led to an arms race to develop the most entertaining and marketable T-shirt cannon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHILADELPHIA — The Philadelphia 76ers like to save their most impressive entertainment innovation for the fourth quarter. So with two minutes left in a recent game, and only after free T-shirts had wafted from the rafters in parachutes and acrobats had dunked off trampolines, did they finally bring out the big gun.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/14/sports/CITY-CANNON-1/CITY-CANNON-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height':