: 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8419164-ffda-5147-8473-8b0666f2352f', 'word_count': 1958, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8419164-ffda-5147-8473-8b0666f2352f'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Chris Christie, whose aides and appointees apparently engineered a traffic nightmare as political payback, sought to change the subject in his State of the State speech.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/nyregion/in-annual-address-christie-turns-focus-from-scandal-to-policy.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Chris Christie, whose aides and appointees apparently engineered a traffic nightmare as political payback, sought to change the subject in his State of the State speech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Chris Christie of New Jersey, scrambling to regain his political footing, delivered a high-stakes State of the State address on Tuesday that mixed arms-length expressions of contrition, a defiant assertion that his troubles are no excuse for inaction and a jarringly timed demand for bipartisanship. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/nyregion/15christie3/15christie3-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/15/nyregion/15christie3/15christie3-articleLarge-v4.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/nyregion/15christie3/15christie3-jumbo-v4.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'ty