2014/01/15/pageoneplus/corrections-january-15-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Corrections appearing in print on January 14, 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRONT PAGE', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Corrections: January 15, 2014', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Corrections', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-15T03:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/95e7d8e6-33bd-5419-b010-966afd7b47a7', 'word_count': 694, 'uri': 'nyt://article/95e7d8e6-33bd-5419-b010-966afd7b47a7'}, {'abstract': '“She Is King” is a portrait of the tennis star Billie Jean King as a hero in an era of entrenched sexism and other inequalities in sports and life.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/theater/she-is-king-focuses-on-turning-points-for-billie-jean-king.html', 'snippet': '“She Is King” is a portrait of the tennis star Billie Jean King as a hero in an era of entrenched sexism and other inequalities in sports and life.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Are you an angry woman? That’s the first question James Day asks a smiling, ebullient Billie Jean King in “She Is King,” part of the Incubator Arts Project’s Other Forces festival. Sure, she says, she’s angry about certain things, but then, who isn’t?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/arts/she/she-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/15/arts/she/she-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ