 will be extended through March so patients can continue treatment while they find other coverage, the Obama administration said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/us/program-to-cover-major-illnesses-is-extended.html', 'snippet': 'The Pre-Existing Condition Insurance Plan will be extended through March so patients can continue treatment while they find other coverage, the Obama administration said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A special health insurance program for people with cancer, heart disease and other serious illnesses will be extended for two additional months, through March, so patients can continue treatment while they search for other coverage, the Obama administration said Tuesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Program to Cover Major Illnesses Is Extended', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Program to Cover Major Illnesses Is Extended', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pear, Robert', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN CANCER SOCIETY', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-15T05:55:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert Pear', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pear', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60268344-bc8d-529e-8dbc-2daa34465c29', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60268344-bc8d-529e-8dbc-2daa34465c29'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/arts/television/whats-on-wednesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (FXX) HOW TO TRAIN YOUR DRAGON (2010) In this feature from DreamWorks Animation, a teenage Viking named Hiccup (voiced by Jay Baruchel) befriends Toothless, an injured dragon, on a windswept island; pleases his demanding father (Gerard Butler); and saves the world in the process. The airborne sequences, like the one above, are particularly compelling, A. O. Scott wrote in The New York Times. “When Hiccup first climbs on Toothless’s back and urges the dragon to take wing, the hearts of the audience soar with a primitive and durable delight,” he said. “The techniques that enabled this feeling may be dauntingly complicated, but the feeling could not be simpler.” At the beginning and the end, “\xa0‘How to Train Your Dragon’ is noisy and action-packed in the usual way,” Mr. Scott added, with “eye-straining, ear-popping large-scale effects.” But they are worth sitting through, he wrote, “for the tenderness, beauty and exhilaration that are the movie’s great strengths.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Wednesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-15T06:00:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e4c4ab7-fce2-5e03-be69-a617267a7343', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e4c4ab7-fce2-5e03-be69-a617267a7343'}, {'abstract': 'In many states under single-party control, lawmakers are steering away from the divisive battles on abortion, gun control, collective bargaining and large tax cuts that have marked recent sessions in state capitols.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/us/politics/after-flurry-of-changes-some-states-ease-up.html', 'snippet': 'In many states under single-party control, lawmakers are steering away from the divisive battles on abortion, gun control, collective bargaining and large tax cuts that have marked recent sessions in state capitols.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — One year ago, Gov. John W. Hickenlooper, a Colorado Democrat, stood before a state legislature controlled by his party and laid out an aggressive agenda that included a sweeping expansion of gun-control laws after the mass shooting at an Aurora movie house. “Why not have universal background checks for all gun sales?” he asked.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/us/jp-ONEPARTY-1/jp-ONEPARTY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 428, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/15/us/jp-ONEPARTY-1/jp-ONEPARTY-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 428}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/us/jp-ONEPARTY-1/jp-ONEPARTY-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/us/jp-ONEPARTY-1/jp-ONEPARTY-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1461, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/us/jp-ONEPARTY-1/jp-ONEPARTY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/15/us/jp-ONEPARTY-1/jp-ONEPARTY-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/us/jp-ONEPARTY-1/jp-ONEPARTY-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Flurry of Changes, Some States Ease Up', 'kicker': 'One-Party Rule', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Flurry of Changes, Some States Ease Up', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Series', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ohio', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kasich, John R', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hickenlooper, John W', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-15T06:07:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Nagourney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nagourney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b27d9d6b-babf-51c0-a3a8-68e2bcb6b32f', 'word_count': 1800, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b27d9d6b-babf-51c0-a3a8-68e2bcb6b32f'}, {'abstract': 'Expected to have half a dozen runways and to handle more passengers than Heathrow, Beijing’s planned new international airport is the center of a naming debate that speaks of the country’s image, commerce and history.', 'web_url': 'https://sinosphere.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/tussling-over-a-name-for-beijings-new-airport/', 'snippet': 'Expected to have half a dozen runways and to handle more passengers than Heathrow, Beijing’s planned new international airport is the center of a naming debate that speaks of the country’s image, commerce and history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What’s in an airport name? In Beijing, a debate has begun over the name of a planned new international airport, the capital’s second. The front-runners are Daxing, Jingji, Lixian, Yongding, Yufa and Zhuque, with the struggle between the two favorites, Daxing and Lixian, seeming to rest on this: What image does China want to present to the world? Money or virtue?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tussling Over a Name for Beijing’s New Airport', 'kicker': 'Sinosphere', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AIRPORTS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beijing Capital International Airport', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Heathrow Airport (London)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlanta (Ga)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tiananmen Square (Beijing)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-15T06:14:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Didi Kirsten Tatlow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Didi', 'middlename': 'Kirsten', 'last