rl': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/us/washington-more-money-sought-for-schools-and-wages.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Jay Inslee asked lawmakers to increase spending on K-12 education and raise the state minimum wage as the Legislature began work on the first state budget in six years without a major financial shortfall.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gov. Jay Inslee asked lawmakers to increase spending on K-12 education and raise the state minimum wage as the Legislature began work on the first state budget in six years without a major financial shortfall. Mr. Inslee, a Democrat, said in his State of the State speech on Tuesday that improving finances made education spending a priority. The Washington Supreme Court has ruled that education spending does not meet constitutional requirements. Washington already has the nation’s highest statewide minimum wage, $9.32, and Mr. Inslee said there was “ample evidence” that an increase in the $1.50 to $2.50 range “does not kill jobs.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Washington: More Money Sought for Schools and Wages', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Northwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Washington: More Money Sought For Schools and Wages', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Inslee, Jay', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Kirk', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (State)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-15T06:47:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline':