: '2014-01-15T19:08:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elanie Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ceaeb8e-70f0-59d9-bb4d-ecafb4d4896f', 'word_count': 149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ceaeb8e-70f0-59d9-bb4d-ecafb4d4896f'}, {'abstract': 'If you’re going to Park City, the mumblecore filmmaker and Sundance veteran Mark Duplass has tips for beating the lines, scoring free food, getting away and more.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/15/the-insider-mark-duplass-on-navigating-the-sundance-film-festival/', 'snippet': 'If you’re going to Park City, the mumblecore filmmaker and Sundance veteran Mark Duplass has tips for beating the lines, scoring free food, getting away and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those looking for a personal guide to the Sundance Film Festival, which begins tomorrow in Park City, Utah and runs through Jan. 26, could do a lot worse than Mark Duplass. The veteran actor and filmmaker, who is known for his roles in such films as “Your Sister’s Sister” and “Safety Not Guaranteed” and is a founding father of the mumblecore genre with his brother, Jay, has been to the festival every year since 2003. This year, he returns to Park City with a pair of films he produced: the comedic drama “The Skeleton Twins,” starring Bill Hader and Kristen Wiig, and “The One I Love,” the debut film by the writer Charlie McDowell, in which Duplass also stars alongside the “Mad Men” actress Elisabeth Moss. Here, Duplass shares his tips for navigating the festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/15/t-magazine/15duplass-oloizia/15duplass-oloizia-articleLarge.jpg', 