 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Opinion', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By PETER MIDDLETON and JAMES SPINNEY', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MIDDLETON', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'SPINNEY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ed3c2c73-c338-5a90-9dc1-b6ff9d98e8c6', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ed3c2c73-c338-5a90-9dc1-b6ff9d98e8c6'}, {'abstract': 'Sarah Robles, an American Olympian who received a two-year suspension after testing positive for a banned substance, said the test result was because of a hormonal precursor supplement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/16/sports/us-olympian-explains-positive-test.html', 'snippet': 'Sarah Robles, an American Olympian who received a two-year suspension after testing positive for a banned substance, said the test result was because of a hormonal precursor supplement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sarah Robles, an American Olympian who received a two-year suspension after testing positive for a banned substance, said the test result was because of a hormonal precursor supplement sometimes used in the course of medical treatment for polycystic ovary syndrome. “I accept the conditions of my suspension,” Robles said. \xa0I made a choice to take care of my health as advised by my doctor.” She said she hoped to compete for a weight-lifting spot at the 2016 Summer Games in Rio de Janeiro. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Olympian Explains Positive Test', 'kicker': 'Sports Briefing | Weight Lifting', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Olympian Explains Positive Test', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pilon, Mary', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weight Lifting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Robles, Sarah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2016)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T04:30:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mary Pilon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pilon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/aacab5bb-33dd-5a25-b677-101c4e6acd4c', 'word_count': 83, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aacab5bb-33dd-5a25-b677-101c4e6acd4c'}, {'abstract': 'New England’s consistently effective running game is a marvel, but Denver is the pick in the A.F.C. title game.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/16/sports/football/conference-championship-probabilities-and-how-the-patriots-run.html', 'snippet': 'New England’s consistently effective running game is a marvel, but Denver is the pick in the A.F.C. title game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Patriots’ running game is finally getting its due. After a 234-yard, 6-touchdown explosion to defeat the Colts in the divisional round, the New England rushing attack is suddenly in the spotlight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/16/sports/football/16nflodds-1/16nflodds-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 113, 'width': 375, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/16/sports/football/16nflodds-1/16nflodds-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 113, 'width': 375, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Conference Championship Probabilities, and How the Patriots Run', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New England Patriots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Denver Broncos', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T04:56:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Burke', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Burke', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'An Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf7b84c9-4750-5b90-94b8-17f8a7fe4ab5', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf7b84c9-4750-5b90-94b8-17f8a7fe4ab5'}, {'abstract': '“Michael Inchbald: A Legacy of Design” is a sale of the talented designer, whose work on the Queen’s Room on the Queen Elizabeth 2 is said to have inspired the sets of “2001: A Space Odyssey.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/16/t-magazine/christies-auction-michael-inchbald.html', 'snippet': '“Michael Inchbald: A Legacy of Design” is a sale of the talented designer, whose work on the Queen’s Room on the Queen Elizabeth 2 is said to have inspired the sets of “2001: A Space Odyssey.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every now and then an auction comes along which throws light upon hidden secrets of the design world. On Jan. 22, Christie’s London will offer Michael Inchbald: A Legacy of Design, an auction of the varied estate of the talented (but in America, underappreciated) British designer who died last year at the age of 92. His career spanned nearly five decades and included many public and private commissions in the U.K. Among the most notable were the interiors of the Berkeley and Savoy Hotels and projects for the Duke of St. Albans and the filmmaker John Schlesinger (“Darling,” “Marathon Man”). Inchbald executed his interiors in diverse styles, ranging from ’60s groovy to richly baronial. Throughout all of his work, however, there are unexpected juxtapositions of objects and a deep understanding of the role of lighting to create atmosphere.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Christie’s Auction Honors the Passing of a British Design Giant', 'kicker': 'By Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T05:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TDesign', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Netto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Netto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f87e102-b5ac-5027-9cc1-5298d49186dd', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f87e102-b5ac-5027-9cc1-5298d49186dd'}, {'abstract': 'The traditional demi-parure jewelry set made a chic comeback at the resort collections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/16/t-magazine/matching-jewelry-is-anything-but-stuffy.html', 'snippet': 'The traditional demi-parure jewelry set made a chic comeback at the resort collections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Matching your bracelet to your necklace may sound old-fashioned. But the demi-parure — the classic coordinating jewelry set, favored by style icons like Diana Vreeland and Grace Kelly in their heyday — is making a comeback. Not long after Kate Middleton was spotted wearing the style, a host of modern collar-cuff pairings are popping up in designers’ resort collections. Saint Laurent and St. John offer subdued takes on the trend in gold and clear crystals, while Balenciaga’s stone-embroidered padded tubes feel at once organic and futuristic. Other versions look downright rock ‘n’ roll, such as Ca & Lou’s geometric shapes in rose-gold and Givenchy’s black and white stones dangling from oxidized metal chains. Whether worn with a cocktail dress or thrown atop a T-shirt for a touch of sparkle, each pair is an instant outfit-maker.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Matching Jewelry Is Anything but Stuffy', 'kicker': 'Market Report', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T05:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TCulture', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fff1a35b-bade-52bd-b210-f7f90fbbeecd', 'word_count': 135, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fff1a35b-bade-52bd-b210-f7f90fbbeecd'}, {'abstract': 'Outside expertise brought to bear on policy makers can be a formidable ally, and that has been the case in the promulgation of the Volcker Rule, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/16/occupy-the-s-e-c-has-its-day/', 'snippet': 'Outside expertise brought to bear on policy makers can be a formidable ally, and that has been the case in the promulgation of the Volcker Rule, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Simon Johnson, former chief economist of the International Monetary Fund, is the Ronald A. Kurtz professor of entrepreneurship at the M.I.T. Sloan School of Management and co-author of “White House Burning: The Founding Fathers, Our National Debt, and Why It Matters to You.”',