rticle', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2ddc89f-8a23-530f-b8c2-21805e6591b6', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2ddc89f-8a23-530f-b8c2-21805e6591b6'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs of Owen Pallett, who composed the score for The Times Magazine’s 2010 Hollywood feature, on the film set.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/16/owen-pallett-in-the-moment/', 'snippet': 'Photographs of Owen Pallett, who composed the score for The Times Magazine’s 2010 Hollywood feature, on the film set.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Owen Pallett was working on the musical score to accompany The Times Magazine’s Hollywood feature “Fourteen Actors Acting” in 2010, I got to see him compose right on the spot. He was on set as Solve Sundsbo filmed Matt Damon, and in the interim until Michael Douglas’s shoot later that day, Pallett watched some of the other films that had already been shot and quickly began making notes. He played some short clips of music from the Internet to give us an idea of the kinds of melodies he was thinking of. I have this memory of Pallett pacing about in the dark, cavernous studio, his pad in hand as he came up with ideas for each of the shorts. That fast-working style was partly what inspired Miki Meek, former online editor of the magazine, to suggest we work with him (indeed, he wrote all 14 scores, which were subsequently recorded by the Czech FILMharmonic Orchestra, in less than two weeks).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/16/magazine/sundsbo03/sundsbo03-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 