onversations with the creative individuals shaping contemporary culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Doug Aitken’s ongoing series of conversations with the creative individuals shaping contemporary culture.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'THE SOURCE', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T22:34:35+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'T Magazine', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/0ca462e8-a292-5fcf-9e55-3c5da4e174f7', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/0ca462e8-a292-5fcf-9e55-3c5da4e174f7'}, {'abstract': 'The Morgan Library & Museum’s first show of Spanish drawings covers four centuries with just 25 objects.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/arts/design/the-morgan-whips-through-four-centuries-of-spanish-drawing.html', 'snippet': 'The Morgan Library & Museum’s first show of Spanish drawings covers four centuries with just 25 objects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although it’s well-stocked with French, Dutch and Italian drawings, the Morgan Library & Museum does not have many from Spain, in part because some of the great Spanish painters, Velázquez included, worked out their ideas directly on canvas. So while the Morgan mounted a 100-piece exhibition last fall on the topic of Venetian drawings from the 18th century, its first-ever show of Spanish drawings whips through four centuries in just 25 objects.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '38', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Few Pearls of the Grisly and the Mundane', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Few Pearls of the Grisly and the Mundane', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenberg, Karen', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Library & Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goya, Francisco de', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T22:35:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2fa5cac-969c-55e9-89b2-bdbdc458d6d0', 'word_count': 749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2fa5cac-969c-55e9-89b2-bdbdc458d6d0'}, {'abstract': 'If store cashiers would sing, “Have a Good Day!,” at Here Arts Center, has the lyrics.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/arts/music/lithuanian-cashiers-in-have-a-good-day.html', 'snippet': 'If store cashiers would sing, “Have a Good Day!,” at Here Arts Center, has the lyrics.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first thing you noticed was the smell. Lingering fumes attested to a black box theater at the Here Arts Center in the South Village freshly repainted white for the American premiere of “Have a Good Day!” The hourlong musical-theater work, presented on Wednesday evening as part of the Prototype: Opera/Theater/Now festival, was mounted by the Lithuanian company Operomanija, making its United States debut.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17PROTOTYPE_SPAN/PROTOTYPE-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17PROTOTYPE