ish detained on Dec. 29 had “confessed” to being members of the Muslim Brotherhood, but their network denied the claim.', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/16/network-denies-that-journalists-detained-in-egypt-confessed-to-terror-charges/', 'snippet': 'Egypt said journalists from Al Jazeera English detained on Dec. 29 had “confessed” to being members of the Muslim Brotherhood, but their network denied the claim.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Egyptian officials said on Thursday that journalists from the Al Jazeera English network detained on Dec. 29 had confessed to being members of the Muslim Brotherhood, which has been declared a terrorist organization by the military-backed government that took power after the ouster in July of President Mohamed Morsi, a member of the group.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Al Jazeera Denies That Journalists Detained in Egypt Confessed to Terror Charges', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AL JAZEERA', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T23:19:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liam Stack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/50d514e0-ca52-549d-9e46-6da7b8ab0e70', 'word_count': 715, 'uri': 'nyt://article/50d514e0-ca52-549d-9e46-6da7b8ab0e70'}, {'abstract': 'Despite remarkable pieces by Dana Michel and Juliana F. May, the American Realness festival mostly descends into banality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/arts/dance/at-american-realness-festival-a-range-of-fringe.html', 'snippet': 'Despite remarkable pieces by Dana Michel and Juliana F. May, the American Realness festival mostly descends into banality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Seventy-five years ago, the art critic Clement Greenberg wrote the lastingly celebrated essay “Avant-Garde and Kitsch,” defining them as two opposite poles in art. Although they have always overlapped, it’s depressing how far kitsch (mass-produced or widely popular art) now masquerades as avant-garde (challenging experimentalism in art). As proof, just check out the current American Realness festival. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17AMERICAN1_SPAN/AMERICAN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 364, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17AMERICAN1_SPAN/AMERICAN1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 364}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17AMERICAN1_SPAN/AMERICAN1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 621, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17AMERICAN1_SPAN/AMERICAN1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1241, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17AMERICAN1_SPAN/17AMERICAN1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17AMERICAN1_SPAN/17AMERICAN1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17AMERICAN1_SPAN/17AMERICAN1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Place Where Kitsch Gets All Dressed Up in Avant-Garde Clothing', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Place Where Kitsch Gets All Dressed Up in Avant-Garde Clothing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ABRONS ARTS CENTER', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'American Realness Festival', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T23:19:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d13fc7a-bc1b-5461-825a-5ec7859e3695', 'word_count': 932, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d13fc7a-bc1b-5461-825a-5ec7859e3695'}, {'abstract': 'A selected guide to performances of indie rock, electronic dance music, hip-hop, neo-psych rock, folk-pop and more in the New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/arts/music/pop-rock-listings-for-jan-17-23.html', 'snippet': 'A selected guide to performances of indie rock, electronic dance music, hip-hop, neo-psych rock, folk-pop and more in the New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Prices may not reflect ticketing service charges. For full reviews of recent concerts: nytimes.com/music. A searchable guide to these and other shows is at nytimes.com/events.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pop & Rock Listings for Jan. 17-23', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings: Pop', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T23:19:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/eea093b9-a97a-5e7f-9e37-de3816fc5a45', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eea093b9-a97a-5e7f-9e37-de3816fc5a45'}, {'abstract': 'The concert “Brazilian Explorative Music,” on Monday, featured players who put fresh spins on the region’s art forms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/arts/music/brazilian-explorative-music-a-concert-of-experimentation.html', 'snippet': 'The concert “Brazilian Explorative Music,” on Monday, featured players who put fresh spins on the region’s art forms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Brazilian popular music has few rivals anywhere for structural sophistication. That hasn’t prevented at least one cabal of experimenters from setting out to add even more intricacies. On Monday night, the third annual “Brazilian Explorative Music,” a concert organized and headlined by the songwriter Pedro Sá Moraes, brought all the members of Coletivo Chama (the Flame Collective) to S.O.B.’s. They brandished advanced ways to construct, dismantle and sometimes disrupt a piece.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Brazil’s Rhythms and Stretching Them Out', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking Brazil’s Rhythms and Stretching Them Out', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sa Moraes, Pedro', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T23:21:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9a97060-3f4a-5d16-8a85-b4bd06a9b48c', 'word_count': 458, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9a97060-3f4a-5d16-8a85-b4bd06a9b48c'}, {'abstract': 'Business officials say advocacy groups often demonize companies unfairly and inaccurately, while the groups question why corporations have attacked such fledgling organizations.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/business/as-worker-advocacy-groups-gain-momentum-businesses-fight-back.html', 'snippet': 'Business officials say advocacy groups often demonize companies unfairly and inaccurately, while the groups question why corporations have attacked such fledgling organizations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As America’s labor unions have lost members and clout, new types of worker advocacy groups have sprouted nationwide, and they have started to get on businesses’ nerves — protesting low wages at Capital Grille restaurants, for instance, and demonstrating outside Austin City Hall in Texas against giving Apple tax breaks.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/business/Faceoff2/Faceoff2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 369, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/17/business/Faceoff2/Faceoff2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 369}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/business/Faceoff2/Faceoff2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name'