 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Alain's Wine & Tapas (Nyack, NY, Restaurant)", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Alain's Bistro (Nyack, NY, Restaurant)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-16T23:27:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily DeNitto', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeNitto', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c776b14b-030f-56ce-9954-a410a2ec993f', 'word_count': 668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c776b14b-030f-56ce-9954-a410a2ec993f'}, {'abstract': 'The British pop band London Grammar performed on Wednesday at Baby’s All Right, a club in Williamsburg, Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/arts/music/london-grammar-british-pop-band-avoids-the-cliche.html', 'snippet': 'The British pop band London Grammar performed on Wednesday at Baby’s All Right, a club in Williamsburg, Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Longing, loving, leaving: Those are the boundaries of the melancholy, impeccable pop universe that London Grammar has built for itself. It’s a realm of quietly tolling piano chords, modestly picked guitar parts and reticent drums, all deferring to Hannah Reid’s velvety, sustained voice. “This one always makes me cry,” Ms. Reid told the audience at Baby’s All Right, a club in Williamsburg, Brooklyn, on Wednesday night as the band started “Stay Awake.” But it was just one of the many lovelorn wishes she would sing during a set that never budged above midtempo and never had to.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/arts/17SUBGRAMMAR_SPAN/SUBGRAMMAR-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width