 'kicker': 'Patrick Chappatte', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T12:50:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/54fab235-6db6-597f-b735-1458e9dc77b9', 'word_count': 6, 'uri': 'nyt://article/54fab235-6db6-597f-b735-1458e9dc77b9'}, {'abstract': 'For his wordless black-and-white film “Visitors,” Godfrey Reggio had a tough time shooting some of the images.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/movies/godfrey-reggios-visitors-is-a-two-way-street-for-viewers.html', 'snippet': 'For his wordless black-and-white film “Visitors,” Godfrey Reggio had a tough time shooting some of the images.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the beginning of the new film “Visitors,” opening on Friday, an image fades up slowly from black. As Philip Glass’s anticipatory strings play on the soundtrack, the figure dominating the screen is gradually revealed to be a gorilla. But shown in slow motion against a black background, the magnificent animal looks as if she is solemnly posing. Barely moving, she stares right into the lens, and seemingly right into the viewer’s eyes for moments on end.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'You Watch This Film as It Watches You', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'You Watch This Film as It Watches You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murphy, Mekado', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': '