rsons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T16:19:58+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'U.S.', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/1b22b4e3-e11d-5467-b143-f7f5d1a9ee75', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/1b22b4e3-e11d-5467-b143-f7f5d1a9ee75'}, {'abstract': 'Felipe Fernández-Armesto’s revisionist history of America pays tribute to its Hispanic past, present and future.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/books/review/our-america-by-felipe-fernandez-armesto.html', 'snippet': 'Felipe Fernández-Armesto’s revisionist history of America pays tribute to its Hispanic past, present and future.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Our America,” Felipe Fernández-\xadArmesto, a British historian of Spanish heritage at the University of Notre Dame, recasts the pilgrimage of Hispanics in the United States as a rich and moving chronicle for our very present. His book navigates five centuries of painful documents, atrocious statements and dubious literature to argue that the United States was, from its beginning, as much a Spanish colonial southern enterprise as an unending march westward. After long periods of migration, deportation and accommodation, the next United States could well be a pluricultural bilingual power, updating the American dream. “Our America” is perhaps the first history to make the case for this nation’s becoming a bright Latin American country.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Ortega/0112-bks-Ortega-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 295, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlar