elated species came to inhabit far-flung regions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This book begins with a small struggle in an arroyo near the southern tip of Baja California. It’s June 2000, and the author and his girlfriend, Tara, are trying to pull a snake from a hole. She’s gripping it by the neck. He’s reaching down the snake’s body trying to gain purchase. “The process is exhausting,” Alan de Queiroz writes, “not because it’s physically difficult, but because we’re fighting against the will of another being; with each pull I feel the snake resisting.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19weiner-sub/0119-bks-Weiner-1389130543715-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 342, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19weiner-sub/0119-bks-Weiner-1389130543715-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 342}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19weiner-sub/0119-bks-Weiner-1389130543715-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 584, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19weiner-sub/0119-bks-Weiner-1389130543715-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1168, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19weiner-sub/19weiner-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19weiner-sub/19weiner-sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19weiner-sub/19weiner-sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Darwin’s Arks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Darwin’s Arks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution (Biology)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Queiroz, Alan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T16:25:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan Weiner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f67d06b7-b727-51c6-9662-dbf08f7a596b', 'word_count': 1176, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f67d06b7-b727-51c6-9662-dbf08f7a596b'}, {'abstract': 'Christine Feehan, whose paranormal romance novel “Dark Wolf” is No. 3 on the hardcover fiction list, wrote her first steamy love scene during a middle school math class.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Christine Feehan, whose paranormal romance novel “Dark Wolf” is No. 3 on the hardcover fiction list, wrote her first steamy love scene during a middle school math class.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Mood: Christine Feehan’s new \xadnovel, “Dark Wolf,” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 3. It’s the 25th book in her “Dark” series of paranormal romances, about a race of shape-shifters called the Carpathians — basically, kindhearted vampires who refuse to kill humans and often have energetic, soul-trembling sex with them. Feehan discovered her calling while she was still in middle school; speaking with the romance website RBL Romantica early in her career, she remembered sitting in math class and working on her first steamy love scene until the teacher confiscated her notebook. “You should have seen his face,” she said. “I was about 13 at the time. I don’t know which of us was more mortified.” But despite her early start she delayed publishing for a long time, working more than 20 years as a martial arts instructor (she has a third-degree black belt in karate) and writing for herself while she and her husband raised 11 children. “I can write with the entire household around me most of the time,” she told another website in 2002. “My husband reads all my books and had better love them — and if he doesn’t, he’d better lie like crazy with a smile on his face. The only thing I avoid while writing a love scene is my children. Children and writing love scenes don’t mix; they get you out of the mood very, very fast.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19feehan/19feehan-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19feehan/19feehan-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 456, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19feehan/19feehan-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19feehan/19feehan-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19feehan/19feehan-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T16:26:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b595e470-efa1-5324-a166-7dcc9495fc2b', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b595e470-efa1-5324-a166-7dcc9495fc2b'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE POWER OF HABIT: Why We Do What We Do in Life and Business,  by Charles Duhigg. (Random House, $16.) Duhigg, a Pulitzer-\xadwinning reporter for The New York Times, looks at the science behind how we form, and break, habits. In engrossing narratives that take us from corporate boardrooms to N.F.L. sidelines and the civil rights movement, he distills research from the fields of social psychology, clinical psychology and neuroscience.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T16:26:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b5ab1ef-0a85-5248-aafd-c98c0ad2dead', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b5ab1ef-0a85-5248-aafd-c98c0ad2dead'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ANDREW’S BRAIN,  by E. L. Doctorow. (Random House, $26.) A neuroscientist frustrated in his research, and with a messy personal life, explores his own oppressive consciousness in Doctorow’s cunning but serious novel.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T16:26:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5547b32-b334-5097-b019-1643281bc4f9', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5547b32-b334-5097-b019-1643281bc4f9'}, {'abstract': 'Nicholas Shakespeare investigates his aunt’s complicated experience in occupied France.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/books/review/nicholas-shakespeares-priscilla.