'A con man hides out at a famous writing program in this satiric novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Feeling like a fraud or a con artist can be a hazard of the writing profession; there’s a fine line, Balzac wrote, between the artist and the criminal. That’s certainly true of Frankie Abandonato — a New York grifter who makes the critical error of pulling a lottery scam on a mobster. Abandonato, the hero of James Magnuson’s novel “Famous Writers I Have Known,” slips town for Texas, holing up at a creative writing institute where he’s mistaken for a J. D. Salingeresque recluse named V. S. Mohle. (The real Mohle was expected on campus to teach a seminar while either reconciling or settling an old score — it’s complicated — with the institute’s fabulously successful founder, Rex Schoeninger, whose sprawling social novels are reminiscent of James Michener’s; their feud culminated years ago in a brawl on “The Dick Cavett Show,” after which Mohle dropped out of society and never published again.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Austin City Limits', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Austin City Limits', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Magnuson, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T16:31:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Langer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Langer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e53a1b7e-f49c-5158-9253-e60b84ba6865', 'word_count': 566, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e53a1b7e-f49c-5158-9253-e60b84ba6865'}, {'abstract': 'Armistead Maupin concludes his series with a reunion of the tribe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/books/review/the-days-of-anna-madrigal-by-armistead-maupin.html', 'snippet': 'Armistead Maupin concludes his series with a reunion of the tribe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the mid-1970s, the managing editor of The San Francisco Chronicle kept a chart in his office with two columns: “heterosexual” and “homosexual.” Whenever a new character appeared in “Tales of the City” — the newspaper’s fiction serial by Armistead Maupin, which begat a stack of popular novels — the name was slotted accordingly.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Bruder/0112-bks-Bruder-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 286, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Bruder/0112-bks-Bruder-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 286}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Bruder/0112-bks-Bruder-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 488, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Bruder/0112-bks-Bruder-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 976, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Bruder/0112-bks-Bruder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/12/books/review/0112-bks-Bruder/0112-bks-Bruder-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth