1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ee1a808-8d2a-591d-ad9d-07195a154d23'}, {'abstract': 'Felipe Fernández-Armesto discusses “Our America,” and Gabriel Sherman talks about his new biography of Roger Ailes.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/book-review-podcast-our-america-a-hispanic-history-of-the-united-states/', 'snippet': 'Felipe Fernández-Armesto discusses “Our America,” and Gabriel Sherman talks about his new biography of Roger Ailes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In The New York Times Book Review, Julio Ortega reviews Felipe Fernández-Armesto’s “Our America: A Hispanic History of the United States.” Mr. Ortega writes:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 214, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/books/review/19cover-shadow/19cover-shadow-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Book Review Podcast: ‘Our America: A Hispanic History of the United States’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hispanic-Americans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Podcasts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AILES, ROGER E', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bosman, Julie', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cowles, Gregory', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fernandez-Armesto, Felipe', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paul, Pamela', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sherman, Gabriel', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T18:06:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cad6522-a10e-5cf6-9a82-5afdcaadf711', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cad6522-a10e-5cf6-9a82-5afdcaadf711'}, {'abstract': 'Even within Congress, battles over loyalty to the Union — and the Constitution — raged.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/civil-war-in-the-senate/', 'snippet': 'Even within Congress, battles over loyalty to the Union — and the Constitution — raged.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No word was more hotly contested during the Civil War than “loyalty.” Republicans persistently accused Democrats of disloyalty and treason for opposing Republican war measures, and no Democrat was immune from accusations of disloyalty, even at the highest levels of the government. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Civil War in the Senate', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Senate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T18:10:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jonathan W. White', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jonathan', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'White', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b5ba88b-51f4-5363-8b42-31b12ccda1af', 'word_count': 1501, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b5ba88b-51f4-5363-8b42-31b12ccda1af'}, {'abstract': 'The new season of “Sherlock,” which already aired in Britain, makes its American debut this Sunday on PBS.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/a-conversation-with-steven-moffat-a-sherlock-co-creator/', 'snippet': 'The new season of “Sherlock,” which already aired in Britain, makes its American debut this Sunday on PBS.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Steven Moffat is the co-creator of the BBC hit “Sherlock” and the lead writer and executive producer of “Doctor Who,” the classic sci-fi series about a time-traveling alien that celebrated its 50th anniversary in November. He also created, in 2000, “Coupling,” a saucy sitcom about the sexual misadventures of 30-something Londoners that was based loosely on his marriage to the television producer Sue Vertue. All this makes the 52-year-old Scotsman one of the most influential figures in British television, and also a target for legions of fans who bombard the Internet with theories about how Holmes could have possibly faked his suicide or whether a woman should play the next Doctor. “It’s very dangerous for me to venture onto social media,” Mr. Moffat said by phone from London.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conversation With Steven Moffat, a ‘Sherlock’ Co-Creator', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cumberbatch, Benedict', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'British Broadcasting Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T18:12:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9544fed5-6536-541c-bfd1-ef99bae9a97e', 'word_count': 1070, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9544fed5-6536-541c-bfd1-ef99bae9a97e'}, {'abstract': 'Can American conservatives legitimately lay claim to the ideas of Edmund Burke?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/burke-in-america/', 'snippet': 'Can American conservatives legitimately lay claim to the ideas of Edmund Burke?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been following with great interest the discussion surrounding Yuval Levin’s new book, “The Great Debate,” which offers an overview of the competing worldviews of Thomas Paine and Edmund Burke in an effort to explain what the subtitle calls “the birth of right and left.” And I’ve been particularly interested in the liberal critiques of Levin’s argument, which have taken an interesting (if perhaps unsurprising) form: Very few reviewers have questioned his interpretation of his subjects’ long-running late-18th century argument, but I’ve noticed repeated attempts to deny their relevance to present-day American conservatism, which a number of left-of-center reviewers have argued owes little-or-nothing to any kind of Burkean worldview. In a later post I want to take up Sheri Berman’s version of this argument, in the liberal journal Democracy, but here let me say something about\xa0William Galston’s response to Levin, in his Wall Street Journal column, which I’ll quote at length:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Burke in America', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Articles of Confederation (1781-89)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitution (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Declaration of Independence (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Democracy (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magna Carta (1215)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Revolutionary War, American (1775-83)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burke, Edmund', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levin, Yuval', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paine, Thomas', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'England', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T18:13:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/133bd879-fcbf-5206-9d5e-1947924430ed', 'word_count': 1783, 'uri': 'nyt://article/133bd879-fcbf-5206-9d5e-1947924430ed'}, {'abstract': 'Rei Kawakubo’s autumn 2014 men’s collection opened rounded windows in the fabric to reveal the shirts beneath.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/fashion/comme-des-garcons-holey-suit.html', 'snippet': 'Rei Kawakubo’s autumn 2014 men’s collection opened rounded windows in the fabric to reveal the shirts beneath.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — “Holy suit” were the two mysterious words spoken by Rei Kawakubo before her Comme des Garçons show kicked off Friday with smart tailoring.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/fashion/18comme-top/18comme-top-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/18/fashion/18comme-top/18comme-top-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/fashion/18comme-top/18comme-top-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/fashion/18comme-top/18comme-top-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/fashion/18comme-top/18comme-top-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/18/fashion/18comme-top/18comme-top-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/fashion/18comme-top/18comme-top-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150,