rrants to Search Cellphones', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Search and Seizure', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fourth Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'First Amendment (US Constitution)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Riley, David L', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lane, Edward R', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T21:03:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Liptak', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Liptak', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f8af33ee-33e5-5f6c-a45c-1bb2aa021e9b', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f8af33ee-33e5-5f6c-a45c-1bb2aa021e9b'}, {'abstract': 'While the Internet of things could make our lives more convenient, it could also serve to compromise our privacy.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/in-the-future-your-fridge-could-get-hacked/', 'snippet': 'While the Internet of things could make our lives more convenient, it could also serve to compromise our privacy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The next great advance in technology may well be the “Internet of Things.” Simply put, many more devices and appliances that you use every day, like refrigerators, thermostats, garage doors and so on will be connected to the Internet, will adapt to your behavior and will be available to do your bidding even when you are not near them.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Future, Your Fridge Could Get Hacked', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberattacks and Hackers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Home Automation and Smart Homes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nest Labs Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T21:04:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vikas Bajaj', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vikas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bajaj', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/adbae87b-4dca-5a34-8de5-0d41bc1cf3e3', 'word_count': 313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/adbae87b-4dca-5a34-8de5-0d41bc1cf3e3'}, {'abstract': 'Deutsche Grammophon will release “St. Carolyn by the Sea,” a collection of works by Bryce Dessner (of the National) and Jonny Greenwood (of Radiohead), performed by the Copenhagen Philharmonic.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/a-pop-ish-album-from-a-storied-classical-label/', 'snippet': 'Deutsche Grammophon will release “St. Carolyn by the Sea,” a collection of works by Bryce Dessner (of the National) and Jonny Greenwood (of Radiohead), performed by the Copenhagen Philharmonic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More evidence, if any were needed, that the boundary between classical music and rock continues to erode: Deutsche Grammophon, once the most staid of the world’s classical music labels, is releasing “St. Carolyn by the Sea,” a collection of works by Bryce Dessner, best known as a guitarist in the National, and Jonny Greenwood, the lead guitarist in Radiohead. The album, recorded by the Copenhagen Philharmonic, led by André de Ridder, is due out March 3.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Where a Storied Classical Label Embraces Rockers', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dessner, Bryce', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenwood, Jonny', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Deutsche Grammophon', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T21:06:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d7147a6-0ee9-5e80-b861-8e1e0f403cf2', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d7147a6-0ee9-5e80-b861-8e1e0f403cf2'}, {'abstract': 'The orchestra announced Friday that its first concert back in Orchestra Hall would be given Feb. 7 and conducted by Stanislaw Skrowaczewski.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/minnesota-orchestra-announces-first-concert-after-lockout/', 'snippet': 'The orchestra announced Friday that its first concert back in Orchestra Hall would be given Feb. 7 and conducted by Stanislaw Skrowaczewski.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the 15-month lockout of its musicians now behind it, the Minnesota Orchestra announced Friday that its first concert back in Orchestra Hall would be given Feb. 7 and would be conducted by Stanislaw Skrowaczewski, its music director from 1960 to 1979.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Minnesota Orchestra Announces First Concert After Lockout', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Minnesota Orchestra', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T21:07:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cooper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8973775d-65ac-54dd-b559-69bc11f11424', 'word_count': 260, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8973775d-65ac-54dd-b559-69bc11f11424'}, {'abstract': 'In the post-Cold-War era, Boris Badenov remains a model villain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/19/opinion/sunday/why-are-russians-still-the-go-to-bad-guys.html', 'snippet': 'In the post-Cold-War era, Boris Badenov remains a model villain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE movie “Jack Ryan: Shadow Recruit,” which opens in theaters this weekend, revolves around an American C.I.A. analyst first introduced in Tom Clancy’s 1984 novel “The Hunt for Red October.” The source material isn’t the only thing that’s a little creaky. Ryan’s destination is Moscow, his target a Russian businessman plotting to crash the American economy through a terrorist attack. In portraying the diabolical oligarch Viktor Cherevin, Kenneth Branagh delivers his lines in the thick, menacingly slow accent that defines Eastern European baddies on screen: “You think this is game, Jack?”', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/sunday-review/19RUSSIANsub2/19RUSSIANsub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/19/sunday-review/19RUSSIANsub2/19RUSSIANsub2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 759}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/sunday-review/19RUSSIANsub2/19RUSSIANsub2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 810, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/sunday-review/19RUSSIANsub2/19RUSSIANsub2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1620, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/sunday-review/19RUSSIANsub2/19RUSSIANsub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/19/sunday-review/19RUSSIANsub2/19RUSSIANsub2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/sunday-review/19RUSSIANsub2/19RUSSIANsub2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Russians: Still the Go-To Bad Guys', 'kicker': 'Dispatch', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russians: Still the Go-To Bad Guys', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cold War Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T21:07:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Kurutz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurutz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/1561ddac-2604-543d-a781-86517146b9c4', 'word_count': 730, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1561ddac-2604-543d-a781-86517146b9c4'}, {'abstract': 'Brought together by a classroom friendship at Paris’s hippest primary school, the French clothier and children’s retailer team up for a pint-sized capsule collection.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/perfect-pairing-a-p-c-and-bonton-design-childrens-wear-thats-too-cool-for-school/', 'snippet': 'Brought together by a classroom friendship at Paris’s hippest primary school, the French clothier and children’s retailer team up for a pint-sized capsule collection.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The A.P.C. founder Jean Touitou believes that children are never too young to be exposed to good design. He founded a creativity-centered Paris school, Atelier de la Petite Enfance (A.P.E. for short), to educate his now-9-year-old daughter Lily; the space, designed by the architect and frequent A.P.C. collaborator Laurent Deroo, features tiny Alvar Aalto chairs and building blocks created by the art director Mathias Augustyniak of M/M Paris. When the school’s art department needed uniforms for the children to wear while painting, Touitou turned to Bonton, the hip French children’s clothier — whose founders, Thomas and Irène Cohen, are the son and daughter-in-law of the owners of\xa0Bonpoint\xa0and\xa0have a daughter in Lily’s class. The uniforms also ended up in Bonton’s five retail outlets, where they became a best seller. “The design was the right balance between playful and straight,” Touitou says. “I sort of knew that we’d have kids together one day, so to speak.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/t-magazine/17apc-moroz/17apc-moroz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/17/t-magazine/17apc-moroz/17apc-moroz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/t-magazine/17apc-moroz/17apc-moroz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/t-magazine/17apc-moroz/17apc-moroz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/t-magazine/17apc-moroz/17apc-moroz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/17/t-magazine/17apc-moroz/17apc-moroz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/17/t-magazine/17apc-moroz/17apc-moroz-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A.P.C. and Bonton Design Children’s Wear That’s Too Cool for School', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Perfect Pairing: Cool in School', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sneakers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'APC (Atelier de Production et de Creation)', 'rank':