, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bahrain', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Sudan', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T21:25:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bd777a2-df32-5031-9153-0966bde6d779', 'word_count': 13, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bd777a2-df32-5031-9153-0966bde6d779'}, {'abstract': 'Anthony Minghella’s “Madama Butterfly” has returned to the Metropolitan Opera with the South African soprano Amanda Echalaz in the title role.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/arts/music/madama-butterfly-with-amanda-echalaz-in-the-title-role.html', 'snippet': 'Anthony Minghella’s “Madama Butterfly” has returned to the Metropolitan Opera with the South African soprano Amanda Echalaz in the title role.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In many ways, Anthony Minghella’s production of Puccini’s “Madama Butterfly,” which returned to the Metropolitan Opera on Thursday, resembles an insect collection in which colorful, exotic specimens are fixed on a neutral background and bathed in a sharp, bright light. It’s a fitting way to present this ever-popular opera about a caddish American naval officer who enters into a nonbinding marriage with a teenage Japanese geisha and then abandons her, pregnant, lovesick and cast out from her community. Featuring innovative puppetry by Blind Summit Theater, it’s also an uncomfortable production that introduces multiple perspectives in ways that illuminate and alienate in equal measure.', 'print_section': 'C', 'pr