'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Robbins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da427e57-6c7e-58cb-bb89-db67625fed4f', 'word_count': 2472, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da427e57-6c7e-58cb-bb89-db67625fed4f'}, {'abstract': '“BigMouth,” from the Belgian actor-writer-director Valentijn Dhaenens, jostles together the words of speechmakers throughout history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/theater/bigmouth-lets-talkers-across-time-have-their-say.html', 'snippet': '“BigMouth,” from the Belgian actor-writer-director Valentijn Dhaenens, jostles together the words of speechmakers throughout history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “BigMouth,” his one-man show in the Under the Radar Festival, the Belgian stage veteran Valentijn Dhaenens takes the long view. Centuries long: In this spare, thought-provoking production, which he directed, Mr. Dhaenens quotes speeches from luminaries across millenniums. Some, like Pericles and George S. Patton, were military commanders; others, like Ronald Reagan and  King Baudouin I of Belgium, national rulers; and still others, like Socrates and the Rev. Dr. Martin Luther King Jr., philosophical leaders. Several, like Nicola Sacco and Ann Coulter, could be called political radicals.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Bit of Bush and bin Laden', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Bit of Bush and bin Laden', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Under the Radar Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'BigMouth (Play)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dhaenens, Valentijn', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T22:15:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Webster', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Webster', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/068dda92-0d56-52dd-9545-ce26b8780086', 'word_count': 348, 'uri': 'nyt://article/068dda92-0d56-52dd-9545-ce26b8780086'}, {'abstract': 'There is an easier way to email a web page link on the Nexus 7 without copying it from the browser and pasting into a message.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/technology/personaltech/sharing-web-links-without-cutting-and-pasting.html', 'snippet': 'There is an easier way to email a web page link on the Nexus 7 without copying it from the browser and pasting into a message.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q. Is there an easier way to email a web page link on the Nexus 7 than copying and pasting the link from the browser into the mail app?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sharing Web Links Without Cutting and Pasting', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Windows (Operating System)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Security', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-17T22:21:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'