ations', 'value': 'Gotham West Market (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Daisy May's BBQ (Manhattan, NY, Restaurant)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T00:27:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3e79adc-ef0a-546b-bac1-af14a339fdfa', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3e79adc-ef0a-546b-bac1-af14a339fdfa'}, {'abstract': 'The Australian government found that nearly every woman has experienced some form of physical violence, sexual violence or both.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/world/asia/papua-new-guinea-where-violence-can-seem-like-the-norm-for-women.html', 'snippet': 'The Australian government found that nearly every woman has experienced some form of physical violence, sexual violence or both.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Papua New Guinea, a developing country of seven million people with a growing market in the mining of natural resources, is one of the most violent places in the world for women, according to the United Nations. In the country’s remote Highlands, the Australian government found that nearly every woman has experienced some form of physical violence, including sexual violence. After decades of abuse from her husband, Agnes, who is now living in a safe house said: “I thought that that was normal for a woman to be beaten by her husband. I never thought it wasn’t right.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Video: Papua New Guinea, Where Violence Can Seem Like the Norm for Women', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Papua New Guinea', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Domestic Violence', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Doctors Without Borders', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T00:31:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8268c187-ffdb-5d23-aa66-81b63e40b62a', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8268c187-ffdb-5d23-aa66-81b63e40b62a'}, {'abstract': 'The automaker will add tow hitches on some Liberty S.U.V.’s and Grand Cherokees in an effort to lower danger from fire in rear-impact collisions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/automobiles/unusual-chrysler-fix-for-jeep-fires-wins-approval.html', 'snippet': 'The automaker will add tow hitches on some Liberty S.U.V.’s and Grand Cherokees in an effort to lower danger from fire in rear-impact collisions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chrysler can go ahead with an unusual recall plan affecting 1.6 million Jeeps, the National Highway Traffic Safety Administration said late Friday. The plan involves the use of a trailer hitch intended for towing to reduce the danger of gas tank fires in rear-impact crashes.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unusual Chrysler Fix for Jeep Fires Wins Approval', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Unusual Chrysler Fix for Jeep Fires Wins Approval', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jensen, Christopher', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobile Safety Features and Defect