 and “change” presidential campaign its distinctive look. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/business/18typeface/18typeface-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 421, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/18/business/18typeface/18typeface-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 421}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/business/18typeface/18typeface-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/business/18typeface/18typeface-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1437, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/business/18typeface/18typeface-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/18/business/18typeface/18typeface-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/18/business/18typeface/18typeface-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Typography Partners Part Ways in Money Fight', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Typography Partners Part Ways in Money Spat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Typography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cohen, Noam', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation (Civil)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hoefler & Frere-Jones', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Frere-Jones, Tobias', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoefler, Jonathan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T00:57:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/802433ca-3bf7-5f1b-b9be-1279de1a56a7', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/802433ca-3bf7-5f1b-b9be-1279de1a56a7'}, {'abstract': 'In the latest installment of the Source, a series of conversations on the roots of creativity, the artist Doug Aitken interviews the actress Tilda Swinton, who talks about identity, language and performance.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/17/the-source-tilda-swinton-in-conversation-with-doug-aitken/', 'snippet': 'In the latest installment of the Source, a series of conversations on the roots of creativity, the artist Doug Aitken interviews the actress Tilda Swinton, who talks about identity, language and performance.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The thing that keeps me being a performer,” Tilda Swinton says, “is my interest in society’s obsession with identity.” Swinton, who will appear in the upcoming Wes Anderson film, “The Grand Budapest Hotel,” and is known for her roles in “I Am Love,” “Michael Clayton” and “Adaptation,” talks to the artist Doug Aitken about what drives her as an actress. (She appeared in the 2007 Aitken film installation “Sleepwalkers.”) The interview is part of the Source, a series of candid video conversations with several dozen noteworthy figures about their creative process, currently on view in a custom pavilion at the Sundance Film Festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Source | Tilda Swinton in Conversation With Doug Aitken', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aitken, Doug', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Swinton, Tilda', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T01:00:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46f63470-9295-50f2-a519-ad6d4b01f13c', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46f63470-9295-50f2-a519-ad6d4b01f13c'}, {'abstract': 'Political machines served the people when the government didn’t.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/opinion/the-forgotten-virtues-of-tammany-hall.html', 'snippet': 'Political machines served the people when the government didn’t.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DURING last summer’s contentious primary campaign, Bill de Blasio managed to get himself selected as his party’s candidate for mayor of New York without the support of the Democratic Party’s once-powerful county organizations. Historically, that’s quite an achievement. Even Ed Koch, the man who helped put a stake in the heart of Tammany Hall in the early 1960s, had the tacit backing of machines in Brooklyn and the Bronx when he captured the mayoralty in 1977.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'cap