a-rhino-killings-increase.html', 'snippet': 'The South African government reported Friday that poachers killed 1,004 rhinos in South Africa last year, up from 668 in 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Poachers killed 1,004 rhinos in South Africa last year, up from 668 in 2012, as they continue to hunt the animals for their valuable horns, the South African government reported Friday. The Department of Environmental Affairs blamed poachers who cross the border from Mozambique to South Africa, then sell the horns to places like Vietnam and China, where they are symbols of wealth and are used in potions. The number of rhinos illegally killed in South Africa has been climbing since at least 2010, when 333 deaths were reported. Susie Ellis, of the global nonprofit group International Rhino Foundation, said the latest figure was almost certainly an undercount. “Those are only the deaths that we know about,” she said. Traffic, an international wildlife watchdog group, said Mozambique treated rhino-related crimes as misdemeanors. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Africa: Rhino Killings Increase', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Africa', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Africa: Rhino Killings Increase', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rhinoceroses', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poaching (Wildlife)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ivory', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T03:37:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louis Lucero II', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louis', 'middlename': 'Lucero', 'lastname': 'II', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/fded215f-48e1-5f0e-8e81-2686d77c49c5', 'word_count': 132, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fded215f-48e1-5f0e-8e81-2686d77c49c5'}, {'abstract': 'President Abdullah Gul signed a measure into law on Friday that makes it a crime to administer emergency first aid without government authorization.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/world/europe/turkey-contentious-medical-bill-signed.html', 'snippet': 'President Abdullah Gul signed a measure into law on Friday that makes it a crime to administer emergency first aid without government authorization.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Over the objections of human rights and medical groups, President Abdullah Gul signed a measure into law on Friday that makes it a crime to administer emergency first aid without government authorization. Critics contend that the law could be used by the police and prosecutors to intimidate and punish doctors and other medical workers for treating protesters wounded in demonstrations against the government, like those in Turkey last year. “Passing a bill that criminalizes emergency care and punishes those who care for injured protesters is part of the Turkish government’s relentless effort to silence any opposing voices,” said Dr. Vincent Iacopino, senior medical adviser at Physicians for Human Rights, one of the groups that had campaigned against the legislation. If convicted, violators could be imprisoned for up to three years and face fines of nearly $1 million. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey: Contentious Medical Bill Signed', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey: Contentious Medical Bill Signed', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'First Aid', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gul, Abdullah', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Physicians for Human Rights', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Emergency Medical Treatment', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T03:40:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rick Gladstone', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gladstone', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/b23a10df-8d1a-5b12-8b43-5dbd0abc1e42', 'word_count': 137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b23a10df-8d1a-5b12-8b43-5dbd0abc1e42'}, {'abstract': 'A former soldier charged with conspiring to kill a federal agent and a federal informer had gotten out of his handcuffs on his own, a federal prosecutor said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/nyregion/to-rambo-hit-man-case-add-an-element-of-houdini.html', 'snippet': 'A former soldier charged with conspiring to kill a federal agent and a federal informer had gotten out of his handcuffs on his own, a federal prosecutor said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When charges were announced last fall against Joseph Hunter, a former Army sergeant accused of being a contract killer, the authorities revealed that one of his nicknames was Rambo.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'To ‘Rambo’ Hit-Man Case, Add an Element of Houdini', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'To ‘Rambo’ Hit-Man Case, Add an Element of Houdini', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Correctional Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gogel, Dennis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Vamvakias, Timothy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Drug Enforcement Administration', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Liberia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunter, Joseph M', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T03:41:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39a38a88-8bff-54b1-bd53-65fe65ac98b4', 'word_count': 435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39a38a88-8bff-54b1-bd53-65fe65ac98b4'}, {'abstract': 'A top Japanese lawmaker on Friday denounced American criticism of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s visit last month to a Tokyo war shrine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/18/world/asia/japan-lawmaker-defends-shrine-visit.html', 'snippet': 'A top Japanese lawmaker on Friday denounced American criticism of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s visit last month to a Tokyo war shrine.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A top Japanese lawmaker on Friday denounced American criticism of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe’s visit last month to a Tokyo war shrine, comparing it to American politicians’ paying their respects at Arlington National Cemetery. The lawmaker, Koichi Hagiuda of Mr. Abe’s governing Liberal Democratic Party, said it was unfair for the United States to expect Japanese leaders to refrain from visiting the Yasukuni shrine, which honors the nation’s war dead, when American politicians can freely honor their fallen soldiers. “If Japan says, ‘It’s unfavorable to pay a visit to Arlington National Cemetery,’ do they refrain from going there?” Mr. Hagiuda, a special adviser to Mr. Abe, was quoted as saying by the Kyodo News Agency. “I assume the United States will say, ‘It’s none of a foreign country’s business.’\xa0” Mr. Hagiuda was responding to an American government statement expressing disappointment with Mr. Abe’s visit to the controversial Shinto shrine on Dec. 26. Washington feared such a visit could anger other Asian nations, where Yasukuni is viewed as a symbol of Japan’s refusal to come to terms with its brutal 20th-century militarism because it honors all war dead, including convicted war criminals. The shrine also houses a museum that views World War II as a noble struggle by Japan to liberate Asia from white imperialism. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan: Lawmaker Defends Shrine Visit', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan: Lawmaker Defends Shrine Visit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-18T03:43:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d2deefe-403e-5584-993a-dcb06fc1c89a', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d2deefe-403e-5584-993a-dcb06fc1c89a'}, {'abstract': 'Lottery numbers for Jan. 17, 2014, in N