rganizations', 'value': 'Ta Kung Pao', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Liaoning (China)', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ukraine', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-20T07:04:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ea666bf-c55e-5a2c-82c8-96b610578afe', 'word_count': 391, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ea666bf-c55e-5a2c-82c8-96b610578afe'}, {'abstract': 'At the Jaipur Literature Festival, Mukulika Banerjee, who wrote “Why India Votes,” spoke with India Ink about why the act of voting is so meaningful for the majority of Indians.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/20/a-conversation-with-anthropologist-mukulika-banerjee/', 'snippet': 'At the Jaipur Literature Festival, Mukulika Banerjee, who wrote “Why India Votes,” spoke with India Ink about why the act of voting is so meaningful for the majority of Indians.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Voting in elections is considered sacrosanct by a large majority of Indians,” Mukulika Banerjee writes in the introduction to her new book, “Why India Votes.” That observation forms the backbone of the anthropology professor’s work, an ethnographic study of 12 sites in India during the 2009 general elections, which explores the motivations and opinions of Indian voters on a range of issues related to the electoral process.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/20/world/20-JLF-Madhulika-IndiaInk/20-JLF-Madhulika