a certain sense of ceremony and a certain yoke of obligation. Before cellphones were omnipresent, the beeper was a badge of professional importance: I’m a doctor — life and death depend on my instant availability.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/science/21KLAS/21KLAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 563, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/21/science/21KLAS/21KLAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 563}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/science/21KLAS/21KLAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/science/21KLAS/21KLAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1573, 'width': 1677, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/science/21KLAS/21KLAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/21/science/21KLAS/21KLAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/science/21KLAS/21KLAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Taste of My Own Manners', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Taste of My Own Manners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cellular Telephones', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-20T20:09:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Perri Klass, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Perri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klass', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f18353eb-aec0-5674-a46c-498484f3603e', 'word_count': 888, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f18353eb-aec0-5674-a46c-498484f3603e'}, {'abstract': 'Listening to a tiny snippet of “Rhapsody in Blue” for hours on United’s customer service line is enough to drive a passenger to a travel agent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/business/sour-notes-on-the-interminable-wait-for-an-airlines-aid.html', 'snippet': 'Listening to a tiny snippet of “Rhapsody in Blue” for hours on United’s customer service line is enough to drive a passenger to a travel agent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'AFTER George Gershwin’s “Rhapsody in Blue” was born, it became a classic of the Jazz Age. But if you ask me, it died this month. The cause was overwork. “Rhapsody” would have been 90 years old on Feb. 12, the anniversary of its first performance in 1924.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/business/ROAD/ROAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/21/business/ROAD/ROAD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 667}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/business/ROAD/ROAD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024