ount': 116, 'uri': 'nyt://article/578a36a4-91b6-5cd8-a889-7cb0cd4c446a'}, {'abstract': 'Letter to the editor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/science/costs-of-climate-change.html', 'snippet': 'Letter to the editor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TO THE EDITOR: ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Costs of Climate Change', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Costs of Climate Change', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-20T21:26:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf2aa8ea-7d0c-54fa-9b8f-11d5476a7276', 'word_count': 126, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf2aa8ea-7d0c-54fa-9b8f-11d5476a7276'}, {'abstract': 'An all-female group show surveys L.A.’s burgeoning metaphysical-art movement.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/20/art-matters-the-mothership-lands-in-downtown-los-angeles/', 'snippet': 'An all-female group show surveys L.A.’s burgeoning metaphysical-art movement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Currently eight and a half months pregnant with her first child, the artist and curator Carly Jo Morgan has been exploring themes of motherhood since long before her own was imminent. Years ago, she designed a tribal-futurist “Mothership” collection for her jewelry line, All for the Mountain, that led to a commission from Sonia Rykiel. And recently, she became a certified birth doula. Now Morgan is co-curating a women’s art show, called “The Mothership,” which opened last Thursday at Dilettante, a creative incubator and event space at the edge of the Arts District in Downtown L.A. “I started thinking about the idea of a force that is guiding these vessels — these women and artists,” explains Morgan, who lives in a cabin in Topanga Canyon, of the show’s concept. “Being pregnant, I do feel very connected to the divine feminine. But growing a baby is also very sci-fi.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Art Matters | “The Mothership” Lands in Downtown Los Angeles', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-20T21:40:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steffie Nelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steffie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/046bea54-7401-553f-917c-4d4dff961f1d', 'word_count': 513, 'uri': 'nyt://article/046bea54-7401-553f-917c-4d4dff961f1d'}, {'abstract': 'According to a new study, primates burn about half as many calories per day as mammals of comparable size — and not simply because the other mammals are more active.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/science/longer-life-span-tied-to-energy-use-in-primates.html', 'snippet': 'According to a new study, primates burn about half as many calories per day as mammals of comparable size — and not simply because the other mammals are more active.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Most mammals reach adulthood within months and die in their teens or 20s. Not so with humans and other primates, which take years to become adults, then live for decades and reproduce infrequently.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type':