'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By RON GLUCKMAN', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'GLUCKMAN', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5554c2fc-86ef-5746-ac63-99bea14f6b61', 'word_count': 1156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5554c2fc-86ef-5746-ac63-99bea14f6b61'}, {'abstract': 'Readers including Jacob Bender of the Council on American Islamic Relations respond to an editorial.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/opinion/obstacles-on-the-road-to-mideast-democracy.html', 'snippet': 'Readers including Jacob Bender of the Council on American Islamic Relations respond to an editorial.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To the Editor:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obstacles on the Road to Mideast Democracy', 'kicker': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obstacles on the Road to Mideast Democracy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Constitutions', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-21T00:45:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/77076dd0-52b0-5517-ae54-adc07eef605a', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77076dd0-52b0-5517-ae54-adc07eef605a'}, {'abstract': 'Christodoulos Xiros, a leftist guerrilla who walked away from prison while on furlough this month, on Monday announced his return to terrorism.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/world/europe/greece-prison-escapee-vows-to-fight-again.html', 'snippet': 'Christodoulos Xiros, a leftist guerrilla who walked away from prison while on furlough this month, on Monday announced his return to terrorism.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A leftist Greek guerrilla who walked away from a prison while on furlough this month announced his return to terrorism on Monday. The fugitive, Christodoulos Xiros, did so in an online video and letter, in which he called on “comrades of armed action” to unite to protest the repercussions of three years of economic austerity in Greece. “Once again I have taken the decision to thunder the guerrilla rifle against those who stole our lives and sold our dreams for a profit,” the letter said. It was dated Jan. 14 and signed by Mr. Xiros, who described himself as “a free member of November 17,” once considered Greece’s deadliest terrorist organization. Mr. Xiros, 55, was serving multiple life terms at the Korydallos Prison near Athens for a series of attacks carried out by the group, chiefly against Greek, British and American business and political targets over nearly three decades. He has been at large since failing to return from a nine-day furlough that began on Jan. 1. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Greece: Prison Escapee Vows to Fight Again', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Greece: Escapee Vows to Fight Again', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kitsantonis, Niki', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Xiros, Christodoulos', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fugitives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'November 17', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greece', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-21T00:46:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Niki Kitsantonis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Niki', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kitsantonis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5881e99-f1f6-5e98-a5fc-bdcbb837124b', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5881e99-f1f6-5e98-a5fc-bdcbb837124b'}, {'abstract': 'Two Navy warships will be in the Black Sea during the Olympics in Sochi, Russia, next month, should they be needed to evacuate Americans, a Pentagon official said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/world/europe/us-plans-naval-backup-for-olympics.html', 'snippet': 'Two Navy warships will be in the Black Sea during the Olympics in Sochi, Russia, next month, should they be needed to evacuate Americans, a Pentagon official said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The American military’s European Command is drawing up plans to have two Navy warships in the Black Sea at the time of the Winter Olympics in Sochi, Russia, next month, should they be needed to evacuate Americans in the event of a terrorist attack or any other emergency, a Pentagon official said Monday. American transport planes would also be on the call in the region. So far, the Pentagon has not put the plans into effect, although that order could come in a matter of days, the official said. The ships are to be passing through the region as part of a regularly scheduled deployment, and the planes would remain at their bases, probably in Europe, until ordered to help. The planning was first reported by CNN on Monday. The State Department would coordinate any evacuation of Americans. Any plans to use the American ships or planes would be done in coordination with and with permission from Russia, American officials said. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Plans Naval Backup for Olympics', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Plans Naval Backup for Olympics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Sochi (Russia)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schmitt, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games (2014)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Navy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Black Sea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evacuations and Evacuees', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-21T00:51:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Schmitt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/1372885a-89b1-573d-8c76-1fe3345a3425', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1372885a-89b1-573d-8c76-1fe3345a3425'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo and Eric T. Schneiderman, New York State’s attorney general, will split $163 million of the $613 million settlement the state received over JPMorgan Chase’s mortgage-lending practices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/nyregion/cuomo-and-attorney-general-settle-part-of-a-money-dispute.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo and Eric T. Schneiderman, New York State’s attorney general, will split $163 million of the $613 million settlement the state received over JPMorgan Chase’s mortgage-lending practices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ALBANY — Gov. Andrew M. Cuomo and Eric T. Schneiderman, New York State’s attorney general, have reached an agreement on how to divide a big piece of the $613 million the state received as part of a securities settlement with JPMorgan Chase.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cuomo and Attorney General Agree on Division of JPMorgan Funds', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Albany Dispute Over Millions Is Partly Settled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schneiderman, Eric T', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mortgage-Backed Securities', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreclosures', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-21T00:52:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susanne Craig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Craig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec8c2a6b-1f4c-5e09-867e-b2fdbdddeab9', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec8c2a6b-1f4c-5e09-867e-b2fdbdddeab9'}, {'abstract': 'Mini issues team orders and stirs up a dispute, and Bryan Clauson takes a midget car win at Tulsa.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/automobiles/monday-motorsports-racers-unhappy-with-a-1-2-3-by-mini-finish-at-the-dakar-rally.html', 'snippet': 'Mini issues team orders and stirs up a dispute, and Bryan Clauson takes a midget car win at Tulsa.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nani Roma of Spain celebrated an anticlimactic and controversial victory in the two-week-long Dakar Rally, which ended Saturday near Valparaíso, Chile. Roma had taken the lead in the rally during the first week, but his teammate, Stephane Peterhansel, the 11-time Dakar winner and defending champion, gradually cut Roma’s lead from 39 minutes to just two minutes by Wednesday. Another teammate, Nassar Al-Attiyah, ran in third place.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/automobiles/21MOTORSPORTS-image/21MOTORSPORTS-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/21/automobiles/21MOTORSPORTS-image/21MOTORSPORTS-image-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/automobiles/21MOTORSPORTS-image/21MOTORSPORTS-image-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/automobiles/21MOTORSPORTS-image/21MOTORSPORTS-image-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/21/automobiles/21MOTORSPORTS-image/21MOTORSPORTS-image-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/21/automobiles/21MOTORSPORTS-image/21MO