 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Schwirtz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwirtz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be28ca20-786b-5985-81f9-44c3f0c77745', 'word_count': 1306, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be28ca20-786b-5985-81f9-44c3f0c77745'}, {'abstract': 'Sysco’s proposed acquisition of US Foods could lead to higher prices for restaurant meals and significantly reduce competition among suppliers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/opinion/a-potentially-harmful-merger.html', 'snippet': 'Sysco’s proposed acquisition of US Foods could lead to higher prices for restaurant meals and significantly reduce competition among suppliers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A proposed merger between two of the largest suppliers of food to restaurants, hotels and school cafeterias could significantly reduce competition and drive up prices of the meals Americans eat outside their homes. Of all the money consumers spend on food, nearly half is spent at restaurants. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Potentially Harmful Merger', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Potentially Harmful Merger', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'US Foods Inc', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sysco Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Trade Commission', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-21T02:03:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5e2c6e6-89c3-5c57-b534-7cadf215f024', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5e2c6e6-89c3-5c57-b534-7cadf215f024'}, {'abstract': 'One family’s trauma offers valuable lessons on how to comfort those in pain.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/opinion/brooks-the-art-of-presence.html', 'snippet': 'One family’s trauma offers valuable lessons on how to comfort those in pain.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tragedy has twice visited the Woodiwiss family. In 2008, Anna Woodiwiss, then 27, was working for a service organization in Afghanistan. On April 1, she went horseback riding and was thrown, dying from her injuries. In 2013, her younger sister Catherine, then 26, was biking to work from her home in Washington. She was hit by a car and her face was severely smashed up. She has endured and will continue to endure a series of operations. For a time, she breathed and ate through a tube, unable to speak. The recovery is slow. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/09/16/opinion/Brooks_New/Brooks_New-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2010/09/16/opinion/Brooks_New/Brooks_New-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/09/16/opinion/Brooks_New/Brooks_New-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 350, 'su