seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-21T05:02:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89c486f7-63a9-5236-8ea6-db806147e40a', 'word_count': 189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89c486f7-63a9-5236-8ea6-db806147e40a'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/21/arts/television/whats-on-tuesday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13, 49) SALINGER (2013) With Paul Alexander’s book “Salinger” as his starting point, Shane Salerno layers biographical material with archival photographs, re-enactments and interviews with high school students, celebrities and even murderers to shed light on J. D. Salinger, above — from his World War II experiences and relationships with younger women to his decision to live in a fortress of privacy. Philip Seymour Hoffman, Edward Norton, John Cusack, Martin Sheen, Tom Wolfe and Gore Vidal offer perspective. Writing in The New York Times, A. O. Scott called the film, presented here by “American Masters,” “less a work of cinema than the byproduct of its own publicity campaign.” He added: “It does not so much explore the life and times of J. D. Salinger as run his memory and legacy through a spin cycle of hype. Salinger moved to the woods of New Hampshire partly to escape the intrusions and indignities of American celebrity culture. ‘Salinger’ is that culture’s revenge.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Tuesday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Tuesday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-21T05:17:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsect