 'A new song by Sun Kil Moon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new song by Sun Kil Moon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '“I Watched The Film The Song Remains The Same”', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Led Zeppelin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-22T21:26:24+0000', 'document_type': 'audio', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': '', '_id': 'nyt://audio/34ae0b85-faa4-5a2a-a293-a0cc3095476d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/34ae0b85-faa4-5a2a-a293-a0cc3095476d'}, {'abstract': 'A response to Michael Needham on populism and conservative policy ideas.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/22/the-tea-party-and-conservative-reform/', 'snippet': 'A response to Michael Needham on populism and conservative policy ideas.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My Sunday column hailed recent forays by Senators Mike Lee and Marco Rubio as evidence that finally, finally, the ideas that conservative policy reformers have been batting around for a few years may be getting some traction in actual Republican politics. Along the way, I briefly characterized the Tea Party-versus-establishment battle that’s dominated G.O.P. politics as having a “policy substrate,” but too often just being “about posturing and score-settling.” This was, of course, a simplification of a more complex story, and I appreciate the response from Mike Needham of Heritage Action\xa0— a figure, it’s fair to say, much involved in the grassroots-establishment fight\xa0— because it gives me an opportunity to expand and clarify a bit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Tea Party and Conservative Reform', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 