m/2014/01/23/world/europe/turkey-more-police-are-fired-or-reassigned.html', 'snippet': 'Nearly 500 police officers were either fired or reassigned Wednesday in what appears to be a purge related to a graft inquiry that has already ensnared many of the prime minister’s allies.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Nearly 500 police officers were either fired or reassigned on Wednesday, local news outlets said, part of what appears to be a purge related to a graft inquiry that has already ensnared many of the prime minister’s allies. News reports said at least some of the reassigned officers took part in the large corruption investigation. Government critics fear the continuing changes in personnel — hundreds of police officers have already been purged — are meant to derail the investigation. Government officials insist, however, that the changes are legitimate efforts to eliminate what they call a “parallel state.” That refers to those in the judiciary and the police force loyal to a rival of Prime Minister Recep Tayyip Erdogan. Wednesday’s reshuffle followed the relocation on Tuesday of nearly 100 judges and prosecutors, a move made during Mr. Erdogan’s first visit in five years to Brussels, where he confronted European Union officials’ concerns about the separation of powers and a free judiciary in Turkey. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey: More Police Are Fired or Reassigned', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Turkey: More Police Are Fired or Reassigned', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arsu, Sebnem', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T03:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sebnem Arsu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sebnem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arsu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/91f1dd6c-5f3d-56c1-bbd4-5660184facf2', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91f1dd6c-5f3d-56c1-bbd4-5660184facf2'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Hawkins gets in his own way.', 'web_url': 'https://wordplay.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/22/if-you-want-to-be-loved-be-lovable/', 'snippet': 'Michael Hawkins gets in his own way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Administrivial Alert!In today’s (Thursday, Jan. 23, 2014) puzzle, there are elements in the print version that cannot be replicated electronically. We recommend using the PDF for the best solving experience. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'If You Want to Be Loved, Be Lovable', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crossword Puzzles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T03:00:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Deb Amlen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Deb', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Amlen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a7efc5b-35eb-507a-9022-067f2aa325d1', 'word_count': 924, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a7efc5b-35eb-507a-9022-067f2aa325d1'}, {'abstract': 'The family of George J. Stinney Jr., who was put to death at 14 for the murder of two white girls, is seeking exoneration in South Carolina, a state where racial matters still simmer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/us/family-of-s-carolina-boy-put-to-death-seeks-exoneration-70-years-later.html', 'snippet': 'The family of George J. Stinney Jr., who was put to death at 14 for the murder of two white girls, is seeking exoneration in South Carolina, a state where racial matters still simmer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SUMTER, S.C. — After South Carolina electrocuted George J. Stinney Jr. in 1944, his family buried his burned, 14-year-old body in an unmarked grave in the hopes the anonymity would allow him to rest in peace.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23EXECUTE2/23EXECUTE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 459, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23EXECUTE2/23EXECUTE2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 459}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23EXECUTE2/23EXECUTE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23EXECUTE2/23EXECUTE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1568, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23EXECUTE2/23EXECUTE2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23EXECUTE2/23EXECUTE2-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23EXECUTE2/23EXECUTE2-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Family of South Carolina Boy Put to Death Seeks Exoneration 70 Years Later', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Family of S. Carolina Boy Put to Death Seeks Exoneration 70 Years Later', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Carolina', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stinney, George J Jr', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mullen, Carmen T', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T03:02:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alan Blinder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Blinder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1a0e1ac-c145-556d-8a2c-d0e2431ec2b0', 'word_count': 903, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1a0e1ac-c145-556d-8a2c-d0e2431ec2b0'}, {'abstract': 'Iran reportedly hanged 40 people in the first two weeks of January, 33 of them in the last week, leading United Nations human rights experts to call for a halt to the executions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/world/middleeast/iran-surge-in-executions-worries-rights-experts.html', 'snippet': 'Iran reportedly hanged 40 people in the first two weeks of January, 33 of them in the last week, leading United Nations human rights experts to call for a halt to the executions.', 'lead_paragraph': ' United Nations human rights experts called on Wednesday for an urgent halt to a surge in executions in Iran in recent weeks, observing that the more moderate tone adopted on human rights since President Hassan Rouhani’s election last year has yet to yield any moderation in the country’s punitive practices. Iran reportedly hanged 40 people in the first two weeks of January, 33 of them in the last week, Ahmed Shaheed, the United Nations expert monitoring human rights in Iran, and Christof Heyns, the organization’s expert dealing with summary executions, said in a joint statement released in Geneva. The hangings carried out by Iran since the start of the year follow the execution of 625 people, including 28 women, and a number of political detainees in 2013, an increase of more than 100 over the number of recorded executions the previous year, Mr. Shaheed said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iran: Surge in Executions Worries Rights Experts', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iran: Surge in Executions Worries Rights Experts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rouhani, Hassan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T03:05:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Cumming-Bruce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cumming-Bruce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/688f35e6-ba6d-54cb-8c86-74320a893f69', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/688f35e6-ba6d-54cb-8c86-74320a893f69'}, {'abstract': 'Local security forces raided the kidnappers’ hide-out in the Libyan capital of Tripoli, the South Korean Foreign Ministry said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/world/asia/south-korea-kidnapped-trade-official-is-rescued.html', 'snippet': 'Local security forces raided the kidnappers’ hide-out in the Libyan capital of Tripoli, the South Korean Foreign Ministry said.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A South Korean trade official kidnapped by gunmen in the Libyan capital of Tripoli was rescued\xa0on Wednesday\xa0by local security forces, who raided the kidnappers’ hide-out, the South Korean Foreign Ministry said on Thursday. The official, Han Seok-woo, the head of the Tripoli office of the government-financed Korea Trade-Investment Promotion Agency, suffered no injuries during the rescue operation, the ministry said in a statement. “The four kidnappers were arrested by the Libyan security officials,” the statement added. Mr. Han, 39, was on his way home from work on Sunday\xa0afternoon when four gunmen stopped his car. South Korea then issued a warning against traveling to Libya and asked the 551 South Korean nationals there to leave.\xa0 ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'South Korea: Kidnapped Trade Official Is Rescued', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Asia', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'South Korea: Kidnapped Trade Official Is Rescued', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Han Seok-woo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tripoli (Libya)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T03:12:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Choe Sang-Hun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Choe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sang-Hun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/254875d1-40e2-5e8f-8aa1-2d469ed1ee60', 'word_count': 114, 'uri': 'nyt://article/254875d1-40e2-5e8f-8aa1-2d469ed1ee60'}, {'abstract': 'No Virginia laws bar elected officials from taking gifts, nor are there restrictions on what gifts family members of officeholders can take.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/us/politics/with-virginia-way-state-thought-it-didnt-need-rules.html', 'snippet': 'No Virginia laws bar elected officials from taking gifts, nor are there restrictions on what gifts family members of officeholders can take.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The gift-giving scandal that federal prosecutors described in evocative detail in their indictment on corruption charges Tuesday of former Gov. Bob McDonnell of Virginia and his wife, Maureen, would be ample evidence of a possible crime in some states.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23GIFTS/23GIFTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23GIFTS/23GIFTS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/23GIFTS/23GIFTS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'j