 through 2009. After Matsui came Kei Igawa, a left-hander who was unable to pitch effectively in the major leagues.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/sports/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 467, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/23/sports/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 467}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/sports/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/sports/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1350, 'width': 1736, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/sports/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/23/sports/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/sports/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1/CITY-YANKEES-GRAPHIC-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Japanese Thread in the Bronx', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Japanese Thread in the Bronx', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanaka, Masahiro', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T05:56:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64adeec4-9d08-5355-9d86-5a58cd6d5c17', 'word_count': 432, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64adeec4-9d08-5355-9d86-5a58cd6d5c17'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/arts/television/whats-on-thursday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (Discovery) THE FIGHTERS In the gritty neighborhood of South Boston, boxing has a rich heritage as not merely a pastime but a way of life. But the sport’s popularity has declined over the past 20 years, and Peter Welch, the gym owner and former fighter above, is determined to bring it back. In this new series, Mr. Welch works with trainers from rival boxing gyms across the city, who will choose their top amateur fighters to represent them in bouts. After seven days of intensive training, the fighters will take their professional futures — and their reputations — to the ring.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Thursday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Thursday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T06:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', '