': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/3e8ec7b1-c146-55d1-90a2-7f1339b772a9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/3e8ec7b1-c146-55d1-90a2-7f1339b772a9'}, {'abstract': 'This episode of CreatureCast presents two organisms that use cilia for locomotion. One is small and unicellular, the other is a much larger animal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/science/earth/moving-without-feet-to-do-so.html', 'snippet': 'This episode of CreatureCast presents two organisms that use cilia for locomotion. One is small and unicellular, the other is a much larger animal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cilia are hairlike structures that project from cells. They can move in coordinated, rhythmic waves that sweep fluid across the cell surface. If cells are fixed in place, this creates a current of fluid; the cilia that line our respiratory tract remove mucus and debris in this way. If the cilia are on the outside of the organism, the current they create can move the entire organism. This episode of CreatureCast presents two organisms that use cilia for locomotion. One is small and unicellular, the other is a much larger animal.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/science/23creaturecast-cilia/23creaturecast-cilia-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 499, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/23/science/23creaturecast-cilia/23creaturecast-cilia-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 499}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/science/23creaturecast-cilia/23creaturecast-cilia-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 852, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images