the new venture of the departing Washington Post analyst Ezra Klein, Slate’s editor, David Plotz, said Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Enlistee to Ezra Klein’s Journalism Venture', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Vox Media Inc', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Washington Post', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klein, Ezra', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yglesias, Matthew', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T21:19:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ravi Somaiya', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ravi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Somaiya', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01da377a-7352-55d2-aa21-f0a605ee49ec', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01da377a-7352-55d2-aa21-f0a605ee49ec'}, {'abstract': 'Would a reformist G.O.P. just end up repeating the profligacy of the Bush era?', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/deficits-and-conservative-reform/', 'snippet': 'Would a reformist G.O.P. just end up repeating the profligacy of the Bush era?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Turning from Mike Needham’s critique to the other end of the political spectrum, I see that Jonathan Chait used my column on reform conservatism to take a break from his usual pattern of explaining why the Republican Party is necessarily a tool of plutocratic interests, now and forever, world without end, and to allow that yes, maybe the next Republican president might do something that might conceivably benefit someone other than the super-rich … but that this would only happen in a replay of the George W. Bush administration, where Republicans learned to stop worrying and love deficit spending once again:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Deficits and Conservative Reform', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T21:19:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e7e54d5-fc96-5737-9de9-ba9813fc8cdd', 'word_count': 900, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e7e54d5-fc96-5737-9de9-ba9813fc8cdd'}, {'abstract': 'Nick Cave, the subject of the documentary “20,000 Days on Earth,” speaks with Melena Ryzik at the Sundance Film Festival.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/video-nick-cave-at-sundance/', 'snippet': 'Nick Cave, the subject of the documentary “20,000 Days on Earth,” speaks with Melena Ryzik at the Sundance Film Festival.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah — Sundance is as much a home for musical performances as movies. At this year’s festival there have been buzzed-about shows by Rick Ross; Damon Albarn of Blur and Gorillaz; Stuart Murdoch, of Belle & Sebastian, who made his directorial debut here with “God Help the Girl,” a musical about the indie scene in Glasgow; and Je