 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/nyregion/26HLI1_SPAN/26HLI1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/nyregion/26HLI1_SPAN/26HLI1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Events on Long Island for Jan. 26 to Feb. 1', 'kicker': 'Calendar', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Island', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T23:48:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/14e15410-7739-51b0-b47b-5493b60202cb', 'word_count': 3313, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14e15410-7739-51b0-b47b-5493b60202cb'}, {'abstract': 'Here’s a guide to some of the search giant’s products that could help travelers, from Glass to Voice Search to Gmail Offline.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/travel/google-tools-for-globetrotters.html', 'snippet': 'Here’s a guide to some of the search giant’s products that could help travelers, from Glass to Voice Search to Gmail Offline.', 'lead_paragraph': '“O.K., Glass,” I said, peering at a photo of a Spanish street sign through Google Glass, the lens-less augmented-reality “glasses.” “Translate this.” I gently tapped, then swiped my pointer finger along a narrow touch pad on the side of the Glass frame as if scrolling through the contact list on my iPhone — only I was scrolling through the names of romance languages, each of which appeared on a tiny virtual screen in the distance, in line with my right eye. When the word “Spanish” materialized, I tapped the side of the glasses to select it, a process you need do only once to lock in a language for an entire trip. Seconds later, the street sign was in English: “Attention, area of construction. No trespassing.”', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/travel/26GETAWAY_SPAN/26JPGETAWAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 337, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/travel/26GETAWAY_SPAN/26JPGETAWAY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 337}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/travel/26GETAWAY_SPAN/26JPGETAWAY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/travel/26GETAWAY_SPAN/26JPGETAWAY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1079, 'width': 1919, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/travel/26GETAWAY_SPAN/26GETAWAY_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/travel/26GETAWAY_SPAN/26GETAWAY_SPAN-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/travel/26GETAWAY_SPAN/26GETAWAY_SPAN-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Google Tools for Globetrotters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Google Tools for Globetrotters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wearable Computing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rosenbloom, Stephanie', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Glass', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Now', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T23:57:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephanie Rosenbloom', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephanie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenbloom', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0ed8e48-fecd-5177-9766-a5ae8a0392a4', 'word_count': 1577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0ed8e48-fecd-5177-9766-a5ae8a0392a4'}, {'abstract': 'Fred W. McDarrah, who for decades was The Village Voice’s only staff photographer, had access to his subjects that photographers could only dream of today.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/nyregion/photographing-new-york-of-the-1960s-and-70s.html', 'snippet': 'Fred W. McDarrah, who for decades was The Village Voice’s only staff photographer, had access to his subjects that photographers could only dream of today.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If we had known that the scrawny guy onstage at that Greenwich Village folk club might someday amount to something, we’d have all been there, camera in hand, to document the event. But we didn’t, so we weren’t. Fred W. McDarrah, on the other hand, made it a point to be there: when Bob Dylan first performed in New York; when Jack Kerouac and Allen Ginsberg held court at house parties in Greenwich Village, or Andy Warhol at his studio, the Factory; when the hippies or the gay liberation movement found their voices; when downtown New York was the incubator for the cultural movements that shook the world. Mr. McDarrah, who died in 2007, was the first photo editor and for decades the only staff photographer of The Village Voice. He had a front-row seat for multiple revolutions, with access that photographers today cannot imagine. “You could be standing three feet from Jimi Hendrix at a cocktail party and start shooting,” said Mr. McDarrah’s son Patrick, 47. “You could never just do that now with Jay Z.” Back then, said Mr. McDarrah’s other son, Timothy, 51, “We’d answer the phone and it would be, ‘Hi, it’s Andy, is Freddie there?’\xa0”', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Capturing the New York of the 1960s and ’70s', 'kicker': 'Album', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Being There', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDarrah, Fred W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Greenwich Village (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chelsea (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Village Voice', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kasher, Steven, Gallery', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-23T23:59:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Leland', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leland', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/612b5683-8062-56e2-9d8e-689e480dfaee', 'word_count': 332, 'uri': 'nyt://article/612b5683-8062-56e2-9d8e-689e480dfaee'}, {'abstract': 'Doctrinal revision needn’t contradict his commitment to the “value of the human person.”', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/should-pope-francis-rethink-abortion/', 'snippet': 'Doctrinal revision needn’t contradict his commitment to the “value of the human person.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Pope Francis has raised expectations of a turn away from the dogmatic intransigence that has long cast a pall over the religious life of many Roman Catholics. His question “Who am I to judge?” suggested a new attitude toward homosexuality, and he is apparently willing to consider allowing the use of contraceptives to prevent sexually transmitted diseases. But his position on what has come to be the hierarchy’s signature issue — abortion — seems unyielding. “Reason alone is sufficient to recognize the inviolable value of each single human life,” he declared in his recent apostolic exhortation, “Evangelii Gaudium,” adding: “Precisely because this involves the internal consistency of our message about the value of the human person, the church cannot be expected to change her position on this question.”I want to explore the possibility, however, that the pope might be open to significant revision of the absolute ban on abortion by asking what happens if we take seriously his claim that “reason alone is sufficient” to adjudicate this issue. What actually follows regarding abortion once we accept the “inviolable value of each single human life”? This appeal to rational reflection has been a central feature of the tradition of Catholic moral teaching. I put forward the following reflections in the spirit of this tradition.There is considerable rational basis for moral concern about abortions. In many (probably most) cases, it would be immoral to abort a pregnancy. (Note, however, that this by no means implies that most abortions actually performed are immoral.) Late-term fetuses, for example, are no different biologically or psychologically from babies born prematurely at the same stage of development. It’s hard to see how killing a premature baby is immoral but killing an identical late-term fetus isn’t. At a minimum, aborting a healthy late-term fetus would, except when the mother’s life is at risk, be immoral — which is no doubt why it is seldom, if ever, done.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Should Pope Francis Rethink Abortion?', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ABORTION', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Francis', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T00:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Gutting', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gutting', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b07f8470-b331-5171-9d57-751e1aee8f8f', 'word_count': 1060, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b07f8470-b331-5171-9d57-751e1aee8f8f'}, {'abstract': 'Every mean girl needs a gay male best friend; at least that’s what the teenagers in “G.B.F.” believe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/movies/in-gbf-being-outed-by-accident-is-a-ticket-to-status.html', 'snippet': 'Every mean girl needs a gay male best friend; at least that’s what the teenagers in “G.B.F.” believe.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Never in the history of North Gateway High had any student admitted out loud to being an honest-to-goodness, proud or even slightly modest homosexual, including me and my best friend,” announces Tanner (Michael J. Willett), the gay teenager who narrates the satirical farce “G.B.F.” Tanner and his best friend, Brent (Paul Iacono), are classmates in an upscale suburban hotbed of adolescent social warfare that might as well be on Planet Q, for all its resemblance to an actual high school.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Teenage Snake Pit, a Chance to Be Trendy', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Teenage Snake Pit, a Chance to Be Trendy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stein, Darren', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mullally, Megan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'G.B.F. (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Iacono, Paul S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willett, Michael J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T00:03:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/47c1e7a2-ac29-56ba-a33b-7b60a0c61048', 'word_count': 475, 'uri': 'nyt://article/47c1e7a2-ac29-56ba-a33b-7b60a0c61048'}, {'abstract': 'A look back at the best — and more bizarre — happenings and offerings during this year’s film extravaganza.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/23/sundance-diary-the-sun-sets-early-on-park-city/', 'snippet': 'A look back at the best — and more bizarre — happenings and offerings during this year’s film extravaganza.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 2014 Sundance Film Festival may not be over yet, but with most of its high-profile premieres wrapped up and a large portion of the celebrity contingent already back in their L.A. and New York enclaves, Park City, Utah, certainly has the feel of a circus ready to leave town. The festival, which officially concludes on Sunday, had a strong lineup this year and featured a total of 118 feature-length films from 37 different countries and 54 first-time filmmakers. And while film sales have been relatively quiet — the reported $3 million that Sony Pictures Classics paid for the opening-night film, “Whiplash,” pales in comparison to the $9.75 million that Fox Searchlight shelled out for “The Way, Way Back” last year — the creative output has been promising.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/t-magazine/23sundance-oloizia1/23sundance-oloizia1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/23/t-magazine/23sundance-oloizia1/23sundance-oloizia1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/t-magazine/23sundance-oloizia1/23sundance-oloizia1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/t-magazine/23sundance-oloizia1/23sundance-oloizia1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1340, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/t-magazine/23sundance-oloizia1/23sundance-oloizia1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/23/t-magazine/23sundance-oloizia1/23sundance-oloizia1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/t-magazine/23sundance-oloizia1/23sundance-oloizia1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Diary | The Sun Sets Early on Park City', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Park City (Utah)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T00:06:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Oloizia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oloizia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a73c4ae-6928-5f42-b3f0-a879522223d0', 'word_count': 788, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a73c4ae-6928-5f42-b3f0-a879522223d0'}, {'abstract': 'Don Pedro is all about alcohol, football on the TVs, video games and Mexican food until midnight on Wednesdays, when glamour, anarchy and subversion reign.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/nyregion/an-off-the-wall-experience-at-don-pedro.html', 'snippet': 'Don Pedro is all about alcohol, football on the TVs, video games and Mexican food until midnight on Wednesdays, when glamour, anarchy and subversion reign.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By 11 p.m. on a recent Wednesday, it looked as if Don Pedro was pretty much done for the night. A group of maybe a dozen people were scattered around the place, watching the credits roll on “American Horror Story.” The bartender took the opportunity to head outside for a smoke. Sitting at a table with drinks and a couple of friends was Joe Kennedy, 24, who moved to New York five months ago from a college town in Missouri. A friend recommended that he check out the place (90 Manhattan Avenue in Brooklyn; there was some debate at the table whether this was East Williamsburg or Bushwick). Mr. Kennedy had been assured that Wednesdays at Don Pedro were “off the wall.” ', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An ‘Off the Wall’ Experience at Don Pedro', 'kicker': 'Neighborhood Joint', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘A Snow Globe of Glitter and Trash’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bars and Nightclubs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Williamsburg (Brooklyn, NY)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Don Pedro (Brooklyn, NY, Restaurant)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T00:09:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ilise Carter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ilise', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4276149-8505-5f90-9a35-994e5e00e391', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4276149-8505-5f90-9a35-994e5e00e391'}, {'abstract': 'A guide to stand-up, improv and comedy shows in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/arts/comedy-listings-for-jan-24-30.html', 'snippet': 'A guide to stand-up, improv and comedy shows in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For news about comedy and humor, including critical commentary: nytimes.com/arts. A searchable guide to these and other shows is at nytimes.com/events.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comedy Listings for Jan. 24-30', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Listings: Comedy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T00:12:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/87f5a243-b4b7-50cd-a01e-3030e61a134f', 'word_count': 573, 'uri': 'nyt://article/87f5a243-b4b7-50cd-a01e-3030e61a134f'}, {'abstract': '“Gimme Shelter,” written and directed by Ronald Krauss and based on a true story, follows Apple, a teenager who has a drug-addicted mother and a father who’s not in the picture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/movies/in-gimme-shelter-a-priest-reaches-out-to-smooth-a-path.html', 'snippet': '“Gimme Shelter,” written and directed by Ronald Krauss and based on a true story, follows Apple, a teenager who has a drug-addicted mother and a father who’s not in the picture.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Gimme Shelter,” written and directed by Ronald Krauss, begins with the familiar “based on a true story” note and ends with side-by-side photographs of actors from the movie and some of the real-life characters they portray. What comes in between is a crude, earnest parable that uses some of the techniques of indie filmmaking to deliver a culturally conservative message. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia'