e': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4898e94a-dd31-5457-920a-b25055e8930a', 'word_count': 1115, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4898e94a-dd31-5457-920a-b25055e8930a'}, {'abstract': 'Manufacturers are designing cigarettes to deliver more nicotine to the lungs. That devious tactic requires a strong response by regulators.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/opinion/even-more-addictive-cigarettes.html', 'snippet': 'Manufacturers are designing cigarettes to deliver more nicotine to the lungs. That devious tactic requires a strong response by regulators.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was a shock to learn from the latest surgeon general’s report that, because of changes in the design and composition of cigarettes, smokers today face a higher risk of lung cancer and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease than smokers in 1964, despite smoking fewer cigarettes. It is equally shocking to learn now that some of today’s cigarettes may be more addictive than those smoked in past years, most likely because the manufacturers are designing them to deliver more nicotine to the lungs to induce and sustain addiction. That devious tactic requires a strong response by regulators.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Even More Addictive Cigarettes', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Even More Addictive Cigarettes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smoking and Tobacco', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nicotine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgeon General (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Addiction (Psychology)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Massachusetts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Health Department (Mass)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nicotine and Tobacco Research (Journal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T02:01:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/abfba75d-1f4c-5f25-bc54-ce66e7498770', 'word_count': 447, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abfba75d-1f4c-5f25-bc54-ce66e7498770'}, {'abstract': 'Mayor Bill de Blasio swiftly delivers on a campaign promise to expand paid time off for sick workers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/opinion/quick-work-on-paid-sick-leave.html', 'snippet': 'Mayor Bill de Blasio swiftly delivers on a campaign promise to expand paid time off for sick workers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mayor Bill de Blasio, swiftly delivering on a campaign promise, joined the City Council speaker, Melissa Mark-Viverito, last week in offering a bill to revise New York City’s law on paid sick leave. Their bill is broader than one that passed last year after much delay and political spatting. It’s also better.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quick Work on Paid Sick Leave', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quick Work on Paid Sick Leave', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Blasio, Bill', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mark-Viverito, Melissa', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paid Time Off', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'City Council (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T02:05:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/7574edb5-f1a9-5e4d-b73e-86dfc667ce4d', 'word_count': 309, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7574edb5-f1a9-5e4d-b73e-86dfc667ce4d'}, {'abstract': 'Why continue a program that invades privacy, violates the law, and doesn’t even work?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/opinion/end-the-phone-data-sweeps.html', 'snippet': 'Why continue a program that invades privacy, violates the law, and doesn’t even work?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once again, a thorough and independent analysis of the government’s dragnet surveillance of Americans’ phone records has found the bulk data collection to be illegal and probably unconstitutional. Just as troubling, the program was found to be virtually useless at stopping terrorism, raising the obvious question: Why does President Obama insist on continuing a costly, legally dubious program when his own appointees repeatedly find that it doesn’t work?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'End the Phone Data Sweeps', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'End the Phone Data Sweeps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surveillance of Citizens by Government', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Privacy and Civil Liberties Oversight Board', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leon, Richard J', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Security Agency', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T02:08:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editorial Board', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editorial Board'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/ae1ccd18-ea81-541b-a2c9-a3236231ea16', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ae1ccd18-ea81-541b-a2c9-a3236231ea16'}, {'abstract': 'A real national social mobility strategy must address youth development from ages 0 to 25.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/opinion/brooks-it-takes-a-generation.html', 'snippet': 'A real national social mobility strategy must address youth development from ages 0 to 25.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the past decade we’ve had a rich debate on how to expand opportunity for underprivileged children. But we’ve probably made two mistakes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/09/16/opinion/Brooks_New/Brooks_New-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2010/09/16/opinion/Brooks_New/Brooks_New-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/09/16/opinion/Brooks_New/Brooks_New-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 350, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'ran