on': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b0f0446-6c6c-5012-a9f0-6c30f8e724de', 'word_count': 25, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b0f0446-6c6c-5012-a9f0-6c30f8e724de'}, {'abstract': 'How is the legalization of same-sex marriage in many states affecting Roman Catholic schools across the nation?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/6-qs-about-the-news-gay-marriages-confront-catholic-school-rules/', 'snippet': 'How is the legalization of same-sex marriage in many states affecting Roman Catholic schools across the nation?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Gay Marriages Confront Catholic School Rules,” Michael Paulson writes about a wave of firings and forced resignations of gay men and lesbians from Roman Catholic institutions across the country.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/Seattle-LN/Seattle-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/23/us/Seattle-LN/Seattle-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/Seattle-LN/Seattle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 540, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/Seattle-LN/Seattle-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/Seattle-LN/Seattle-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/23/us/Seattle-LN/Seattle-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/23/us/Seattle-LN/Seattle-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '6 Q’s About the News | Gay Marriages Confront Catholic School Rules', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T09:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/30e86c90-8a30-586a-8016-cbe395515a7d', 'word_count': 129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30e86c90-8a30-586a-8016-cbe395515a7d'}, {'abstract': 'The gravitational pull of a possible 2016 campaign is bringing all the old Clinton characters into her orbit. Can she make the stars align, or will chaos prevail?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/magazine/hillary-clinton.html', 'snippet': 'The gravitational pull of a possible 2016 campaign is bringing all the old Clinton characters into her orbit. Can she make the stars align, or will chaos prevail?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hillary Clinton was nodding solemnly to the mother of a 9/11 victim when Huma Abedin, standing across the room, called out, “Let’s load!” to the staff members and bodyguards. The former secretary of state had yet to pick up her award from the Voices of September 11th, but her entourage was already preparing to shuttle her off to the next event, a benefit for God’s Love We Deliver, which was co-hosted by the designer Michael Kors and where she would sit next to the Vogue editor and former Obama bundler Anna Wintour. It was just another hectic fall evening in Manhattan for Clinton, and she was keeping herself busy as usual in the “is she or isn’t she” interim. There were paid speeches to give (at $200,000 a pop) to the American Society of Travel Agents and the National Association of Realtors, filled with the wisdom gleaned from being the nation’s top diplomat (“leadership is a team sport” was one favorite; “you can’t win if you don’t show up” was another). There were awards to receive, like the one from Chatham House, a think tank in London, to which Clinton traveled on a commercial 757 like the one she used to command while working at the State Department. (“That was fun!” she said of the flight.) And there were Beverly Hills galas to attend, which soon turned into schmooze sessions, like the ones with Harvey Weinstein and Richard Plepler and Jeffrey Katzenberg, yet another major Obama bundler.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26cover_sometext/26cover_sometext-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26cover_sometext/26cover_sometext-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26cover_sometext/26cover_sometext-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26cover_sometext/26cover_sometext-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1683, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26cover_sometext/26cover_sometext-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26cover_sometext/26cover_sometext-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26cover_sometext/26cover_sometext-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Planet Hillary', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Madam Secretary of the Universe', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Hillary Rodham', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T10:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amy Chozick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chozick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e2cad1c0-6af1-5acd-85ab-f527bd057b9f', 'word_count': 5644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e2cad1c0-6af1-5acd-85ab-f527bd057b9f'}, {'abstract': 'Should the right to free speech trump our desire to end the use of hateful words?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/do-laws-that-ban-offensive-words-make-the-world-a-better-place/', 'snippet': 'Should the right to free speech trump our desire to end the use of hateful words?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Israeli politicians are considering a ban on the word “Nazi” in any setting except for educational purposes. Backers of the law say they want to combat everything from rising anti-Semitism to teenage trash talk. But should the right to free speech trump our desire to end the use of hateful words? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Do Laws That Ban Offensive Words Make the World a Better Place?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T10:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d42de72-4196-5ae1-8937-5ede89d25b9c', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d42de72-4196-5ae1-8937-5ede89d25b9c'}, {'abstract': 'Operation Condor, the secret plan carried out by several South American dictatorships to eliminate left-wing opponents, killed as many as 60,000 people. The photographer João Pina has spent a decade unearthing its mysteries.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/exposing-the-legacy-of-operation-condor/', 'snippet': 'Operation Condor, the secret plan carried out by several South American dictatorships to eliminate left-wing opponents, killed as many as 60,000 people. The photographer João Pina has spent a decade unearthing its mysteries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1975, six South American military dictatorships conspired to concoct a secret plan to eliminate their left-wing opponents. Not only would the intelligence services of Argentina, Bolivia, Brazil, Chile, Paraguay and Uruguay trade information with each other and kidnap, disappear and kill their own domestic foes, they would also cooperate in identifying and killing exiles from partner countries who had taken refuge elsewhere.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/22/blogs/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 602, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/22/blogs/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 602}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/22/blogs/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1021, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/22/blogs/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2006, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/22/blogs/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/22/blogs/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/22/blogs/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J/20140122-lens-pina-slide-D64J-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Exposing the Legacy of Operation Condor', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None