ng) the competition.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You became the world chess champion after a win in November. How did you go about training for the big match? The most important thing to study is opening theory, but I also study the latest trends to try to stay ahead, discovering new schemes and doing tactical exercises. I try to get into a chess mood.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1574, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1574}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 391, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 781, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/magazine/26talk/26talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Magnus Carlsen, Chess Champion: ‘My Moves Speak for Themselves’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘My Moves Speak for Themselves’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chess', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Norway', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carlsen, Magnus', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Susan Dominus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Susan', 'lastname': 'Dominus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b7a3037-58bf-568b-8351-d9eb3da5a8c2', 'word_count': 686, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b7a3037-58bf-568b-8351-d9eb3da5a8c2'}, {'abstract': 'To mark the conclusion of the Paris Couture collections, we catalog the bows — some proud, some playful, some humble, some all of the above — of 11 fashion visionaries.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/curtain-call-the-designers-of-paris-couture-collections-take-their-bows/', 'snippet': 'To mark the conclusion of the Paris Couture collections, we catalog the bows — some proud, some playful, some humble, some all of the above — of 11 fashion visionaries.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Designers project their personalities not only through their collections but also in their appearances at the end of their runway shows. To mark the conclusion of the Spring 2014 Paris Couture Collections, we catalog the bows — some proud, some playful, some humble, some all of the above — of 11 fashion visionaries.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Curtain Call | The Designers of Paris Couture Collections Take Their Bows', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Couture (Fashion)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By don Ashby', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ebf1f305-8552-5ba0-9b90-0dd4ff6e71af', 'word_count': 59, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ebf1f305-8552-5ba0-9b90-0dd4ff6e71af'}, {'abstract': 'How a sleepy New York State town armed the Union.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/the-guns-of-the-hudson/', 'snippet': 'How a sleepy New York State town armed the Union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The sleepy Hudson River Valley community of Cold Spring, N.Y., is arguably one of the most idyllic villages in the state. Sitting on the east bank of the Hudson, it offers spectacular views of the Highlands across the river, including the United States Military Academy at West Point. Its art galleries, antique shops, and fine restaurants make for a delightful weekend getaway for residents of New York City, an hour to the south. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Guns of the Hudson', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Military Academy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cold Spring (NY)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ron Soodalter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ron', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Soodalter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abf495fe-6ddb-50f4-b34b-621c1dd06ede', 'word_count': 1797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abf495fe-6ddb-50f4-b34b-621c1dd06ede'}, {'abstract': 'A look back at the books that made it to the big screen.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/the-year-in-adaptation.html', 'snippet': 'A look back at the books that made it to the big screen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cinematic book adaptations dominated 2012, with “Life of Pi,” “Silver Linings Playbook” and “Anna Karenina” springing from novels, and “Lincoln” and “Argo” drawing their stories from the nonfiction shelves. There was plenty of bookish inspiration in 2013, but the results elicited less enthusiasm. Martin Scorsese’s Oscar contender, “The Wolf of Wall Street,” was adapted from a memoir by Jordan Belfort. (The book begins: “You’re lower than pond scum.”) The movie’s leading man, Leonardo DiCaprio, also played the most famous literary character brought to the screen in 2013: Jay Gatsby. In The New Republic, David Thomson wrote his review in the form of a letter from Jay himself, who judged: “It’s stupefying, it’s vulgar, it’s demeaning.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26openbook/26openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 265, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26openbook/26openbook-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 265}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26openbook/26openbook-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26openbook/26openbook-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1020, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26openbook/26openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26openbook/26openbook-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26openbook/26openbook-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Year in Adaptation', 'kicker': 'Open Book', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Year in Adaptation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:01:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/969777ef-2378-5bbd-9ee2-8114b9033666', 'word_count': 438, 'uri': 'nyt://article/969777ef-2378-5bbd-9ee2-8114b9033666'}, {'abstract': 'With China’s rise and the Federal Reserve’s economic action both in retreat, investors have been heading for the exits in markets as far removed as Buenos Aires, Istanbul and Beijing, with effects spilling over into the rest of the world.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/a-worldwide-market-slump-gains-traction/', 'snippet': 'With China’s rise and the Federal Reserve’s economic action both in retreat, investors have been heading for the exits in markets as far removed as Buenos Aires, Istanbul and Beijing, with effects spilling over into the rest of the world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 9:20 p.m. | The ascent of developing countries over the last decade has been fueled by two global trends: the steady rise of China and the willingness of the Federal Reserve to stimulate the economy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/25/business/Markets/Markets-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/25/business/Markets/Markets-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/25/business/Markets/Markets-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/25/business/Markets/Markets-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/25/business/Markets/Markets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/25/business/Markets/Markets-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/25/business/Markets/Markets-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Economic Shifts in U.S. and China Batter Markets', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Economic Shifts in U.S. and China Batter Markets', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ARGENTINA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:01:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nathaniel Popper', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nathaniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Popper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7a7b7cf-fbfd-5b98-9015-1a47bb42235f', 'word_count': 1192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7a7b7cf-fbfd-5b98-9015-1a47bb42235f'}, {'abstract': 'Navigating the terrors of adulthood, the characters in Ben Marcus’s story collection resort to extreme survival strategies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/leaving-the-sea-stories-by-ben-marcus.html', 'snippet': 'Navigating the terrors of adulthood, the characters in Ben Marcus’s story collection resort to extreme survival strategies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ben Marcus achieved the startling visions of his first story collection, “The Age of Wire and String,” and the novels “Notable American Women” and “The Flame Alphabet” largely by placing the reader in strange and unfamiliar worlds that turned out to be our world after all. The stories of his new collection, “Leaving the Sea,” still contain peculiar linguistic and perceptual tics, but he has added to his arsenal narratives that are less relentlessly unfamiliar, less rigorously dis-enchanted, populated by characters full of longing and visible regret, though for what is not always clear. For fans of the old style, like me, there are still plenty of messed-up points of view, and we can be reassured that while most of the work in this collection begins on familiar ground, it happily disintegrates into confusion and dread. In the end Marcus presents a deeper range of identification for the reader, more emotional complexity, but still plenty of the chair-gripping alienation that marked his previous books.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26SUBKRUSOE/26SUBKRUSOE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 511, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26SUBKRUSOE/26SUBKRUSOE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 752, 'width': 511, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26SUBKRUSOE/26SUBKRUSOE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26SUBKRUSOE/26SUBKRUSOE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26SUBKRUSOE/26SUBKRUSOE-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'There Must Be Some Misunderstanding', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'There Must Be Some Misunderstanding', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marcus, Ben', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:03:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jim Krusoe', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krusoe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e90e34c-abcb-53e5-913b-66807f238444', 'word_count': 1129, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e90e34c-abcb-53e5-913b-66807f238444'}, {'abstract': 'The author remembers when looks started to matter in publishing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/writer-of-a-certain-age.html', 'snippet': 'The author remembers when looks started to matter in publishing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I started out as a television dramatist in the ’60s, it was a truism that the more fanciable the female lead, the higher the ratings. Casting was a simple matter of sitting by the director while he flipped through acting photo directories and selected his preference. Theater was no different. When I took to writing plays in the ’70s, it was understood that if two mature women were talking onstage, audience members would cough and shift in their seats, but if two men of any age were talking, the audience would pay some attention. And if a man of any age and a young woman were alone onstage, you could hear a pin drop. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Wendell/0126-bks-Wendell-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 254, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Wendell/0126-bks-Wendell-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 254}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Wendell/0126-bks-Wendell-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 433, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Wendell/0126-bks-Wendell-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 865, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Wendell/0126-bks-Wendell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Wendell/0126-bks-Wendell-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Wendell/0126-bks-Wendell-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Writer of a Certain Age', 'kicker': 'Author’s Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Writer of a Certain Age', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:04:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fay Weldon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weldon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba7910a7-6dfa-5073-93a6-1217ff9d9622', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba7910a7-6dfa-5073-93a6-1217ff9d9622'}, {'abstract': 'In this novel, a girl left with the Shakers begins to see angels.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/the-visionist-by-rachel-urquhart.html', 'snippet': 'In this novel, a girl left with the Shakers begins to see angels.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If, as the Shaker lyrics suggest, it truly is a gift to be simple, consider the emotional intricacies of Rachel Urquhart’s transfixing debut novel, “The Visionist.” Urquhart’s characters, many of them Shakers or Shakers-to-be, lead lives gifted with devastating complexity. Their paths resemble not the austere angles of a Shaker meetinghouse but rather the rapturous choreography of the dances Shakers were known for and named after.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26DERMONT/26DERMONT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1021, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26DERMONT/26DERMONT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1021}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26DERMONT/26DERMONT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 602, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26DERMONT/26DERMONT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1204, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26DERMONT/26DERMONT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26DERMONT/26DERMONT-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26DERMONT/26DERMONT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Possession', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Possession', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Urquhart, Rachel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:05:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amber Dermont', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amber', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dermont', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5ce97999-8625-5e1a-9365-2a2fe08d76d6', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5ce97999-8625-5e1a-9365-2a2fe08d76d6'}, {'abstract': 'Two women, white and black, strive for freedom in Sue Monk Kidd’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/the-invention-of-wings-by-sue-monk-kidd.html', 'snippet': 'Two women, white and black, strive for freedom in Sue Monk Kidd’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Unlikely alliances are a staple of fiction, and the unlikelier the better, from Huck and Jim floating down the Mississippi to Frodo and Gollum creeping toward Mordor — because the real drama lies in watching how dissimilar characters turn out to be brothers (or sisters) under the skin. Sue Monk Kidd followed this principle in her best-selling first novel, “The Secret Life of Bees,” in which a 14-year-old white girl and her family’s black servant join in fleeing abuse in the South Carolina of the civil rights era. Kidd’s latest novel, “The Invention of Wings,” also set largely in South Carolina, involves another unusual duo, in this case a slave and a daughter of the family that owns her.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Berne/0126-bks-Berne-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 509, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Berne/0126-bks-Berne-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 703, 'width': 509, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Berne/0126-bks-Berne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Berne/0126-bks-Berne-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-Berne/0126-bks-Berne-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking Flight', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking Flight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kidd, Sue Monk', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:06:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Suzanne Berne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Suzanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6053fa3f-4dd9-52a3-8fa4-f03239c39602', 'word_count': 891, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6053fa3f-4dd9-52a3-8fa4-f03239c39602'}, {'abstract': 'Valerie Martin’s novel explores the enduring enigma of a 19th-century ship found floating in the Atlantic without captain or crew.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/the-ghost-of-the-mary-celeste-by-valerie-martin.html', 'snippet': 'Valerie Martin’s novel explores the enduring enigma of a 19th-century ship found floating in the Atlantic without captain or crew.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Dec. 4, 1872, the brigantine Mary \xadCeleste was discovered abandoned, adrift in the North Atlantic near the Azores. The ship was empty and its crew missing, although its cargo and provisions were intact, as were the crew’s possessions. The weather was calm and the ship was seaworthy, but no trace of the crew — or of the captain’s wife and 2-year-old child — was ever found. Newspapers around the world carried the story, and it inspired one of Arthur Conan Doyle’s first published pieces of fiction, “J. Habakuk Jephson’s Statement,” printed anonymously in the British journal Cornhill. From then until the present, dozens of other investigations, novels, historical accounts, television documentaries and movies have been devoted to what has become the prototypical ghost ship. Given this background, what could Valerie Martin possibly contribute to the story in her new novel?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-VERNON/0126-bks-VERNON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 727, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-VERNON/0126-bks-VERNON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 727}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-VERNON/0126-bks-VERNON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 846, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-VERNON/0126-bks-VERNON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1720, 'width': 1420, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-VERNON/0126-bks-VERNON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-VERNON/0126-bks-VERNON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/0126-bks-VERNON/0126-