rsons', 'value': 'Hayasaki, Erika', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T16:09:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paige Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paige', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6397fe14-6910-534b-b098-b8cb0889a243', 'word_count': 1245, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6397fe14-6910-534b-b098-b8cb0889a243'}, {'abstract': 'A drama of revenge plays out in wintry upstate New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/books/review/the-kept-by-james-scott.html', 'snippet': 'A drama of revenge plays out in wintry upstate New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Winter and discontent. The future Richard III pairs those elements in the first line of Shakespeare’s play, and they are also paired, with piercing chill, in James Scott’s debut novel, “The Kept.” It is 1897. Elspeth Howell is making her way to an isolated cabin in upstate New York. Elspeth is pursued by cruel weather and her past as “a sinner,” a woman besieged by “anger, covetousness, thievery.” Unlike “false and treacherous” Richard, she believes she has conquered her worst impulses. She bears gifts for her five children and her husband, Jorah. She longs for the comforts of home. What she discovers, instead, is the worst kind of retribution — a slaughter of innocents triggered by her own compulsive history.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26HAGY/26HAGY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/books/review/26HAGY/26HAGY-articleLarg