 'snippet': 'The writer-director-stars of a film about a New Zealand “undead community” think vampires might be big.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARK CITY, Utah – Vampires have finally descended on Sundance. In its 30th year the festival may be, as Manohla Dargis writes more vital than ever to the indie film scene. But that doesn’t mean it’s immune to the lure of big thrill entertainment that the undead provide. This year’s festival had not one but three vampire flicks: the high-profile “Only Lovers Left Alive,” from Jim Jarmusch, starring Tilda Swinton and Tom Hiddleston; “Girl Walks Home Alone at Night,” billed as “cinema’s first Iranian vampire western;” and “What We Do in the Shadows,” from writer-director-stars Taika Waititi and Jemaine Clement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sundance Video:  Jemaine Clement and Taika Waititi Are Onto Something', 'kicker': 'Carpetbagger', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clement, Jermaine', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waititi, Taika', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T19:35:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melena Ryzik', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melena', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ryzik', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7bfa405a-d290-5c9f-b07c-f2032d0c95bb', 'word_count': 237, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7bfa405a-d290-5c9f-b07c-f2032d0c95bb'}, {'abstract': 'America’s love affair with British TV may have reached a peak with “Downton Abbey,” but viewers are also finding plenty of room in their hearts for “Doc Martin.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/arts/television/the-british-series-doc-martin-takes-off-in-us.html', 'snippet': 'America’s love affair with British TV may have reached a peak with “Downton Abbey,” but viewers are also finding plenty of room in their hearts for “Doc Martin.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'America’s love affair with British TV may have reached a peak with “Downton Abbey,” but viewers are also finding plenty of room in their hearts for the decidedly different “Doc Martin,” a decade-old series about a cantankerous doctor and the ensemble of quirky neighbors and patients he looks down on in the fictional Cornwall coastal village of Portwenn. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/arts/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/arts/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/arts/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/arts/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/arts/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN/26DOC-MARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/26/arts/26DOC-MARTIN_SPAN/26DOC-MARTIN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 't