ne, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-24T21:09:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Owles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Owles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/440217bb-3a84-5d7e-a62b-a9cc5a1bbd4e', 'word_count': 1575, 'uri': 'nyt://article/440217bb-3a84-5d7e-a62b-a9cc5a1bbd4e'}, {'abstract': 'The demise of the discount chain after 94 years shows the shift in the way people shop for designer clothing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/nyregion/closing-of-loehmanns-reflects-changes-in-shopping.html', 'snippet': 'The demise of the discount chain after 94 years shows the shift in the way people shop for designer clothing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whenever a store, bar, restaurant or other institution closes in New York after decades, there’s a tendency to mourn the loss of an entire way of life, a vanished style of inhabiting the city that can no longer flourish in the context of new economies with new people too young, too preoccupied, too cavalier to appreciate the old and intrinsically superior, more genuine folkways. It is hard to mine the same kind of resonance from the closing of Loehmann’s, now shutting down after 94 years, if only because the hunger for discount would seem to be eternal. And yet the end of Loehmann’s still says something about our altered expectations of the retail experience.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/26/nyregion/26BIGCITY_SPAN/26BIGCITY_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/26/nyregion/26