ae4d-9e28-5f13-a699-ad16250d668f', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55f7ae4d-9e28-5f13-a699-ad16250d668f'}, {'abstract': 'The multi-room production in Brooklyn uses dance and the music of Richard Wagner to recast the story of the braided lip-syncing duo as a classical tragedy.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/24/after-hours-an-opera-finds-pathos-in-the-milli-vanilli-saga/', 'snippet': 'The multi-room production in Brooklyn uses dance and the music of Richard Wagner to recast the story of the braided lip-syncing duo as a classical tragedy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The deconstructed, multiroom opera “Wow (Work in Progress)” at BRIC Arts in Brooklyn, about the late-’80s rise and fall of Milli Vanilli, isn’t the first to wring classical tragedy from modern-day cultural calamity — the composer Mark-Anthony Turnage did it with pieces about Anna Nicole Smith and Jerry Springer — but it might be the strangest. The creative trio of Joe Diebes, Christian Hawkey and David Levine cast the story of the braided European pop duo, who fell from grace when it was revealed that they’d been lip-syncing all along, as a postmodern tragedy about two handsome naïfs devoured by the pop-culture machine.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/24/t-magazine/24wow-murphy/24wow-murphy-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/24/t-magazine/24wow-murphy/24wow-murphy-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/24/t-magazine/24wow-murphy/24wow-murphy-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'im