 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7d31c5e3-c5af-5328-867d-6c5b284bc3d8', 'word_count': 813, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7d31c5e3-c5af-5328-867d-6c5b284bc3d8'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/26/arts/television/whats-on-today.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (CBS) THE 56TH ANNUAL GRAMMY AWARDS L L Cool J returns as host of this ceremony honoring artists in country, hip-hop, jazz, pop and rock, and broadcast live from the Staples Center in Los Angeles. Jay Z leads the pack with nine nominations; Kendrick Lamar, Macklemore (above) & Ryan Lewis, Justin Timberlake and Pharrell Williams follow with seven apiece; and Drake and the mastering engineer Bob Ludwig have five each. A “Grammy Moment” will feature Merle Haggard; Kris Kristofferson, a recipient of the 2014 Recording Academy Lifetime Achievement Award; Willie Nelson; and Blake Shelton. Billie Joe Armstrong and Miranda Lambert will perform a tribute to Phil Everly, who died on Jan. 3. Other sets will feature Paul McCartney and Ringo Starr, who will also receive lifetime achievement awards; Nile Rodgers, Mr. Williams and Stevie Wonder with Daft Punk; Pink and Nate Ruess; Metallica and Lang Lang; Madonna, Lorde, Katy Perry, John Legend, Kacey Musgraves, Taylor Swift and Keith Urban. Nine Inch Nails, Queens of the Stone Age, Dave Grohl and Lindsey Buckingham will rock the finale. Pre-event coverage begins at 4 on E!; TV Guide joins the red carpet at 5; and E! moves to the after-party at 11:30.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s On Today?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None