 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a726b2e6-97f8-5408-b49c-15d3a5070070', 'word_count': 1074, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a726b2e6-97f8-5408-b49c-15d3a5070070'}, {'abstract': 'The 56th annual Grammy Awards featured jarring shifts in tone and some strange partnerships.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/27/arts/music/grammy-awards-feature-wild-swings-and-partnerships.html', 'snippet': 'The 56th annual Grammy Awards featured jarring shifts in tone and some strange partnerships.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Grammy Awards broadcast, live on CBS from the Staples Center in Los Angeles, cautiously waited until after 11 p.m. to present its segment by Macklemore & Ryan Lewis, a rapper-and-producer team from Seattle. As they performed their song supporting same-sex marriage, “Same Love,” 33 couples, gay and straight, got married; perhaps the worrisome content was not the marriages but an antigay epithet amid the rhymes. It had been a good night for the duo, which was named best new artist and won three rap categories (only the Grammys don’t call it hip-hop). They were joined onstage by Madonna, singing “Open Your Heart”; by Queen Latifah presiding over the vows; and by the song’s chorus singer, Mary Lambert.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/arts/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/27/arts/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/arts/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/arts/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1351, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/arts/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/27/arts/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/arts/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1/sub2-jpGRAMMYS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Night of Music Marked by Some Wild Mood Swings', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Night of Music Marked by Some Wild Mood Swings', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammy Awards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T05:17:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Pareles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pareles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ce532aa2-9aea-522f-bed3-944e96cb089b', 'word_count': 798, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ce532aa2-9aea-522f-bed3-944e96cb089b'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/27/arts/television/whats-on-monday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '8 P.M. (Flix) A ROOM WITH A VIEW (1986) An Englishwoman (Helena Bonham Carter, above left with Judi Dench)) is torn between her strait-laced fiancé (Daniel Day-Lewis) and a young bohemian (Julian Sands) in this Merchant-Ivory adaptation of E. M. Forster’s 1908 novel, with an Oscar-winning screenplay by Ruth Prawer Jhabvala. Writing in The New York Times, Vincent Canby said the film “is like a holiday out of time.” In Robert Altman’s “Gosford Park” (2001), at 10, a coterie of aristocrats and their servants gather for a weekend shooting party at a grand English country estate in November 1932, during which their host, Sir William McCordle (Michael Gambon) is murdered. Whodunit? The suspects include Sir William’s chilly wife, Lady Sylvia (Kristin Scott Thomas), and her younger sister (Geraldine Somerville), with whom she cut cards to determine who would snag the man and his millions; the handsome young valet (Ryan Phillippe) on whom Lady Sylvia has set her sights; a gay American movie producer (Bob Balaban, who devised the movie’s concept with Mr. Altman) researching his latest project; the head housemaid (Emily Watson), whose affair with Sir William is common knowledge though never spoken of; and Lady Sylvia’s aunt, the imperious Countess of Trentham (Maggie Smith), whose livelihood is dependent on the allowance that Sir William has threatened to take away. Writing in The Times, Stephen Holden called the film, for which Julian Fellowes won an Oscar for best screenplay, “a melt-in-your-mouth hunk of 12-layer English spice cake.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Monday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Monday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T05:18:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/a859b991-e052-50bb-8ec6-b78033abad9d', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a859b991-e052-50bb-8ec6-b78033abad9d'}, {'abstract': 'Daft Punk, the French electronic music duo, won album of the year at a ceremony whose celebration of both establishment stars and newcomers reflected a changed music business.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/27/arts/music/grammys-laud-giants-and-upstarts.html', 'snippet': 'Daft Punk, the French electronic music duo, won album of the year at a ceremony whose celebration of both establishment stars and newcomers reflected a changed music business.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES — Newcomers, establishment stars and even a pair of French “robots” shared the spotlight at the 56th annual Grammy Awards ceremony on Sunday night, reflecting a changed music business in which top celebrities command constant attention yet a monster hit can come from anywhere.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grammys Laud Giants and Upstarts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grammys Laud Giants and Upstarts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammy Awards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Daft Punk (Music Group)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Pharrell', 'r