 Nazi attacks on Jews, some commentators saw evidence of a more widespread attitude among the very rich.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas J. Perkins, an elite investor in Silicon Valley, provoked outrage and shock over the weekend when he compared criticism of the wealthy to Nazi attacks on Jews.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/dbpix-perkins3/dbpix-perkins3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 931, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/28/business/dbpix-perkins3/dbpix-perkins3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 931}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/dbpix-perkins3/dbpix-perkins3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 660, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/dbpix-perkins3/dbpix-perkins3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1519, 'width': 979, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/dbpix-perkins3/dbpix-perkins3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/28/business/dbpix-perkins3/dbpix-perkins3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/dbpix-perkins3/dbpix-perkins3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nazi Remarks Make Perkins Persona Non Grata in Silicon Valley', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Perkins, Thomas James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Wall Street Journal', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T16:25:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/114c8b5d-9288-52ec-8e2b-3c9e1c92861c', 'word_count': 419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/114c8b5d-9288-52ec-8e2b-3c9e1c92861c'}, {'abstract': 'Of all the things I thought might be difficult about having children, I never realized it would be so much work to get them to do any work.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/27/age-appropriate-chores-for-children-and-why-theyre-not-doing-them/', 'snippet': 'Of all the things I thought might be difficult about having children, I never realized it would be so much work to get them to do any work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This Montessori chart of “Age-Appropriate Chores for Children” is making the Facebook rounds. (The shares on my page came from  the Facebook page of “Maria Montessori.”) With it, the commentary. Apparently, a lot of “other parents” have complained about this list. Those “other parents” say things like “children aren’t slaves” and “carrying firewood is dangerous!” Scroll down, and you do find those parents, noting that the label on the cleaning supplies says to “keep away from children,” and so they do, and what 10-year-old can mow the lawn? Who would trust a 12-year-old with hedge clippers?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/style/motherlode-chores/motherlode-chores-articleLarge.png', 'height': 770, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/27/style/motherlode-chores/motherlode-chores-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 770}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/style/motherlode-chores/motherlode-chores-jumbo.png', 'height': 821, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/style/motherlode-chores/motherlode-chores-superJumbo.png', 'height': 821, 'width': 640, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/style/motherlode-chores/motherlode-chores-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/27/style/motherlode-chores/motherlode-chores-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/27/style/motherlode-chores/motherlode-chores-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Age-Appropriate Chores for Children (and Why They’re Not Doing Them)', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Montessori, Maria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T16:26:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/31e6ee94-fdd7-5aac-ba6e-24390a4dcc8e', 'word_count': 632, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31e6ee94-fdd7-5aac-ba6e-24390a4dcc8e'}, {'abstract': 'In our latest installment of this exclusive series of interviews by Doug Aitken, the artist interviews the chef and food activist Alice Waters.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/27/the-source-alice-waters-in-conversation-with-doug-aitken/', 'snippet': 'In our latest installment of this exclusive series of interviews by Doug Aitken, the artist interviews the chef and food activist Alice Waters.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I think if you buy from people who are taking care of the land,” Alice Waters says, “you’re supporting the future of this country.” The chef activist speaks to Doug Aitken in Berkeley, Calif., at an Edible Schoolyard, her project to educate students about agriculture, economy and the environment. The interview is part of the Source, a series of candid video conversations with several dozen noteworthy figures about their creative processes, currently on view in a custom pavilion at the Sundance Film Festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Source | Alice Waters in Conversation With Doug Aitken', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Chefs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Aitken, Doug', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waters, Alice', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berkeley (Calif)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T16:30:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d162c67-6531-5e34-a1f1-cbad49359e8d', 'word_count': 140, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d162c67-6531-5e34-a1f1-cbad49359e8d'}, {'abstract': 'Stanislas Wawrinka captured his first major title at the Australian Open, defeating Novak Djokovic and Rafael Nadal along the way. But his victory wasn’t enough to crack the Big Four.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/sports/tennis/did-wawrinka-crack-tenniss-big-four-or-just-dent-it.html', 'snippet': 'Stanislas Wawrinka captured his first major title at the Australian Open, defeating Novak Djokovic and Rafael Nadal along the way. But his victory wasn’t enough to crack the Big Four.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MELBOURNE, Australia — For nine years now, four players have defined an era of men’s tennis, their play so consistent the rest of the ATP World Tour seemed to exist on another planet. There was Roger Federer, Rafael Nadal, Novak Djokovic and Andy Murray, and then there was everybody else.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/sports/tennis/28tennis/28tennis-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 444, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images