y clients and other small-business owners today.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Today in Small Business: Data Breaches', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T17:47:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gene Marks', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marks', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/855df9e6-304c-58a2-aca6-2f54b2a28163', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/855df9e6-304c-58a2-aca6-2f54b2a28163'}, {'abstract': 'When federal jobless benefits expired on Dec. 28, 1.3 million long-term unemployed Americans were abruptly cut off.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/27/for-the-unemployed-state-help-isnt-enough/', 'snippet': 'When federal jobless benefits expired on Dec. 28, 1.3 million long-term unemployed Americans were abruptly cut off.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Congressional Republicans who oppose extending federal unemployment benefits are saying, in effect, that state-provided jobless benefits are adequate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For the Unemployed, State Help Isn’t Enough', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment Insurance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T17:48:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Teresa Tritch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Teresa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tritch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15057b19-2e6d-56cc-8930-5ff579134ce6', 'word_count': 511, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15057b19-2e6d-56cc-8930-5ff579134ce6'}, {'abstract': 'The Security Council unanimously passed a resolution pressing governments around the world not to pay ransom money to terrorist kidnappers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/world/un-council-resolution-urges-no-ransom-to-terrorists.html', 'snippet': 'The Security Council unanimously passed a resolution pressing governments around the world not to pay ransom money to terrorist kidnappers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNITED NATIONS — The United Nations Security Council on Monday pressed governments around the world not to pay ransom money to terrorist kidnappers. The resolution, submitted by Britain and unanimously passed by the 15-member Council, is not enforceable, nor does it resolve the longstanding dispute at the United Nations over the definition of a terrorist. The British ambassador to the United Nations, Mark Lyall Grant, told reporters the resolution was intended to send a message to terrorist organizations like Al Qaeda that rely on ransom payments as a major source of financing. “We need to be honest about the impact ransoms have in sustaining terrorist activity,” the ambassador said. Governments rarely confirm, let alone publicly discuss, the details of ransom payments to kidnappers. The resolution applies only to payments to terrorist organizations, not other types of kidnappers.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.N. Council Resolution Urges No Ransom to Terrorists', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'United Nations: A Plea to End Ransom to Terrorists', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hostages', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidnapping', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T17:50:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Somini Sengupta', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Somini', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sengupta', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f82d7dce-bd34-548c-9265-fb588a57edec', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f82d7dce-bd34-548c-9265-fb588a57edec'}, {'abstract': 'The Mall in Columbia reopened with tight security as the police continued to investigate the background of the suspect in the shooting there, Darion Aguilar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/us/maryland-mall-prepares-to-reopen-after-deadly-shooting.html', 'snippet': 'The Mall in Columbia reopened with tight security as the police continued to investigate the background of the suspect in the shooting there, Darion Aguilar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'COLUMBIA, Md. — Shoppers returned on Monday to the Maryland mall where a shooting two days earlier in a skateboard and apparel store left three people dead.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/us/28MALL-1/28MALL-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge