'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-27T19:59:43+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Ferran Adrià', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ferran', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Adrià', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/f27449c0-2b37-5607-898c-d69b8d59f874', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/f27449c0-2b37-5607-898c-d69b8d59f874'}, {'abstract': 'If you really want to know how to become a leader and how to accomplish big things that others say are crazy even to try, look no further than one big trait: force of personality.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/27/why-chris-christie-could-have-been-an-entrepreneur/', 'snippet': 'If you really want to know how to become a leader and how to accomplish big things that others say are crazy even to try, look no further than one big trait: force of personality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What I see in the rise of Chris Christie, and now his recent crisis, is the same thing that I have long seen in the rise and fall (and often rise again) of fast-growth entrepreneurs. Through sheer force of personality, they rise up and get market-changing results over people, over bureaucracies, over anything that gets in their way.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Chris Christie Could Have Been an Entrepreneur', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Christie, Christopher J', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}