9c5-d360-51d8-b8ef-17dd141941a2', 'word_count': 762, 'uri': 'nyt://article/973e49c5-d360-51d8-b8ef-17dd141941a2'}, {'abstract': 'Parents of a gay son who will be marrying his boyfriend are thrilled by the marriage, but less so that their son is dropping their last name.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/booming/what-does-it-mean-when-a-gay-son-takes-his-husbands-surname.html', 'snippet': 'Parents of a gay son who will be marrying his boyfriend are thrilled by the marriage, but less so that their son is dropping their last name.', 'lead_paragraph': "Q. Dear Civil Behavior: My husband and I are thrilled that our son, who is in his late 20s, will be marrying his boyfriend in California this spring. I was not so happy, however, when he told me he’ll be dropping our last name and taking his fiancé's. When I married my husband in the early ‘80s I hyphenated my name, which felt like a good compromise. My husband and I are very hurt by our son’s decision, particularly since neither of us know any other same-sex couples who have chosen to do this (and we’d hoped to have grandchildren that carry on our family name). I also worry what this says about the dynamics of their relationship. I don’t want to be the mom who meddles with her son’s happiness, but am I right to be concerned? And how should I go about expressing my feelings? — Anonymous", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/booming/28civil-booming-names/28civil-booming-names-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/28/booming/28civil-booming-names/28civil-booming-names-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/booming/28civil-booming-names/28civil-booming-names-jumbo.jpg'