graph': 'Phillip Swagel is a professor at the School of Public Policy at the University of Maryland and was assistant secretary for economic policy at the Treasury Department from 2006 to 2009.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/economy/28economix-yellen/28economix-yellen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 410, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/28/business/economy/28economix-yellen/28economix-yellen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 410}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/economy/28economix-yellen/28economix-yellen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 700, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/economy/28economix-yellen/28economix-yellen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1400, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/economy/28economix-yellen/28economix-yellen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/28/business/economy/28economix-yellen/28economix-yellen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/28/business/economy/28economix-yellen/28economix-yellen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Challenges for the Yellen Fed', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inflation (Economics)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interest Rates', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Quantitative Easing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bernanke, Ben S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenspan, Alan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Yellen, Janet L', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Reserve System', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-28T16:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phillip Swagel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phillip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swagel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c1c8ad74-4e1f-5fe4-aee0-0a8894c61849', 'word_count': 1431, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c1c8ad74-4e1f-5fe4-aee0-0a8894c61849'}, {'abstract': 'Like hundreds of women during the Civil War, Jennie Hodgers disguised herself as a man in order to fight. Unlike most others, though, after the war ended, her cover remained.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/albert-cashiers-secret/', 'snippet': 'Like hundreds of women during the Civil War, Jennie Hodgers disguised herself as a man in order to fight. Unlike most others, though, after the war ended, her cover remained.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the spring of 1914, a Civil War veteran named Albert Cashier arrived at the Illinois state hospital for the insane with symptoms of advanced dementia. As a young private, Cashier had fought at the siege of Vicksburg, where he and his comrades broke the spine of the Confederacy, and his name was inscribed on the Illinois victory monument there. He had lived out the intervening years in modest circumstances, working as a farm hand, a laborer and, on occasion, a street lamplighter, one of the many former soldiers whose civilian lives never achieve the glory of their wartime service. He was destined for the same obscurity in death, had it not been for a secret that the state hospital made public: Albert Cashier was actually a woman named Jennie Hodgers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Albert Cashier’s Secret', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's Rights", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-28T16:00:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean R. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': 'R.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9bc42e59-dd3a-5202-adf8-21ed07e09f7d', 'word_count': 1353, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9bc42e59-dd3a-5202-adf8-21ed07e09f7d'}, {'abstract': 'More choice for travelers, and perhaps some good deals.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/more-ships-sailing-the-caribbean/', 'snippet': 'More choice for travelers, and perhaps some good deals.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A fleet of new ships in the Caribbean could mean not just greater selection but better deals for tropics-bound sailors. In November, the year-old, 3,502-guest MSC Divina began sailing year-round to the Caribbean from Miami. The new 4,000-passenger Norwegian Getaway launches Feb. 8, offering Caribbean itineraries from its home port, also Miami. In May, Princess Cruises introduces the 3,560-passenger Regal Princess in the Mediterranean; it will move to Fort Lauderdale in November to serve the eastern Caribbean. Making its debut in November, Royal Caribbean International’s 4,905-passenger Quantum of the Seas will offer regular Caribbean and Bahamas sailings from Cape Liberty, N.J.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Ships Sailing the Caribbean', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Princess Cruises', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Caribbean International', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caribbean Area', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-28T16:02:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elaine Glusac', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elaine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glusac', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/588593c1-d96e-5469-b894-7e0a83e25c61', 'word_count': 236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/588593c1-d96e-5469-b894-7e0a83e25c61'}, {'abstract': 'The Lloyds Banking Group will eliminate an additional 1,080 jobs and outsource 310 jobs as part of a restructuring it began in 2011.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/lloyds-banking-group-to-cut-more-than-1000-jobs/', 'snippet': 'The Lloyds Banking Group will eliminate an additional 1,080 jobs and outsource 310 jobs as part of a restructuring it began in 2011.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – The Lloyds Banking Group said on Tuesday that it would eliminate an additional 1,0