 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Leigh Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': 'Leigh', 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8590a307-0b73-5364-93fd-3adfb4c88dbf', 'word_count': 1660, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8590a307-0b73-5364-93fd-3adfb4c88dbf'}, {'abstract': 'As the Chinese get ready for Lunar New Year feasts, several areas in China have banned the sale of live chickens, as fears grew over the spread of avian influenza.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/world/asia/china-chicken-sales-suspended-over-concerns-about-avian-flu.html', 'snippet': 'As the Chinese get ready for Lunar New Year feasts, several areas in China have banned the sale of live chickens, as fears grew over the spread of avian influenza.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the Chinese get ready for Lunar New Year feasts, several areas in China have banned the sale of live chickens, and the Hong Kong government started culling 20,000 of the birds on Tuesday, as fears grew over the spread of avian influenza. The decision to kill the chickens, all from a single Hong Kong market, came after officials detected a new case of the deadly H7N9 virus in a bird imported from mainland China, across the border from Hong Kong in Guangdong Province. In southeastern China, New Year’s festivities often include the killing of a chicken. Contact with live poultry remains the main means of transmission for the virus. The H7N9 virus was first detected in March, and more than 200 cases have been reported so far, with roughly 50 deaths tied to the flu.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'China: Chicken Sales Suspended Over Concerns About Avian Flu', 'kicker': 'World Briefing', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Asia: China: Chicken Sales Suspended 