{'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/ebf6f99d-1c50-5887-95e1-6212170b6d6c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/ebf6f99d-1c50-5887-95e1-6212170b6d6c'}, {'abstract': 'To help appreciate the president’s speech, let’s take a preliminary visit to that citadel of reason, the Republican Party.', 'web_url': 'https://keller.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/principles-what-principles/', 'snippet': 'To help appreciate the president’s speech, let’s take a preliminary visit to that citadel of reason, the Republican Party.', 'lead_paragraph': ' To help appreciate President Obama’s fifth State of the Union address, with its to-do list of executive actions and its implicit fatalism about working with Congress, I suggest a preliminary visit to that citadel of reason, the Republican Party. There, the great minds and loud voices of the right are debating whether to adhere to the strategy of confrontation and sabotage that appeases the radicals in the conservative base, or to introduce a measure of pragmatism that might broaden the party’s appeal. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Principles? What Principles?', 'kicker': 'Bill Keller', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immigration and Emigration', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T03:27:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Keller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Keller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89ab4f63-0758-5bf5-825e-560ac184860c', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89ab4f63-0758-5bf5-825e-560ac184860c'}, {'abstract': 'When the president mentioned the benefits of health care reform, Democrats cheered and Republicans grimaced.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/28/looking-callous-at-the-sotu/', 'snippet': 'When the president mentioned the benefits of health care reform, Democrats cheered and Republicans grimaced.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Having covered more State of the Union addresses than I care to count, I know perfectly well that the applause breaks are ritualistic. But during tonight’s speech, there were several moments that were emblematic of the partisan divides in this country, and the callous nature of Republican domestic policy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Looking Callous at the SOTU', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boehner, John A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T03:29:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/aad0e82e-916a-52b7-9e92-16e5548e9c30', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/aad0e82e-916a-52b7-9e92-16e5548e9c30'}, {'abstract': 'Quotation of the day for Wednesday, January 29, 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/pageoneplus/quotation-of-the-day-for-wednesday-january-29-2014.html', 'snippet': 'Quotation of the day for Wednesday, January 29, 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’m eager to work with all of you. But America does not stand still — and neither will I. So wherever and whenever I can take steps without legislation to expand opportunity for more American families, that’s what I’m going to do.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quotation of the Day for Wednesday, January 29, 2014', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Quotation of the Day', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T03:32:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Summary', 'section_name': 'Corrections', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/ee6a19da-2716-5dc8-bda0-429910e668d1', 'word_count': 52, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ee6a19da-2716-5dc8-bda0-429910e668d1'}, {'abstract': 'Is deepening inequality inevitable? The case for a global wealth tax.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/opinion/capitalism-vs-democracy.html', 'snippet': 'Is deepening inequality inevitable? The case for a global wealth tax.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Thomas Piketty’s new book, “Capital in the Twenty-First Century,” described by one French newspaper as a “a political and theoretical bulldozer,” defies left and right orthodoxy by arguing that worsening inequality is an inevitable outcome of free market capitalism.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/edsall-contributor/edsall-contributor-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2013/10/04/opinion/edsall-contributor/edsall-contributor-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption