cle questioning aspects of her account of rising from teenage mother to Harvard Law School graduate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The two daughters of Wendy Davis, a candidate for governor, put out an open letter on Tuesday in response to a newspaper article questioning aspects of her account of rising from teenage mother to Harvard Law School graduate. The Dallas Morning News reported this month that the daughters of the Democratic candidate stayed with her second husband in Texas while she attended school and that he won custody when the couple divorced. Ms. Davis’s campaign has said the couple had joint custody. Republicans and conservatives accused her of misleading voters. Dru Davis, 25, said in her letter, “Sadly I feel the need to be crystal clear on the malicious and false charge of abandonment as nothing could be further from the truth.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Texas: Candidate’s Daughters Respond to Criticism', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Southwest', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Texas: Candidate’s Daughters Respond to Criticism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fernandez, Manny', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davis, Wendy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Texas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Child Custody and Support', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Dallas Morning News', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T04:48:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/896f