e of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T14:40:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Wheaton and Marc Santora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wheaton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Marc', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Santora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ee2fa5b-be3f-52ff-b048-df9ea6e80ada', 'word_count': 1163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ee2fa5b-be3f-52ff-b048-df9ea6e80ada'}, {'abstract': 'There’s nothing new about do-it-yourself — D.I.Y. is how medical products used to be invented.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/playing-with-toys-and-saving-lives/', 'snippet': 'There’s nothing new about do-it-yourself — D.I.Y. is how medical products used to be invented.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Apollo 13” is a good movie, but one 95-second stretch is what turned it into it a geek classic.\xa0\xa0 Their Command Module disabled, three astronauts use the Lunar Module as a lifeboat. Carbon dioxide is building up. The Command Module’s filters are available to scrub the air, but they’re square, and the receptacles for them on the Lunar Module are round. “Well, I suggest you gentleman invent a way to put a square peg in a round hole … rapidly,” the flight director Gene Kranz in Houston tells his engineers.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Playing With Toys and Saving Lives', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Do It Yourself', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medical Devices', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'v