0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'Quynhanh Do', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Quynhanh', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Do', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/844370cf-37e6-5faa-8042-6256c898475f', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/844370cf-37e6-5faa-8042-6256c898475f'}, {'abstract': 'After the State of the Union address, White House officials are hosting a day of social media engagement via Twitter, Facebook and a Google Hangout. If you have questions about how this year’s agenda will affect your family, here’s how to ask.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/big-block-of-cheese-day-offers-a-chance-to-ask-questions/', 'snippet': 'After the State of the Union address, White House officials are hosting a day of social media engagement via Twitter, Facebook and a Google Hangout. If you have questions about how this year’s agenda will affect your family, here’s how to ask.', 'lead_paragraph': "After the State of the Union Address, the White House is embarking on a day of social media outreach: “The First-Ever Virtual ‘Big Block of Cheese Day.'” First, a brief history lesson: according to the current administration, President Andrew Jackson in 1837 placed an ad announcing an open house at the White House — an opportunity for any and all to mingle with the president and his staff. The event featured a 1,400-pound block of cheese that sat in the main foyer of the White House. ", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Big Block of Cheese’ Day: The White House Offers a Chance to Ask Questions', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T17:18:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfee1827-b6b0-5e27-ae6b-d081f76b01b0', 'word_count': 597, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfee1827-b6b0-5e27-ae6b-d081f76b01b0'}, {'abstract': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/classical-playlist-zuill-bailey-natasha-paremski-leon-kirchner-and-more/', 'snippet': 'Times critics share what they’ve been listening to lately.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BRITTEN: Cello Symphony and Cello SonataZuill Bailey, cellist; North Carolina Symphony, Grant Llewellyn, conductor; Natasha Paremski, pianist(Telarc)Benjamin Britten composed several important works for his younger friend and colleague the cellist Mstislav Rostropovich. The longest, most substantial score was the Symphony for Cello and Orchestra (1963), a dark, wrenching and overpowering piece that Britten once called “the finest thing I’ve written.” An exciting new recording — a live performance in vivid sound — couples the brilliant, searching American cellist Zuill Bailey with the fine conductor Grant Llewellyn and the North Carolina Symphony. The CD includes Mr. Bailey’s gripping account of Britten’s 1961 Cello Sonata, with the pianist Natasha Paremski. (Anthony Tommasini)', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/arts/classical-natasha/classical-natasha-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge'