y', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chad Bray', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bray', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4af2b557-4c43-58bd-a091-53a5eb8b6380', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4af2b557-4c43-58bd-a091-53a5eb8b6380'}, {'abstract': 'Thirty-four years ago, Donna Speigel opened a consignment store in Cincinnati. Today she has 11 stores, 90 employees and annual revenue that she said exceeds $5 million.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/30/business/smallbusiness/how-one-owner-gets-people-to-take-tour-buses-to-her-consignment-shops.html', 'snippet': 'Thirty-four years ago, Donna Speigel opened a consignment store in Cincinnati. Today she has 11 stores, 90 employees and annual revenue that she said exceeds $5 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Perhaps fittingly for a onetime director of guest relations at an amusement park, Donna Speigel has turned the Snooty Fox chain of shops — consignment shops, no less — into Cincinnati destinations. Her customers come from as far away as Indianapolis and Louisville, even paying $25 to reserve a seat on “Fox Hunt” tour buses, where they are served boxed lunches and company lore. At the stores, they get wine and dessert and typically shop, if not until they drop, at least until they have taxed the bus’s overhead racks and lower storage compartments with their purchases.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/smallbusiness/30sbiz/30sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/30/business/smallbusiness/30sbiz/30sbiz-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype'