rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Costco Wholesale Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Minimum Wage', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State of the Union Message (US)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T20:23:09+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Politics', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/7e7412ce-f22a-5279-ba3a-9fdf0bbb4ba1', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/7e7412ce-f22a-5279-ba3a-9fdf0bbb4ba1'}, {'abstract': 'A new exhibition at the Museum of Modern Art captures the architect’s ambivalence toward urbanism by contrasting his unrealized plans for a suburban utopia with several proposals for massive skyscrapers.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/on-view-frank-lloyd-wrights-love-hate-relationship-with-the-american-city/', 'snippet': 'A new exhibition at the Museum of Modern Art captures the architect’s ambivalence toward urbanism by contrasting his unrealized plans for a suburban utopia with several proposals for massive skyscrapers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Frank Lloyd Wright was generally not a fan of cities. He thought them dirty, congested and hollow. In his 1932 book, “The Disappearing City,” he wrote of the American metropolis: “A parasite of the spirit is here, a whirling dervish in a whirling vortex.” The exhibition “Frank Lloyd Wright and the City: Density vs. Dispersal,” which opens this Saturday at the Museum of Modern Art, examines the legendary architect’s feelings about urbanism through his plans for a horizontal anti-metropolis he called Broadacre City, a lifelong project that some consider to have been a blueprint for suburbia. Perhaps surprisingly, there are also schemes for several unrealized skyscrapers, the very icons of the cities he held in such noisy contempt.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On View | Frank Lloyd Wright’s Love-Hate Relationship With the American City', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Museums', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, Frank Lloyd', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T20:24:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Curkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Curkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/118e9545-0811-55d2-b9a9-a57a2b62e794', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/118e9545-0811-55d2-b9a9-a57a2b62e794'}, {'abstract': 'The European Union revealed a long-awaited proposal to reduce the systemic risk posed by big banks like the Volcker Rule in the United States, but it may be a long time before the European Parliament considers it.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/29/e-u-bank-proposal-arrives-but-late-to-the-party/', 'snippet': 'The European Union revealed a long-awaited proposal to reduce the systemic risk posed by big banks like the Volcker Rule in the United States, but it may be a long time before the European Parliament considers it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The European Union on Wednesday revealed a long-awaited proposal to reduce the systemic risk posed by big banks, a measure that would bring the bloc’s regulations more closely into line with those of the United States. But it is unlikely to become law anytime soon.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/dbpix-eubanks2/dbpix-eubanks2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/30/business/dbpix-eubanks2/dbpix-eubanks2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/dbpix-eubanks2/dbpix-eubanks2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/dbpix-eubanks2/dbpix-eubanks2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/dbpix-eubanks2/dbpix-eubanks2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/30/business/dbpix-eubanks2/dbpix-eubanks2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/dbpix-eubanks2/dbpix-eubanks2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Europe’s Banking Risk Plan Is a Long Shot', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-29T20:26:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Jolly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jolly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/362b97f1-4ecc-5964-acfa-319d7370df3b', 'word_count': 407, 'uri': 'nyt://article/362b97f1-4ecc-5964-acfa-319d7370df3b'}, {'abstract': 'A wife ruminates big and small in “Dept. of Speculation,” Jenny Offill’s latest novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/30/books/jenny-offills-dept-of-speculation.html', 'snippet': 'A wife ruminates big and small in “Dept. of Speculation,” Jenny Offill’s latest novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two innovative books published in the 1970s used fragmentation and discontinuity to create kaleidoscopic portraits of their heroines’ lives. Renata Adler’s “Speedboat,” narrated by a woman who worked at a tabloid newspaper, jump-cut among vignettes from her life to capture the incongruities of her experience and the larger incongruities of American life in the ’60s and ’70s. Elizabeth Hardwick’s “Sleepless Nights,” whose narrator bore more than a passing resemblance to the author, mapped the contours of her life by putting memories, aphorisms and observations on shuffle.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/arts/30book/30book-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 667, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/30/arts/30book/30book-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 667}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/arts/30book/30book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 92