nal': 'By Raphael Minder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Raphael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Minder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad04a807-c8bf-564c-b6a6-61e043426b61', 'word_count': 1136, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad04a807-c8bf-564c-b6a6-61e043426b61'}, {'abstract': 'Monochromatic pieces, in shades from light camel to dark mahogany, add velvety softness to the resort collections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/30/t-magazine/suede-safari-style.html', 'snippet': 'Monochromatic pieces, in shades from light camel to dark mahogany, add velvety softness to the resort collections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1968, Yves Saint Laurent introduced its first belted safari jacket, which propelled the hunting-expedition look into mainstream fashion. This season, Saint Laurent’s designer Hedi Slimane updated the original, introducing a more polished, shorter version in solid suede. And the safari legacy lives on in other designers’ resort collections, where monochromatic, all-suede staples abound in shades ranging from light camel to dark mahogany. The thick buckle strap becomes a simple, yet sleek design element on an Alexander McQueen skirt, Calvin Klein halter and Gucci boot — a nod to the practical, waist-cinching detail of the trench. And the velvety skin adds sturdy softness to a pair of Jimmy Choo sandals and a Proenza Schouler cross-body bag.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/t-magazine/30market-suede-barsamian/30market-suede-barsamian-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/30/t-magazine/30market-suede-barsamian/30market-suede-barsamian-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp