om video and photography to painting, sculpture and large-scale installation.', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Artistic Celebration of the Beauty, Spectacle and Masculinity of Soccer', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On View: Fu00fatbol Sunday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Soccer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Los Angeles County Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-30T19:41:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brooke Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brooke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/908fe01f-eed5-5c80-9a74-97784ec696fc', 'word_count': 297, 'uri': 'nyt://article/908fe01f-eed5-5c80-9a74-97784ec696fc'}, {'abstract': 'Readers share their thoughts on the latest generation of fitness devices, like the Fitbit Force.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/30/fitness-trackers-lifeline-or-flatline/', 'snippet': 'Readers share their thoughts on the latest generation of fitness devices, like the Fitbit Force.', 'lead_paragraph': ' In this week’s personal technology column, I reviewed the Fitbit Force, a sleek new personal fitness tracker that is worn on the wrist. My feelings were mixed. I thoroughly enjoyed tracking my daily and weekly activity levels, and looking at the pretty visualizations that the Fitbit software creates from that data, but it was hard to parse deep insights from that information. The software, which is fairly sophisticated, still seems like it is not quite smart enough to help me figure out how to change my personal habits to be more healthy. I couldn’t recommend spending $130 on a rubber bracelet that tells me what I already know works — eat less, move more — to live well and stay in shape.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/STATE-JP2/STATE-JP2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 418, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/30/business/STATE-JP2/STATE-JP2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 418}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/STATE-JP2/STATE-JP2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 581, 'width': 834, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/STATE-JP2/STATE-JP2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 581, 'width': 834, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/STATE-JP2/STATE-JP2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/30/business/STATE-JP2/STATE-JP2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/business/STATE-JP2/STATE-JP2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fitness Trackers: Lifeline or Flatline?', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wearable Computing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fitbit', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-30T19:50:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jenna Wortham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jenna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wortham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13c8b1ae-e104-5a51-8c3d-8ad5feb50fc3', 'word_count': 459, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13c8b1ae-e104-5a51-8c3d-8ad5feb50fc3'}, {'abstract': 'Poets will be reading their sestinas at Poets House, and “The Wonderful Wizard of Odd” features clowns at Lincoln Center.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/nyregion/celebrating-sestinas-at-poets-house.html', 'snippet': 'Poets will be reading their sestinas at Poets House, and “The Wonderful Wizard of Odd” features clowns at Lincoln Center.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Followers of contemporary American poetry can’t fail to notice that traditional forms, employing rhymes and recognizable meters, are pretty firmly out of fashion. Browsing through the January issue of Poetry magazine, an admittedly unscientific but fairly typical sampling, you find almost exclusively free verse. Most of today’s poets, to paraphrase Robert Frost, seem to be quite happy playing tennis without a net.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '29', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31MISER_SPAN/31MISER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31MISER_SPAN/31MISER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image',