 since Armistead Maupin began charting the interlocking lives of a handful of its colorful fictional denizens in 1976. The city that was once a beacon for people looking to escape the strictures of conformity — and the puritanical streak in American culture — has now become a high-amenity bedroom community for the epicenter of the tech universe, Silicon Valley. And so the bohemian aerie at the center of Mr. Maupin’s beloved “Tales of the City” novels, 28 Barbary Lane, has now passed from the hands of Anna Madrigal, the one-woman marijuana dispensary who presided over her tenants as both a spiritual guru and a surrogate mother. Now, the building belongs to dot-commers who, in the words of one of the characters from Mr. Maupin’s new novel, “The Days of Anna Madrigal,” have “made it look like a five-star B and B.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1068, 'width': 1603, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/arts/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN/31JPBOOKMAUPIN_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Saying Goodbye to a City and Its Characters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Saying Goodbye to a City and Its Characters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Maupin, Armistead', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Days of Anna Madrigal (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-30T22:02:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles Isherwood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Isherwood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/65243248-1c0d-53fc-88d0-2b1c849297d2', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65243248-1c0d-53fc-88d0-2b1c849297d2'}, {'abstract': 'Three tunes, discussed.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/30/my-invisible-head-not-talking-economics/', 'snippet': 'Three tunes, discussed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Soundcheck on WNYC, talking musical tastes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'My Invisible Head Not Talking Economics', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-30T22:02:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eef91402-af8c-5c75-b3de-6c96cfbc6549', 'word_count': 102, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eef91402-af8c-5c75-b3de-6c96cfbc6549'}, {'abstract': 'A four-month-old pan-Asian lounge on Merrick Road offers sushi, cooked dishes and some unexpected textures.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/nyregion/a-review-of-2one2-in-rockville-centre.html', 'snippet': 'A four-month-old pan-Asian lounge on Merrick Road offers sushi, cooked dishes and some unexpected textures.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Patrons at 2one2, a pan-Asian restaurant named for its address on Merrick Road in Rockville Centre, enter through the bar. And it’s quite a bar, too, taking up almost half the space, with five large televisions, a 90-inch screen on another wall and plenty of room for a mingling crowd.', 'print_section': 'LI', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/nyregion/02SUBDINELI2/02SUBDINELI2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/nyregion/02SUBDINELI2/02SUBDINELI2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/nyregion/02SUBDINELI2/02SUBDINELI2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'