 Caveat', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cuomo, Andrew M', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kaplan, Thomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Budgets and Budgeting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T06:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Kaplan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kaplan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88762c9c-dcd1-57c5-86ab-1cdf16188c89', 'word_count': 495, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88762c9c-dcd1-57c5-86ab-1cdf16188c89'}, {'abstract': 'Television highlights.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/arts/television/whats-on-friday.html', 'snippet': 'Television highlights.', 'lead_paragraph': '9 P.M. (13) BARRYMORE In this “Great Performances” presentation, Christopher Plummer, above, recreates his 1997 Tony Award-winning portrayal of the actor John Barrymore as a boozy, physical wreck struggling in the final weeks of his life in 1942 to revive his Broadway triumph in “Richard III” from two decades earlier. “Barrymore’s memory sometimes fails him, but the words never do, or rather he never fails them: He grabs at their prose and poetry with force, flinging away some, caressing others,” Manohla Dargis wrote in The New York Times about this film adaptation by Érik Canuel of the William Luce play. “Mr. Plummer stumbles beautifully, poignantly and often, leering and searching through a haze of memory or, with concern edged with panic, calling for ‘a line, a line’ much as Richard III calls for a horse. From smile to sneer, he captures Barrymore’s majesty and grandiloquence, recites his triumphs and humiliations.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s on Friday', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What’s On Friday', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T06:00:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kathryn Shattuck', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kathryn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shattuck', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/f46bc6c9-b450-50b4-a636-969785f93a9a', 'word_count': 744, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f46bc6c9-b450-50b4-a636-969785f93a9a'}, {'abstract': 'A second interview with W.D. Snodgrass, this time near the end of his life, somehow brought things full circle.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/booming/knocking-once-again-on-the-poets-door.html', 'snippet': 'A second interview with W.D. Snodgrass, this time near the end of his life, somehow brought things full circle.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Somber news comes with the territory I patrol as obituaries editor for this newspaper, and it was in that capacity that I learned that W.D. Snodgrass was dying.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/booming/31mystory-booming-poet1/31mystory-booming-poet1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 397, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/31/booming/31mystory-booming-poet1/31mystory-booming-poet1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 397}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/201