e, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/arts/02DARGIS1/02DARGIS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/arts/02DARGIS1/02DARGIS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/arts/02DARGIS1/02DARGIS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/arts/02DARGIS1/02DARGIS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/arts/02DARGIS1/02DARGIS1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ignore the Young at Your Peril', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ignore the Young at Your Peril', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Film Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T13:14:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ef0cb84c-1c10-578d-842c-ef5cc0e659d7', 'word_count': 640, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ef0cb84c-1c10-578d-842c-ef5cc0e659d7'}, {'abstract': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center faces internal flux and competition in New York City. Industry figures and The Times’s chief film critics weigh in.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/movies/suggestions-for-renewing-the-film-society-of-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center faces internal flux and competition in New York City. Industry figures and The Times’s chief film critics weigh in.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Film Society of Lincoln Center was born 45 years ago in an age when screenings of foreign and other nonstudio films weren’t easily found. It was given the task of overseeing the New York Film Festival, a fledgling annual event at the time, but also with fostering what were described as “movie activities.” In the decades since, that nebulous term has been interpreted to mean a lively variety of repertory programs, retrospectives and, in the last few years, even first-run releases. While the annual festival has thrived, the fortunes of this nonprofit society have ebbed and flowed. In 2011, the organization expanded its footprint with the addition of the Elinor Bunin Munroe Film Center. But the society also recently underwent a leadership shake-up, its second in three years, and its year-round offerings face stiff competition from other screens around the city and online. A new executive director has yet to be named, and in this interim period, it seemed like the time to ask what a 21st-century Film Society should look like.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Prescriptions for a Cinema Refuge', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Prescriptions for a Cinema Refuge', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Film Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hope, Ted', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T13:23:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam W. Kepler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Kepler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c337a5c-3336-5e28-b119-4464514b762d', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c337a5c-3336-5e28-b119-4464514b762d'}, {'abstract': 'The grouchy retiree of “Nebraska” and the horrifying matriarch of “August: Osage County” were inspired by writers’ relatives.', 'web_url': 'https://carpetbagger.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/write-what-you-know-especially-if-its-difficult-family/', 'snippet': 'The grouchy retiree of “Nebraska” and the horrifying matriarch of “August: Osage County” were inspired by writers’ relatives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOS ANGELES – Violet Weston, the horrifying matriarch of “August: Osage County” who got Meryl Streep her 18th Oscar nomination, is famously based on the screenwriter Tracy Letts’s own grandmother. Mr. Letts’s characterization of her, he has said, was kinder than she was in real life.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/01/arts/tracy-letts/tracy-letts-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/01/arts/tracy-letts/tracy-letts-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 699}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/01/arts/tracy-letts/tracy-letts-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 879, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': N