 'byline': {'original': 'By Thomas Fuller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Thomas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fuller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67419260-d188-5388-af26-82eeffc6ebe9', 'word_count': 942, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67419260-d188-5388-af26-82eeffc6ebe9'}, {'abstract': 'In our latest installment of this exclusive series of interviews by Doug Aitken, the artist interviews the Los Angeles-based installation artist Liz Glynn.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/the-source-liz-glynn-in-conversation-with-doug-aitken/', 'snippet': 'In our latest installment of this exclusive series of interviews by Doug Aitken, the artist interviews the Los Angeles-based installation artist Liz Glynn.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I think about functioning as somewhere between an architect and a scientist,” says Liz Glynn. “So I create the space and set up an experiment, but then I get out of the way and sort of see what happens.” In conversation with Doug Aitken, the 32-year-old artist from Los Angeles talks about her work, which includes site-specific installations for performances and collective happenings as well as modern-day monuments and handmade sculptural objects. The interview is part of the Source, a series of candid video conversations with several dozen noteworthy figures about their creative processes, currently on view in a custom pavilion at the Sundance Film Festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Source | Liz Glynn in Conversation With Doug Aitken', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sundance Film Festival (Park City, Utah)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Diller, Liz', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:01:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8834e1ab-5dfa-5029-aa5a-62c315d1e9c6', 'word_count': 147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8834e1ab-5dfa-5029-aa5a-62c315d1e9c6'}, {'abstract': 'On moving to Michigan and how to lunch with the ladies.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/magazine/elaine-strich-broadway-legend-entertaining-is-hard.html', 'snippet': 'On moving to Michigan and how to lunch with the ladies.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After years of vowing to retire, you gave up your cabaret act at the Cafe Carlyle and your apartment at the Carlyle Hotel and moved to Birmingham, Mich. What made you finally take the leap? I wasn’t getting the quality of work that I wanted to get. I’m a funny age — funny ha-ha, funny peculiar, funny, funny. I could hardly open my mouth on the stage without getting a laugh. That’s a pretty sensational thing to brag about, but it’s also dangerous. I had a great time, and I’m very glad it’s over. Oh, my God, it’s hard. Entertaining is hard.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02talk/02talk-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 1168, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02talk/02talk-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1168}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02talk/02talk-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 527, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02talk/02talk-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1053, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02talk/02talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02talk/02talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02talk/02talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Elaine Stritch, Broadway Legend: ‘Entertaining Is Hard’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘You Can’t Enjoy Them Sober’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stritch, Elaine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Elaine Stritch: Shoot Me (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carlyle Hotel (Manhattan, NY, Hotel)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Dave', 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f5ed8ff-dd9e-54b8-8521-111bd147184b', 'word_count': 763, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f5ed8ff-dd9e-54b8-8521-111bd147184b'}, {'abstract': 'In the bleak wasteland of 21st-century publishing, there is only one hope: The young-adult novel. That’s a problem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/magazine/our-young-adult-dystopia.html', 'snippet': 'In the bleak wasteland of 21st-century publishing, there is only one hope: The young-adult novel. That’s a problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I sometimes wonder what Dante or Milton or any of those guys would make of the modern appetite for the young-adult epic. It wasn’t always a lucrative thing, writing grand, sweeping, fantastical stories, you know. It was a job for nose-to-the-grindstone, writing-for-the-ages types, and worldly rewards were low. Milton died in penury, blind and obscure; Dante met his maker in literal exile. Would they look with envy upon their celebrated and moneyed modern analogues — your J. K. Rowlings, your Suzanne Collinses?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02riff/mag-02Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 90, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02riff/mag-02Riff-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 90}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02riff/mag-02Riff-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 153, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02riff/mag-02Riff-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 306, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'c