: 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/magazine/19cover_text/19cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 730, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/19/magazine/19cover_text/19cover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 730}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/magazine/19cover_text/19cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 842, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/magazine/19cover_text/19cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1684, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/magazine/19cover_text/19cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/19/magazine/19cover_text/19cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/19/magazine/19cover_text/19cover-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Reply All: The 1.19.14 Issue', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 1.19.14 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0b80153-6afc-5a9b-bedc-c74130dbec1a', 'word_count': 613, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0b80153-6afc-5a9b-bedc-c74130dbec1a'}, {'abstract': 'A first summer in New York, and the promise of a life to grow into.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/magazine/grown-up-clothes.html', 'snippet': 'A first summer in New York, and the promise of a life to grow into.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I was 19, I went to New York City, my first trip East. I’d won a Mademoiselle contest and would be one of 20 young women working at the magazine for a month in the summer. It was 1953, and I was excited but also frightened and daunted; I hadn’t been on an airliner before, and no one I knew had been to New York except my Sioux Falls uncle, Bill, a buyer for a department store. In case of difficulties, I was to call my Uncle Bill’s New York friend, Mr. Herbert Solomon.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 900, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02lives/mag-02Lives-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Grown-Up Clothes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Grown-Up Clothes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Diane', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Careers and Professions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c61d23d-58f2-50f2-abe4-6a6a76729135', 'word_count': 772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c61d23d-58f2-50f2-abe4-6a6a76729135'}, {'abstract': 'And the surprising long-term benefits of playing them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/magazine/who-made-that-first-person-shooter-game.html', 'snippet': 'And the surprising long-term benefits of playing them.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It was like playing with dolls,” says the video-game designer and 3-D graphics creator John Carmack, thinking back to the computer entertainments before Call of Duty, Halo, Doom and all the other “first-person-shooter” games. “In a typical game of that time, your character was maybe 16-by-16 pixels in an overhead view. Maybe it did some cute things that made you smile. But you were still controlling a tiny little thing on the screen.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 398, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 398}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02wmt/mag-02WMT-t_CA0-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That First-Person-Shooter Game?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (First-Person-Shooter Video Game)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Engber, Daniel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'id Software', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': '3-D Devices and Effects', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer and Video Games', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carmack, John D', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Engber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Engber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b1a352e3-7d5b-5fbe-8792-47151cbb3b75', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b1a352e3-7d5b-5fbe-8792-47151cbb3b75'}, {'abstract': 'Rachael Ray violates the Third Commandment; Richard Sherman takes on “Peanuts.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2014/02/02/magazine/02-one-page-magazine.html', 'snippet': 'Rachael Ray violates the Third Commandment; Richard Sherman takes on “Peanuts.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rachael Ray violates the Third Commandment; Richard Sherman takes on “Peanuts.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02OPM-thumb/02OPM-thumb-jumbo.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02OPM-thumb/02OPM-thumb-superJumbo.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02OPM-thumb/02OPM-thumb-thumbStandard.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02OPM-thumb/02OPM-thumb-thumbStandard.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02OPM-thumb/02OPM-thumb-thumbLarge.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Breaking: Kids Are Obnoxious', 'kicker': 'The One-Page Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:07+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/037b10f6-5b17-5a8d-ac80-8b6d0176cc46', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/037b10f6-5b17-5a8d-ac80-8b6d0176cc46'}, {'abstract': 'The ethics of advertising.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/magazine/get-out-of-my-subconscious.html', 'snippet': 'The ethics of advertising.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Our subconscious beliefs, which we cannot easily understand or change, motivate many of the decisions we make. Some companies use manipulative branding techniques that create untrue associations between their products and some deep human desires   —   like sex or social status   —   with the goal of having the viewer establish this connection unconsciously. The desired result, presumably, is that the consumer will then buy the product with the misguided intent of fulfilling said desire. Is this practice unethical? ADAM GIDDING, NEW YORK', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 1316, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1316}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 467, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 934, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/ethicist/ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Get Out of My Subconscious!', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hiding in Plain Sight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klosterman, Chuck', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/4556cabf-cde4-5528-b195-d436d52786b9', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4556cabf-cde4-5528-b195-d436d52786b9'}, {'abstract': 'And it’s not just as a reaction against the snootiness of Scottish single malts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/magazine/theres-a-reason-that-rye-is-having-a-moment.html', 'snippet': 'And it’s not just as a reaction against the snootiness of Scottish single malts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If we were playing a round of word association a few years ago and you said, “Rye,” I wouldn’t have skipped a beat before saying, “Pastrami.” For most of my drinking life, rye whiskey seemed like tough-guy stuff, cowboy stuff. And to drink as they drank (or at least as I imagined they did) seemed affected or unduly performative. Why would I replace my everyday Irish whiskey with rye? Why trade the bourbon in my manhattan for another brown American spirit?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02drink1/02drink1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02drink1/02drink1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 655}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02drink1/02drink1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 938, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02drink1/02drink1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1876, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02drink1/02drink1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02drink1/02drink1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02drink1/02drink1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'There’s a Reason That Rye Is Having a Moment', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Go With the Grain', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schaap, Rosie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bourbon (Whiskey)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whiskey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grain', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rosie Schaap', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rosie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schaap', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7490fbd-f5cc-599a-af37-7eaafce77587', 'word_count': 398, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7490fbd-f5cc-599a-af37-7eaafce77587'}, {'abstract': 'The Harbin Ice and Snow World in northeast China.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/magazine/the-empire-state-building-in-ice.html', 'snippet': 'The Harbin Ice and Snow World in northeast China.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every winter, hundreds of thousands of tourists visit the Harbin Ice and Snow World in northeast China, near the Songhua River, to see its vast collection of elaborately carved ice structures, many of them illuminated with colorful LEDs. The arduous task of quickly collecting all that ice for an exhibition that opens in early January begins in mid-December, and the ice city has been getting bigger every year. This time around, 180,000 cubic meters of ice were used. Some of the finished sculptures this year, which remain in place until March, are a homage to structures around the world, from the Colosseum to the Empire State Building. Zengyu Wang, the executive manager of the event, says the local government in Harbin dreamed up the festival as a lure to promote winter tourism. When visitors see the ice for the first time, he says, they have one reaction: “Very shocked.” Julie Bosman', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '39', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Empire State Building in Ice', 'kicker': 'Look', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Ice Follies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harbin (China)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:18:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Photographs by Massimo Vitali', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Photographs', 'middlename': 'Massimo', 'lastname': 'Vitali', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6fba80c-be92-567c-85eb-2052b23aaf1a', 'word_count': 177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6fba80c-be92-567c-85eb-2052b23aaf1a'}, {'abstract': 'NHK is under criticism amid signs it is hewing to an agenda approved by the rightist government of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/01/world/asia/japans-public-broadcaster-faces-accusations-of-shift-to-the-right.html', 'snippet': 'NHK is under criticism amid signs it is hewing to an agenda approved by the rightist government of Prime Minister Shinzo Abe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — First, there was the abrupt resignation of a president accused by governing party politicians of allowing an overly liberal tone to news coverage. Then, his newly appointed successor immediately drew public ire when he seemed to proclaim that he would loyally toe the line of the current conservative government.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan’s Public Broadcaster Faces Accusations of Shift to the Right', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NHK (Japan Broadcasting Corp)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'ABE, SHINZO', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:22:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a8b59a2-7abd-5e34-8f7b-d509d71867d8', 'word_count': 1143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a8b59a2-7abd-5e34-8f7b-d509d71867d8'}, {'abstract': 'The survivors of Jagdish Sharan Verma, who was instrumental in strengthening laws protecting women, said he wouldn’t have accepted any award had he been alive.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/judges-family-denies-pride-was-behind-rejection-of-3rd-highest-award/', 'snippet': 'The survivors of Jagdish Sharan Verma, who was instrumental in strengthening laws protecting women, said he wouldn’t have accepted any award had he been alive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI — The family of the late Jagdish Sharan Verma, the former Supreme Court chief justice whose report helped strengthen Indian sexual assault laws, said it had rejected one of India’s top awards for the judge because he would have never accepted it had he been alive.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Judge’s Family Denies Pride Was Behind Rejection of 3rd-Highest Award', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khan, Vilayat', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mukherjee, Pranab', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Singh, Khushwant', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Verma, Jagdish Sharan', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Supreme Court of India', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T14:42:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Betwa Sharma', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Betwa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sharma', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16ce9199-57ff-5854-86c0-458502d51ff6', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16ce9199-57ff-5854-86c0-458502d51ff6'}, {'abstract': 'Timothy D. Cook, Apple’s chief executive, keeps promising a new product category from Apple, but investors are getting antsy as other product sales start to slow.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/investors-and-customers-yearn-for-an-apple-ithingymajig/', 'snippet': 'Timothy D. Cook, Apple’s chief executive, keeps promising a new product category from Apple, but investors are getting antsy as other product sales start to slow.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Let’s play a little game. If you were Timothy D. Cook, Apple’s chief executive, and you could have any gadget in the world that you wanted, what would it be? Forget that such a gadget doesn’t exist yet. Remember, you’re the chief executive. of Apple — you can just pick up your iPhone 10, scroll down to Jony Ive’s number and tell him to build you an iThingamajig.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/technology/bits-apple/bits-apple-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/technology/bits-apple/bits-apple-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 343, 'width': 592, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/30/technology/bits-apple/b