19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/c4bce7b9-3f78-5100-b767-26d457d491b9', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c4bce7b9-3f78-5100-b767-26d457d491b9'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE REAL JANE AUSTEN: A Life in Small Things,  by Paula Byrne. (Harper Perennial, $16.99.) Contrary to Virginia Woolf’s pronouncement, in 1928, that “we have lives enough of Jane Austen,” Austen’s appeal has proved irresistible. Byrne’s vividly persuasive biography shirks chronological constraints, using objects of special significance to Austen — an East Indian shawl, an ivory miniature, velvet cushions — to depict her as an exuberant, worldly woman of fierce convictions.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:14:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/22208751-55ad-553c-bba7-31030f50f4bc', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/22208751-55ad-553c-bba7-31030f50f4bc'}, {'abstract': 'Sarah Addison Allen’s novel “Lost Lake,” No. 5 on the hardcover fiction list, is her first book since she announced, in a 2011 Facebook post, that she had advanced-stage breast cancer.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/09/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'Sarah Addison Allen’s novel “Lost Lake,” No. 5 on the hardcover fiction list, is her first book since she announced, in a 2011 Facebook post, that she had advanced-stage breast cancer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Healthy Distance: Sarah Addison Allen’s “Lost Lake,” about a young widow trying to recover from grief at a magical Georgia resort, is new on the hardcover fiction list at No. 5. It’s Allen’s fifth novel — or sixth, if you count the early romance she wrote under a pseudonym — but her first since announcing, in a 2011 Facebook post, that she had advanced-stage breast cancer. (“For those of you who don’t want to read the word ‘boob,’ ‘breast’ or ‘bazookas’ a gazillion times,” she wrote, “I’ll let you know how it ends: I’m going to be fine. Now, for the rest of you, I’m going to reveal more about my cleavage than I think I’ve ever told anyone.”)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/02allen/02allen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 261, 'width': 273, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/02allen/02allen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 261, 'width': 273, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/02allen/02allen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/02allen/02allen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/02allen/02allen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:15:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fbe8123-8d23-5879-b287-2c629a64a930', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fbe8123-8d23-5879-b287-2c629a64a930'}, {'abstract': 'The story of a 1971 raid on an F.B.I. office and what it revealed about bureau practices.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/the-burglary-by-betty-medsger.html', 'snippet': 'The story of a 1971 raid on an F.B.I. office and what it revealed about bureau practices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On a March evening in 1971, eight antiwar protesters burglarized an F.B.I. office in Media, Pa., just outside Philadelphia, with astonishing ease. A few weeks of elementary surveillance had shown them the vulnerability of the target: There were no cameras to elude, no alarms to disconnect. Because the building contained residential apartments, the group chose the night of the first Joe Frazier-\xadMuhammad Ali heavyweight championship fight, an ideal distraction. It turned out that the Pennsylvania office, like so many others across the country, had almost no physical protection. Security was largely symbolic, resting on the bureau’s carefully buffed reputation for efficiency in tracking down America’s “most wanted” criminals, from bank robbers to atomic spies. Put simply, no one messed with J. Edgar Hoover’s F.B.I. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02OSHINSKY/02OSHINSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 511, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02OSHINSKY/02OSHINSKY-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 511}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02OSHINSKY/02OSHINSKY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 789, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02OSHINSKY/02OSHINSKY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 672, 'width': 789, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02OSHINSKY/02OSHINSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02OSHINSKY/02OSHINSKY-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02OSHINSKY/02OSHINSKY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Breaking In', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Breaking In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Medsger, Betty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hoover, J Edgar', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Bureau of Investigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:15:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Oshinsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oshinsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/e7a9eda9-1d1f-5b15-bcc9-f28491f45c73', 'word_count': 1281, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e7a9eda9-1d1f-5b15-bcc9-f28491f45c73'}, {'abstract': 'Two new books examine whether cultural traits underlie the success of certain overachieving subgroups.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/the-hybrid-tiger-and-the-triple-package.html', 'snippet': 'Two new books examine whether cultural traits underlie the success of certain overachieving subgroups.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Quanyu Huang’s new book, “The Hybrid Tiger: Secrets of the Extraordinary Success of Asian-\xadAmerican Kids,” may sound like yet another flogging for hapless Western parents, but it’s not.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-LOH/0202-bks-LOH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 812, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-LOH/0202-bks-LOH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 812}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-LOH/0202-bks-LOH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 757, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-LOH/0202-bks-LOH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1514, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-LOH/0202-bks-LOH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-LOH/0202-bks-LOH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-LOH/0202-bks-LOH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Secrets of Success', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "'The Hybrid Tiger' and 'The Triple Package'", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chua, Amy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rubenfeld, Jed', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huang, Quanyu', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Triple Package: How Three Unlikely Traits Explain the Rise and Fall of Cultural Groups in America (Book)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Hybrid Tiger: Secrets of the Extraordinary Success of Asian-American Kids (Book)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:16:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sandra Tsing Loh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sandra', 'middlename': 'Tsing', 'lastname': 'Loh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/063c9da3-b82a-5363-9468-8a9bd9d3d9a4', 'word_count': 1171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/063c9da3-b82a-5363-9468-8a9bd9d3d9a4'}, {'abstract': 'In Jude Cook’s novel, a failed poet relives his calamitous relationship with his wife.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/byron-easy-by-jude-cook.html', 'snippet': 'In Jude Cook’s novel, a failed poet relives his calamitous relationship with his wife.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How disgusting the British find themselves! Let me rephrase that. If you were to go by the output of a certain strand of young, male British fiction writer, you could easily be forgiven for thinking the main activity of British life is being disgusted by the physical form of oneself and one’s fellows. Ian McEwan began a short story noting the peculiar smell asparagus lends the urine (“it suggests sexual activity of some kind between exotic creatures”). Martin Amis spent large parts of his early career detailing his characters’ bleeding gums and cracked molars. Will Self’s books come so awash in bodily fluids that if you were to throw them against a wall they’d most likely stick. Male self-disgust, rooted in the moist tumult of adolescence, and extended into one’s 20s and 30s under cover of literary careers aping the clinical imperturbability of Nabokov and Joyce, has almost bequeathed us its own genre. We might call it, in the manner of those outraged letters to British broadsheets, “Disgusted of Tunbridge Wells.” ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,