imited time of year is that it forces me to devote a week of Recipes for Health to cabbage. I love this nutrient-dense cruciferous vegetable for many reasons: the way it sweetens as it cooks in the pan; the price per pound; the way it is comfortable in a soup or a pie, a stew, a gratin or a salad, a stir-fry or a timbale. Yet I sometimes forget to pay attention to cabbage. It can be overshadowed by other winter vegetables like kale, its more fashionable cousin.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/health/31recipehealth/31recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/31/health/31recipehealth/31recipehealth-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/health/31recipehealth/31recipehealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/health/31recipehealth/31recipehealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/health/31recipehealth/31recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/01/31/health/31recipehealth/31recipehealth-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/health/31recipehealth/31recipehealth-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Winter Cabbage With a Mediterranean Flair', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cabbage', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:16:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martha Rose Shulman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martha', 'middlename': 'Rose', 'lastname': 'Shulman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e1cf0219-152b-519a-a87c-2ea65093d604', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e1cf0219-152b-519a-a87c-2ea65093d604'}, {'abstract': 'Employees who hold shares in start-ups are often wealthy on paper, but have few ways to access that wealth. A new company, Equidate, is trying to get around rules that restrict employees’ ability to sell their shares with a novel way to provide liquidity.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/start-up-aims-to-circumvent-rules-on-private-stock-sales/', 'snippet': 'Employees who hold shares in start-ups are often wealthy on paper, but have few ways to access that wealth. A new company, Equidate, is trying to get around rules that restrict employees’ ability to sell their shares with a novel way to provide li...', 'lead_paragraph': 'You have likely heard of “first world problems.” But here is a Silicon Valley problem — and a company trying to solve it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Start-Up Aims to Circumvent Rules on Private Stock Sales', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Initial Public Offerings', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Equidate Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:17:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/57e0a930-ca31-5ca8-a3a5-775dac0e0597', 'word_count': 510, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57e0a930-ca31-5ca8-a3a5-775dac0e0597'}, {'abstract': 'A young woman disappears and evidence points to a troubled war veteran in Joyce Carol Oates’s novel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/carthage-by-joyce-carol-oates.html', 'snippet': 'A young woman disappears and evidence points to a troubled war veteran in Joyce Carol Oates’s novel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Brooklyn Museum, an exhibit called “War/Photography: Images of Armed Conflict and Its Aftermath,” which went up in November, featured a photograph by Nina Berman of a young Marine sergeant, Ty Ziegel, who was horrifically injured and disfigured by a suicide bomb in Iraq in 2004. Returning home, he underwent scores of surgeries and in 2006 married his sweetheart in Illinois. The marriage didn’t last, and Ziegel died in December 2012. But nobody who saw the “Marine Wedding” series will be able to forget him — or the damage wrought by war on his body, his life and his family. Joyce Carol Oates’s new novel puts the homecoming of a similarly wounded warrior at its center, doing with words what the Berman portraits did with images.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Schillinger/0202-bks-Schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 549, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Schillinger/0202-bks-Schillinger-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 549}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Schillinger/0202-bks-Schillinger-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 936, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Schillinger/0202-bks-Schillinger-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1716, 'width': 1877, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Schillinger/0202-bks-Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Schillinger/0202-bks-Schillinger-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Schillinger/0202-bks-Schillinger-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Lonely Hunter', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lonely Hunter', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Oates, Joyce Carol', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schillinger, Liesl', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Carthage (Book)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:17:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/765b9e9f-6534-5a45-a67e-a0c1b39a6f98', 'word_count': 1303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/765b9e9f-6534-5a45-a67e-a0c1b39a6f98'}, {'abstract': 'Douglas R. Egerton argues that the view of Reconstruction as a failure overlooks the era’s progressive reforms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/douglas-r-egertons-wars-of-reconstruction.html', 'snippet': 'Douglas R. Egerton argues that the view of Reconstruction as a failure overlooks the era’s progressive reforms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As the 150th anniversary of the Civil War heads toward its conclusion, the anniversary of Reconstruction, the turbulent era that followed, looms on the horizon. Properly commemorating it poses a challenge, since for no period of American history does a wider gap exist between scholarly understanding and public consciousness.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-FONER/0202-bks-FONER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 498, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-FONER/0202-bks-FONER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 498}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-FONER/0202-bks-FONER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 850, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-FONER/0202-bks-FONER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1169, 'width': 1409, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-FONER/0202-bks-FONER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-FONER/0202-bks-FONER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-FONER/0202-bks-FONER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Liberated and Unfree', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Liberated and Unfree', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Foner, Eric', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Egerton, Douglas R', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reconstruction Era', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:18:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Foner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Foner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd470391-0f4e-5574-b2dd-f97a60b35753', 'word_count': 1210, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd470391-0f4e-5574-b2dd-f97a60b35753'}, {'abstract': 'A novel about the World War II Japanese invasion of Alaska.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/the-wind-is-not-a-river-by-brian-payton.html', 'snippet': 'A novel about the World War II Japanese invasion of Alaska.', 'lead_paragraph': 'They called it the “Aleutian stare.” During World War II, American soldiers stationed in the remote, windswept archipelago stretching for 1,100 miles west of the Alaskan mainland fought a secret war against Japanese invaders. Driven mad by the cold, the isolation and the “dim, colorless light,” some were taken away in straitjackets, gazing into the distance, “unfocused, unspeaking.” The rest of the country didn’t even know they were there.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02SUBFERGUSON/02SUBFERGUSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 325, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02SUBFERGUSON/02SUBFERGUSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 325}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02SUBFERGUSON/02SUBFERGUSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 555, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02SUBFERGUSON/02SUBFERGUSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1110, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02SUBFERGUSON/02SUBFERGUSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02SUBFERGUSON/02SUBFERGUSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02SUBFERGUSON/02SUBFERGUSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Unknown War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Unknown War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Payton, Brian', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:18:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Ferguson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ferguson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fb50d748-b879-5288-b05d-54b5fc4c3317', 'word_count': 570, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fb50d748-b879-5288-b05d-54b5fc4c3317'}, {'abstract': 'In Mary Miller’s first novel, a teenage girl and her fractured family travel cross-country in preparation for the rapture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/mary-millers-last-days-of-california.html', 'snippet': 'In Mary Miller’s first novel, a teenage girl and her fractured family travel cross-country in preparation for the rapture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just as Arthur Phillips’s “Prague” follows a group of Americans living not in the title city but in less trendy Budapest, Mary Miller’s terrific first novel, “The Last Days of California,” never quite makes it to the Golden State. And I don’t think it’s giving anything away to say it doesn’t turn out to be the last days, either.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Muchnik/0202-bks-Muchnik-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 389, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Muchnik/0202-bks-Muchnik-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 389}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Muchnik/0202-bks-Muchnik-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 663, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Muchnik/0202-bks-Muchnik-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1327, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Muchnik/0202-bks-Muchnik-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Muchnik/0202-bks-Muchnik-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-Muchnik/0202-bks-Muchnik-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Are We There Yet?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Are We There Yet?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Miller, Mary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:19:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laurie Muchnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laurie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Muchnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/44e3f9fc-d803-5783-ad2f-05ec08e36307', 'word_count': 1146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44e3f9fc-d803-5783-ad2f-05ec08e36307'}, {'abstract': 'The plight of 11 American P.O.W.’s in North Vietnam.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/defiant-by-alvin-townley.html', 'snippet': 'The plight of 11 American P.O.W.’s in North Vietnam.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alvin Townley, the author of books about naval aviation and Eagle Scouts, has written a gripping account of the “Alcatraz Eleven,” a group of American prisoners of war in North Vietnam who formed a tough core of heroic resisters despite brutal and relentless efforts to break them and convert them into fodder for the Communist propaganda machine. Readers of even passing acquaintance with the Vietnam War will most likely know of Jeremiah Denton’s televised 1966 interview — one of the first glimpses of the life of United States military pilots who had been shot down and captured. American viewers watched in horror and puzzlement as Denton, an emaciated, hunched figure dressed in gray prison garb, defiantly telegraphed the word t-o-r-t-u-r-e by blinking out the letters in Morse code. His brave act was followed by yet more torture, and some time later Vietnamese authorities bundled him along with 10 other men out of the infamous Hanoi Hilton, as Hoa Lo prison in the North Vietnamese capital was known (and where John McCain, probably the most famous P.O.W. of the war, would be held). The destination of the Eleven was an even filthier hellhole a few miles away, which they nicknamed Alcatraz.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02ROBINSON/02ROBINSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 354, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02ROBINSON/02ROBINSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 354}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02ROBINSON/02ROBINSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02ROBINSON/02ROBINSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 758, 'width': 1283, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02ROBINSON/02ROBINSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02ROBINSON/02ROBINSON-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02ROBINSON/02ROBINSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Limits of Endurance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Limits of Endurance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisoners of War', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Townley, Alvin', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:20:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f20a656c-dc76-5696-9f3c-226d24efdd9f', 'word_count': 700, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f20a656c-dc76-5696-9f3c-226d24efdd9f'}, {'abstract': 'Penelope Lively looks back on a life of reading and writing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/dancing-fish-and-ammonites-by-penelope-lively.html', 'snippet': 'Penelope Lively looks back on a life of reading and writing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Penelope Lively is writing from another country, a place she calls “old age.” She is 80, and so, she assures us, she is now “a native” of this territory, an “authority.” “Dancing Fish and Ammonites” is about growing old, about memory and history, about reading and writing and the way experiences become associated with certain objects — with a book, say, or a piece of pottery. Lively has explored these themes before, in her many novels and in a handful of previous memoirs. This time, though, she says she wants to address them more abstractly: “I find myself thinking less about what has happened to me but interested in this lifetime context, in the times of my life.” She’s not trying to tell us a story.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02THOMAS/02THOMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 293, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02THOMAS/02THOMAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 293}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02THOMAS/02THOMAS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02THOMAS/02THOMAS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 645, 'width': 1319, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02THOMAS/02THOMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02THOMAS/02THOMAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/02THOMAS/02THOMAS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'At a Distance', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At a Distance', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lively, Penelope', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T16:20:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Louisa Thomas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Louisa', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Thomas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cf9febd-da88-55bb-afcc-363bede07c9f', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cf9febd-da88-55bb-afcc-363bede07c9f'}, {'abstract': 'In Isabel Allende’s “Ripper,” a teenage sleuth investigates a series of killings with the help of an interactive mystery game she plays online.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/books/review/isabel-allendes-ripper-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Isabel Allende’s “Ripper,” a teenage sleuth investigates a series of killings with the help of an interactive mystery game she plays online.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Isabel Allende says that RIPPER (Harper, $28.99) is her first murder mystery, and that it was a lot of fun to write. It’s probably more accurate to say that this ungainly but thoroughly charming book is the author’s own eccentric notion of a murder mystery, and that it’s a lot of fun to read. Also, it features a teenage sleuth — “idiosyncratic of appearance, timorous of character, but magnificent of mind,” according to her besotted grandfather — who is pretty much irresistible.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-CRIME/0202-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 815, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-CRIME/0202-bks-CRIME-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 815}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/books/review/0202-bks-CRIME/0202-bks-CRIME-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 754, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'supe