 636, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af9084cc-ea41-5de4-becf-c440d995b7c3'}, {'abstract': 'Half a century ago this week, the Beatles arrived in New York and changed popular culture. Now, dozens of concerts, exhibitions, discussions, books and albums are celebrating what happened in 1964.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/arts/music/beatle-fans-hear-yesterday-again.html', 'snippet': 'Half a century ago this week, the Beatles arrived in New York and changed popular culture. Now, dozens of concerts, exhibitions, discussions, books and albums are celebrating what happened in 1964.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was inevitable, time marching on as it does, and yet it also hard to believe: Half a century has passed since the Beatles touched down in New York for the first time, on Feb. 7, 1964, and seduced the country with three performances on “The Ed Sullivan Show” and three concerts, one at the Washington Coliseum and two at Carnegie Hall. Everything about them — their pudding basin haircuts, their Cardin suits and pointed boots, their sharp, irreverent sense of humor — seemed outlandish, compared with American pop groups. And though their music was firmly rooted — as they were always quick to point out — in American rhythm and blues, soul and rock, they produced a sound that was fresh, energetic and unmistakably their own.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Beatle Fans Hear Yesterday Again', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Beatle Fans Hear Yesterday Again ', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kozinn, Allan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Beatles, The', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T17:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/534b5af1-4833-54f2-834f-8cbaeb8837a1', 'word_count': 1522, 'uri': 'nyt://article/534b5af1-4833-54f2-834f-8cbaeb8837a1'}, {'abstract': 'A reporter tore a ligament in her knee and began the hunt for an orthopedist with Jelly and Need, two new social question-and-answer smartphone apps. It turned out that the audiences, and advice, were quite different.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/test-run-jelly-vs-need-comparing-two-new-social-q-a-apps/', 'snippet': 'A reporter tore a ligament in her knee and began the hunt for an orthopedist with Jelly and Need, two new social question-and-answer smartphone apps. It turned out that the audiences, and advice, were quite different.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In case anyone was looking for further evidence that the five-star rating system is broken, I heard a horrific story the other day from someone who used Yelp to select a five-star-rated veterinarian when his vet was out of town, only to get stuck with a $10,000 vet bill and a dead pet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/technology/31bits-qa-need/31bits-qa-need-articleLarge.png', 'height': 1065, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/01/31/technology/31bits-qa-need/31bits-qa-need-articleLarge.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 1065}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/01/31/technology/3