s', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e9835d-efd2-5ac3-b50b-abea1245dd4b', 'word_count': 484, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e9835d-efd2-5ac3-b50b-abea1245dd4b'}, {'abstract': 'A glimpse at what guys and gals are wearing this season.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/seen-on-the-street-outside-the-fallwinter-2014-copenhagen-womens-shows/', 'snippet': 'A glimpse at what guys and gals are wearing this season.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A glimpse at what guys and gals are wearing this season.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seen on the Street | Outside the Fall/Winter 2014 Copenhagen Women’s Shows', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:21:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Katz Sinding', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': 'Katz', 'lastname': 'Sinding', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b8d23f6c-bf23-58a1-b34b-0915354b68bf', 'word_count': 11, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b8d23f6c-bf23-58a1-b34b-0915354b68bf'}, {'abstract': 'The artist Kysa Johnson created paintings for the concourse level of the Empire State Building that were installed in 2000. The building’s owner cannot locate them and says they were “likely destroyed.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/artist-files-suit-over-missing-empire-state-building-paintings/', 'snippet': 'The artist Kysa Johnson created paintings for the concourse level of the Empire State Building that were installed in 2000. The building’s owner cannot locate them and says they were “likely destroyed.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'For many years, visitors to the concourse level of the Empire State Building walked by a series of large, lush paintings that seemed to be abstractions but that actually represented the atomic or molecular structure of the materials — brick, steel, cement, aluminum — used in the skyscraper’s construction.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Artist Files Suit Over Missing Empire State Building Paintings', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Empire State Building (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:26:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bdb62302-f8fc-55f4-be4b-1455f4985d90', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bdb62302-f8fc-55f4-be4b-1455f4985d90'}, {'abstract': 'The practice has benefits, but it is far more effective as a technique of self-management than as a means of personal transformation, Tony Schwartz writes in the Life@Work column.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/more-mindfulness-less-meditation/', 'snippet': 'The practice has benefits, but it is far more effective as a technique of self-management than as a means of personal transformation, Tony Schwartz writes in the Life@Work column.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s the promise: Meditation – and mindfulness meditation, in particular – will reduce your cortisol level, blood pressure, social anxiety and depression. It will increase your immune response, resilience and focus and improve your relationships — including with yourself. It will also bolster your performance at work and provide inner peace. It may even cure psoriasis. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/01/business/dbpix-meditation/dbpix-meditation-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2014/02/01/business/dbpix-meditation/dbpix-meditation-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/01/business/dbpix-meditation/dbpix-meditation-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/01/business/dbpix-meditation/dbpix-meditation-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/01/business/dbpix-meditation/dbpix-meditation-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2014/02/01/business/dbpix-meditation/dbpix-meditation-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/01/business/dbpix-meditation/dbpix-meditation-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'More Mindfulness, Less Meditation', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meditation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:28:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tony Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schwartz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/826fc269-6ab4-5d8c-9dae-c5b0a8581f0d', 'word_count': 977, 'uri': 'nyt://article/826fc269-6ab4-5d8c-9dae-c5b0a8581f0d'}, {'abstract': 'By all accounts, young people are gravitating away from watching television in the traditional way, but that’s not stopping Nickelodeon.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/arts/television/teacher-and-troll-both-start-with-t.html', 'snippet': 'By all accounts, young people are gravitating away from watching television in the traditional way, but that’s not stopping Nickelodeon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By all accounts, young people are gravitating away from watching television in the traditional way, but that’s not stopping Nickelodeon from going after very young people. On Monday afternoon at 1, the channel unveils “Wallykazam!,” an animated series full of early literacy lessons for preschoolers. It’s a “Sesame Street” approach transplanted to a land of magic and kid-friendly mythical beings.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Teacher and Troll Both Start With ‘T’', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Television', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Teacher and Troll Both Start With ‘T’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:30:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil Genzlinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Genzlinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9934c71f-86ec-5cc6-87a7-59eedd7779c7', 'word_count': 200, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9934c71f-86ec-5cc6-87a7-59eedd7779c7'}, {'abstract': '“Roadshow: The Fall of Film Musicals in the 1960s,” through Friday, has five Hollywood musicals on the screen at the Museum of Modern Art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/movies/harmonic-requiem-for-a-genre.html', 'snippet': '“Roadshow: The Fall of Film Musicals in the 1960s,” through Friday, has five Hollywood musicals on the screen at the Museum of Modern Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It won’t be grand: no overtures or entr’actes or glossy souvenir programs for sale in the lobby. But “Roadshow: The Fall of Film Musicals in the 1960s,” through Friday, has five of those rare birds — Hollywood musicals — glittering and strutting across the screen at the Museum of Modern Art.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Harmonic Requiem for a Genre', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Film', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Harmonic Requiem for a Genre', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:31:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Saltz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Saltz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42138a7f-4dcb-598c-b3d0-53f859e4a9e0', 'word_count': 178, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42138a7f-4dcb-598c-b3d0-53f859e4a9e0'}, {'abstract': 'Since it began organizing exhibitions in 2012, the Robert Rauschenberg Foundation has looked far and wide for artists, staying mostly clear of the career of its famous founder.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/arts/design/what-lives-on-after-death.html', 'snippet': 'Since it began organizing exhibitions in 2012, the Robert Rauschenberg Foundation has looked far and wide for artists, staying mostly clear of the career of its famous founder.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Since it began organizing exhibitions in 2012, the Robert Rauschenberg Foundation has looked far and wide for artists, staying mostly clear of the career of its famous founder, who died in 2008 and wanted the foundation to serve as a home for idiosyncratic, politically vocal work.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Lives On After Death', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Art', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'What Lives on After Death', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:32:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randy Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ed842e89-a1ed-54fc-a11e-a56d6818b58a', 'word_count': 203, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ed842e89-a1ed-54fc-a11e-a56d6818b58a'}, {'abstract': 'Ballet and modern dance once were bitter enemies, but ballet dancers sneaked off to modern classes, and modern dancers turned up in ballet studios.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/arts/dance/translations-that-move.html', 'snippet': 'Ballet and modern dance once were bitter enemies, but ballet dancers sneaked off to modern classes, and modern dancers turned up in ballet studios.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ballet and modern dance once were bitter enemies. Yet because dancers tend to be blessed with curiosity, ballet dancers sneaked off to modern classes, and modern dancers turned up in ballet studios. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Translations That Move', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Translations That Move', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:32:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jack Anderson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jack', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anderson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e602dae0-0c18-58a4-88d6-a3d82c101b1e', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e602dae0-0c18-58a4-88d6-a3d82c101b1e'}, {'abstract': 'Although the Miller Theater at Columbia University is indelibly tied to contemporary composers and early music, the Bach canon has long formed a third pillar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/arts/music/three-composers-basking-in-bach.html', 'snippet': 'Although the Miller Theater at Columbia University is indelibly tied to contemporary composers and early music, the Bach canon has long formed a third pillar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Although the Miller Theater at Columbia University is indelibly tied to contemporary composers and early music, the voluminous canon of Johann Sebastian Bach has long formed a third pillar of that institution’s programming. But this season, the theater’s customary Bach series gets an intriguing overhaul with Bach, Revisited, a three-concert sequence in which prominent living composers pair selections by Bach with their own works to try to illuminate connections.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Three Composers Basking in Bach', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Classical', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Three Composers Basking in Bach', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:34:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steve Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edd8c2b6-53e4-514c-8789-f2de78a85ddc', 'word_count': 159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edd8c2b6-53e4-514c-8789-f2de78a85ddc'}, {'abstract': 'The Panamanian pianist Danilo Pérez is an exceptional musician, even without much framework.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/02/arts/music/the-musical-trail-of-an-explorer.html', 'snippet': 'The Panamanian pianist Danilo Pérez is an exceptional musician, even without much framework.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Panamanian pianist Danilo Pérez is an exceptional musician, even without much framework, as in the loosest, most intuitive moments of Wayne Shorter’s current band, of which he’s been a member since 2000. But he puts a lot of framework in his own music: He wants to represent jazz within its broadest possible contexts of place and history. He’s about to release a new record, “Panama 500” (Mack Avenue), which integrates Panamanian folklore, European classical music and jazz. Some of it describes the Spanish explorer Vasco Núñez de Balboa’s crossing the isthmus of Panama in 1513. ', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Musical Trail of an Explorer', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead | Pop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Musical Trail Of an Explorer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2014-01-31T18:34:28+0000