ad-59dc-acb3-74cefc9df65a', 'word_count': 298, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3b813e1-03ad-59dc-acb3-74cefc9df65a'}, {'abstract': 'A lot of articles have been written about Amy Chua and Jed Rubenfeld — some of them so contradictory that they almost cancel each other out.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2014/01/31/a-couple-of-contradictions/', 'snippet': 'A lot of articles have been written about Amy Chua and Jed Rubenfeld — some of them so contradictory that they almost cancel each other out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the course of working on the profile of Amy Chua and Jed Rubenfeld that I wrote for this weekend’s magazine, I came across many things that had been written about the couple — some of them so contradictory that they almost canceled each other out. Chua’s supporters see her as a courageous truth-teller, willing to sacrifice her good name to show how permissive parenting has put American children in peril; her critics see her as a cynical pop-cultural savant, exploiting stereotypes and anxieties to sell some books. Reactions to Rubenfeld have been similarly erratic, though less extreme. Here is a sliver of what’s out there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02chua-book-cover/02chua-book-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 357, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02chua-book-cover/02chua-book-cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 357, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/02/02/magazine/02chua-book-cover/02chua-book-cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': 